window.NTI=window.NTI||{};window.NTI.registerBodies("aws",{"aws-01-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-1-cloud-concepts-overvi":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-1-cloud-concepts-overview","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module1-CloudConcepts-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS_Module1_CloudConceptsOverview_Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 1: Cloud Concepts Overview Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS_Module1_CloudConceptsOverview_Notes.md --- 1. What Is Cloud Computing? The formal definition comes from NIST the U.S. National Institute of Standards and Technology , and it is worth reading slowly because every phrase in it is tested somewhere in this course: On-demand access to a shared pool of configurable resources — networks, servers, storage, applications, services — that can be provisioned and released with minimal effort, over the internet, pay-as-you-go. Unpack that sentence piece by piece. On-demand means you do not file a ticket and wait weeks; you click, call an API, and the resource exists minutes later. Shared pool means the provider owns massive data centers and slices them among thousands of customers — this is what makes the economics work, because your idle capacity is someone else's peak usage. Configurable "},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"resources is the list of everything that follows in this course: virtual machines, disks, networks, databases. Provisioned and released with minimal effort is elasticity — the defining property of cloud. And pay-as-you-go is the billing model that makes the rest possible: metering replaces purchasing. NIST lists five essential characteristics. They are not trivia — each one maps to a concrete AWS behavior you will see in later modules: 1. On-demand self-service. You provision without human interaction from the provider. In AWS terms: the console, the CLI, CloudFormation. No purchase order, no sales call. 2. Broad network access. Resources are reachable over standard networks and protocols. This is why everything in AWS has an API endpoint and why hybrid designs Direct Connect, VPN exist at all. 3. Resource pooling. Multi-tenancy. Your EC2 instance physically shares hardware with other customers' instances; the hypervisor isolates you. Pooling is the reason AWS can offer lower unit prices than your own data center — utilization across the pool stays high. 4. Rapid elasticity. Scale out and in quickly, sometimes automatically Auto Scaling groups, Lambda concurrency . This is the sing"},{"anchor":"1-what-is-cloud-computing","text":"le property that traditional data centers cannot match: they must buy for peak and sit idle the rest of the time. 5. Measured service. Metering. Every API call, GB-month, and vCPU-hour is counted, which is what makes pay-as-you-go bills possible — and what makes Cost Explorer and Budgets Module 2 necessary. --- 2. Cloud Service Models — How Much Do You Manage? The service models answer one question: where is the line between what AWS manages and what you manage? The line moves. The exam tests exactly where it sits for each model, so learn the pattern rather than memorizing rows: the further you move from IaaS toward SaaS, the more AWS takes over. IaaS Infrastructure as a Service gives you raw building blocks — virtual machines, disks, networks. AWS owns the hardware, the virtualization layer, the data center, and the physical network. You own everything from the operating system upward: OS patching, middleware, runtime, application, data. EC2 is the canonical example. Choose IaaS when you need full control — custom OS images, legacy software, special drivers. PaaS Platform as a Service hands you a ready platform: you deploy code or data, and AWS manages the OS, middleware, and runt"},{"anchor":"2-cloud-service-models-how-much-do-you-manage","text":"ime underneath. You keep only the application and the data. Elastic Beanstalk and RDS are the examples to remember. Choose PaaS when you want to stop thinking about servers but still own the application logic. SaaS Software as a Service is a finished product you simply use. AWS manages the entire stack; you manage only your data and how you use the product. WorkMail and Chime are the AWS examples. There is almost nothing to operate — which is also why there is almost nothing to customize. FaaS / Serverless Functions as a Service is worth treating as a fourth model: you supply function code, AWS manages everything else including scaling to zero. Lambda and Fargate are the examples. It is the extreme end of the same spectrum — maximum AWS management, minimum customer management. Recap table use after reading, not instead of it : Model AWS manages You still manage AWS examples --- --- --- --- IaaS Hardware, virtualization, data center, networking OS, middleware, runtime, app, data EC2, EBS, VPC PaaS All of IaaS + OS, middleware, runtime Application & data only Elastic Beanstalk, RDS SaaS The entire stack Only your data & usage WorkMail, Chime FaaS Everything incl. scaling Function cod"},{"anchor":"deployment-models","text":"e only Lambda, Fargate Mnemonic — who owns the OS: IaaS = you, PaaS = provider, SaaS = stop thinking about infra, FaaS = functions only. Deployment models Separate question from service models: where does the cloud physically live? Public cloud AWS Regions, shared infrastructure is the default for most workloads — fastest to scale, lowest operational burden. Private cloud your data center with cloud-style APIs exists for data-residency rules or legacy constraints you cannot move. Hybrid public + private linked by Direct Connect or VPN is how real migrations happen: keep the sensitive system where it is, burst new demand to AWS, move gradually. Community cloud shared by organizations with common concerns, e.g. government or research consortia rounds out the list. Decision flow: need full control and already own the capex → private. Need to keep something on-prem but grow in cloud → hybrid. Otherwise → public. --- 3. Six Advantages of Cloud Computing — And What They Buy Each advantage is really an answer to a business pain. Learn the pain, not just the slogan: 1. Trade capital expense for variable expense. Before cloud, launching meant buying servers — a capital outlay approved month"},{"anchor":"3-six-advantages-of-cloud-computing-and-what-they-buy","text":"s in advance. In cloud, spend tracks usage hour by hour. The business impact: capital is freed to invest in product instead of hardware, and failed experiments cost little. 2. Benefit from massive economies of scale. AWS buys hardware, power, and bandwidth at a scale no single company matches, and utilization across millions of customers stays high. Unit prices fall — and AWS has a history of passing those drops to customers. 3. Stop guessing capacity. Traditional planning forces a choice between expensive overprovisioning idle peak fleet and outages underprovision . Elasticity ends the dilemma: scale up for the sale, down at night, automatically if configured. 4. Increase speed and agility. Self-serve APIs plus 200+ services mean a team can go from idea to production infrastructure in an afternoon. Cycle time — the real business metric — collapses from months to minutes. 5. Stop spending money running data centers. Power, cooling, physical security, facility maintenance, hardware refresh cycles: undifferentiated heavy lifting. Every dollar and hour spent there is not spent on what makes the business different. 6. Go global in minutes. Reaching customers on another continent used t"},{"anchor":"4-aws-service-categories-the-map","text":"o mean building there. Now it means selecting another Region. Latency and data-residency requirements are handled by placement, not construction. Mnemonic: T · E · S · S · G · G → Trade · Economies · Stop guessing · Speed · no DCs, Global reach · Go global. How the exam phrases it: \"varying workloads\", \"spiky traffic\", \"unpredictable demand\" all point at elasticity and variable expense. \"Focus on business, not racks\" points at advantage 5. --- 4. AWS Service Categories — The Map The console groups 200+ services into categories. The exam does not ask you to enumerate services; it asks you to recognize which bucket a service belongs to , usually with a scenario. Learn the buckets and anchor each with two or three flagship services: - Compute — where code runs: EC2, Lambda, ECS, Beanstalk. - Storage — where data lives: S3, EBS, EFS, Glacier. - Database — structured data: RDS, DynamoDB, Redshift. - Networking & Content Delivery — how it connects: VPC, Route 53, CloudFront, Direct Connect. - Security, Identity & Compliance — who can do what: IAM, KMS, WAF, Shield. This bucket is MADE of exam answers — when a question involves permissions, encryption, or auditing, look here first. - Mana"},{"anchor":"5-aws-cloud-adoption-framework-caf-six-perspectives","text":"gement & Governance — observe and govern: CloudWatch, CloudTrail, Config, Organizations. - Analytics — big data and streaming: Athena, EMR, Kinesis. - AI/ML — models and intelligence: SageMaker, Rekognition, Comprehend. Two placements the exam loves to test: IAM lives under Security, Identity & Compliance — not Management, even though it feels administrative. VPC lives under Networking & Content Delivery — not Compute, even though EC2 instances live inside VPCs. When in doubt, ask what the service does , not where it is used . --- 5. AWS Cloud Adoption Framework CAF — Six Perspectives CAF is not a product and not a service. It is a planning lens : a way to check that every stakeholder group is ready for the move to cloud, because migrations stall whenever one group is ignored. The framework organizes guidance into six perspectives , and each perspective is a collection of capabilities — concrete outcomes owned by specific people. Walk through them as roles, because the exam names the role and asks for the perspective: - Business business managers, finance, product owners asks whether the move is worth it: business case, value metrics, portfolio prioritization. If the question menti"},{"anchor":"this-module-s-focus-security-operations","text":"ons justifying spend or measuring value, it is Business. - People HR, learning & development, people managers handles the human side: roles, skills, training, org change, culture. \"Skills gap\", \"training plan\", \"resistance to change\" → People. - Governance CIO, PMO, enterprise architects keeps the program under control: governance, risk management, program management. Portfolio-level oversight lives here. - Platform CTO, platform architects designs the technical foundation: architecture patterns, migration strategies, the reusable landing zone every workload deploys into. - Security CISO, security analysts pursues four named goals worth memorizing — visibility, auditability, control, agility — through five capabilities: identity and access management, detective control, infrastructure security, data protection, incident response. - Operations IT operations and support managers keeps the lights on across three time horizons the module names explicitly — day-to-day, quarter-to-quarter, year-to-year — through service monitoring, application performance monitoring, resource inventory, release and change management, reporting and analytics, business continuity and disaster recovery, and"},{"anchor":"6-shared-responsibility-first-look","text":" the IT service catalog. Mnemonic: B · P · G · P · S · O → Business · People · Governance · Platform · Security · Operations. Exam signal: the stakeholder name is the hint. \"CISO\" or \"auditability\" → Security. \"Day-to-day ops\", \"monitoring\", \"BC/DR\" → Operations. \"Business case\" or \"value\" → Business. \"Skills\" or \"training\" → People. This module's focus: Security + Operations The source notes for this module cover two of the six perspectives in detail, because they pair naturally: Security defines the controls, Operations lives with them daily. The Security perspective exists so the organization meets its security objectives systematically rather than bolting controls on after migration. The Operations perspective exists because cloud changes how work gets done — current operating procedures must be written down, process changes identified, and training planned, or adoption fails at the human layer even when the technology is ready. --- 6. Shared Responsibility — First Look One of the most examined ideas in the entire foundations course, introduced here and deepened in Module 4. The split is captured in two short phrases: - AWS: \"Security OF the cloud.\" The physical data centers, t"},{"anchor":"7-aws-well-architected-framework-preview","text":"he hardware, the compute / storage / database infrastructure, the global network of Regions and AZs, and patching of managed services like RDS and Lambda. Everything you never see. - Customer: \"Security IN the cloud.\" Your data and its encryption, your IAM users and policies, OS patching on resources you manage, security group and NACL rules, application code. Everything you touch or configure. The pattern to internalize: the more you configure, the more you own. On EC2 you own the guest OS; on RDS AWS patches the database engine; on Lambda you own only code and the IAM role. The line moves with managed-ness. --- 7. AWS Well-Architected Framework — Preview Where CAF plans the adoption journey , the Well-Architected Framework WAF evaluates the architectures you build once there. Six pillars, each a design discipline: Operational Excellence is running and monitoring systems to deliver business value, with continuous improvement built in — runbooks, game days, blameless retros. Security is protecting data, systems, and assets: confidentiality, integrity, access control, detective mechanisms. The course returns to both constantly. Reliability is recovering from failure and meeting dema"},{"anchor":"8-global-infrastructure-preview-deep-dive-in-module-3","text":"nd: multi-AZ deployment, backups, auto-scaling, chaos testing. Performance Efficiency is using IT resources efficiently: right-sized instance types, serverless where the pattern fits, experiment rather than guess. Cost Optimization is delivering required value at the lowest price: right-sizing, Reserved and Spot usage, tagging for accountability. Sustainability , the newest pillar, is minimizing environmental impact: efficient regions, efficient workloads, measured utilization. Mnemonic: O · S · R · P · C · S → Ops · Security · Reliability · Perf · Cost · Sustainability. --- 8. Global Infrastructure Preview Deep-Dive in Module 3 Three terms, three scopes — learn them now, master them in Module 3. A Region is a geographic area containing two or more Availability Zones; you pick Regions by latency to users, cost differences, data-residency law, and which services are available there. An Availability Zone is one or more discrete data centers with independent power and networking, linked by low-latency fiber; deploying across AZs is how you get high availability. An edge location is a CDN cache for CloudFront and Route 53 — it makes content fast worldwide, but you never deploy servers "},{"anchor":"9-migration-strategies-the-6-rs","text":"to it. --- 9. Migration Strategies — The 6 Rs When an organization moves existing workloads, each one gets classified. The classification drives cost, risk, and timeline, so the exam frames questions as \"which strategy fits this situation\": Rehost \"lift and shift\" moves the workload as-is onto EC2. Fastest, lowest risk, no code changes — but also no cloud benefits beyond escaping the old data center. Replatform lifts with minor changes: swap the self-managed database for RDS, containerize the app. Small effort, real operational gain, no rewrite. Repurchase drops the legacy product for SaaS — the old license server becomes someone else's problem. Right when software reaches end-of-life. Refactor re-architects cloud-native: decompose the monolith, go serverless, adopt managed services throughout. Highest long-term value, highest upfront investment. Retire switches things off — audits always find workloads nobody uses, and migrating them is pure waste. Retain deliberately keeps something on-prem for now: compliance, complexity, or business timing. Order them by effort when reasoning: Retire zero → Retain zero, for now → Rehost → Replatform → Repurchase → Refactor most . --- 10. Knowle"},{"anchor":"10-knowledge-check","text":"dge Check 1. Q: A company runs unpredictable, spiky workloads and wants no upfront hardware cost. Which cloud advantages matter most? A: Trading capital expense for variable expense, plus stop guessing capacity elasticity absorbs the spikes instead of hardware bought for peak . 2. Q: Who owns OS patching on an EC2 instance — AWS or the customer? A: The customer. EC2 is IaaS: AWS owns hardware, virtualization, and the data center; everything from the guest OS up is yours. 3. Q: The CISO asks which CAF perspective covers incident response. A: Security — incident response is one of its five capabilities, alongside IAM, detective control, infrastructure security, and data protection. 4. Q: Where does IAM appear in the console's service categories? A: Security, Identity & Compliance — not Management, despite feeling administrative. 5. Q: A workload must stay on-premises for legal reasons but burst to cloud at peak. Which deployment model, and how is it connected? A: Hybrid, linked by Direct Connect or VPN. --- 11. Key Takeaways - Cloud computing NIST : on-demand, pooled, elastic, measured. Five characteristics, each mapping to real AWS behavior. - Service models move the management line"},{"anchor":"11-key-takeaways","text":": IaaS EC2 → PaaS Beanstalk, RDS → SaaS WorkMail → FaaS Lambda . More AWS-managed as you go right. - Six advantages answer six business pains; exam phrases \"spiky\", \"unpredictable\", \"not racks\" map to elasticity, variable expense, no DCs. - CAF = six perspectives; the stakeholder name reveals the answer. - Shared responsibility: AWS secures OF, you secure IN; the line moves with managed-ness. - Every migration workload gets one of the 6 Rs, ordered by effort. --- 12. Glossary - NIST — National Institute of Standards and Technology; authors the cloud definition. - Elasticity — provisioning and releasing resources on demand. - Multi-tenancy — many customers sharing pooled infrastructure. - Capex / Opex — capital vs operational expense. - IaaS / PaaS / SaaS / FaaS — service models by management split. - Public / Private / Hybrid / Community — deployment models by location. - CAF — Cloud Adoption Framework; six perspectives. - Perspective / Capability — stakeholder grouping / owned outcome. - WAF — Well-Architected Framework; six pillars. - Region / AZ / Edge location — geography / isolated DCs / CDN cache. - 6 Rs — Rehost, Replatform, Repurchase, Refactor, Retire, Retain. - EC2 / S3 /"},{"anchor":"12-glossary","text":" RDS / VPC / IAM / EBS — core service abbreviations. --- 13. Additional Resources - Cloud computing with AWS https://aws.amazon.com/what-is-aws/ - Types of cloud computing https://aws.amazon.com/types-of-cloud-computing/ - AWS Cloud Products https://aws.amazon.com/products/ - Overview of AWS whitepaper PDF https://d1.awsstatic.com/whitepapers/aws-overview.pdf - What is AWS? video https://aws.amazon.com/awstv/watch/3059da9f36d/ - AWS Cloud Adoption Framework whitepaper https://d1.awsstatic.com/whitepapers/aws_cloud_adoption_framework.pdf - 6 Strategies for Migrating Applications to the Cloud https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/enterprise-strategy/6-strategies-for-migrating-applications-to-the-cloud/"}]},"aws-02-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-2-cloud-economics-and-b":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-2-cloud-economics-and-billing","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module2-EconomicsBilling-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS_Module2_CloudEconomicsAndBilling_Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 2: Cloud Economics and Billing Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS_Module2_CloudEconomicsAndBilling_Notes.md --- 1. AWS Pricing Philosophy — Pay Only for What You Use The pricing philosophy is one sentence: pay-as-you-go . You pay only for what you consume, for as long as you consume it — no upfront capital outlay, no long-term lock-in by default. Everything else in this module is a consequence of that sentence. AWS describes four fundamental characteristics of its pricing. Learn them as behaviors, not slogans. Pay as you go means metering: usage is counted per second, per hour, or per gigabyte, and you can start and stop anytime — the default posture for almost every service. Save when you commit is the discount for predictability: if you promise AWS a steady baseline for one or three years Reserved Instances, Savings Plans , the same hardware costs up to ~72% less. Pay less by usi"},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"ng more is tiering: unit prices fall as volume rises, and AWS has a public history of cutting prices as its own scale grows. Custom pricing is the enterprise tier: at large commit volumes, contracts are negotiated rather than list-priced. Mnemonic: P · S · M · C → Pay-as-you-go · Save when reserved · More usage = less · Custom at scale. One confusion to kill early, because the exam exploits it: pay-as-you-go is the overall philosophy, while On-Demand is one specific EC2 purchase option. On-Demand, Reserved, Spot, and Savings Plans all live inside pay-as-you-go. A question saying \"which pricing model charges only for usage\" is asking about the philosophy, not the EC2 option. What you actually pay for Nearly every AWS bill reduces to three dimensions. Compute is time on processors: EC2 hours, Lambda invocations plus GB-seconds, Fargate vCPU-hours — mostly per-second billing. Storage is gigabyte-months plus requests: S3 storage and API calls, EBS gigabytes provisioned, EFS gigabytes stored — where the storage class and region change the rate. Data transfer out is bytes leaving AWS: EC2 to the internet, inter-Region traffic, CloudFront to origin — tiered, with the per-GB price falling "},{"anchor":"1-aws-pricing-philosophy-pay-only-for-what-you-use","text":"as volume rises. The asymmetry to memorize: inbound transfer is free , and much intra-AWS traffic is free intra-AZ, many intra-Region service-to-service paths, uploads to S3 . Outbound is metered. When a scenario asks what drives a bill, look for the outbound arrow. --- 2. EC2 Pricing — The Exam's Favorite Example EC2 pricing is where the abstract philosophy becomes concrete options, and the exam returns to it constantly. Each option answers a different workload shape: On-Demand is the baseline: no commitment, pay by the second, highest unit price. It exists for the unknown — spiky, unpredictable, short-lived work where any commitment would be a gamble. Reserved Instances trade a one- or three-year promise of steady usage for up to ~72% off the same capacity: databases, baseline fleets, anything you know will run. Savings Plans offer similar discounts for a different promise — dollars per hour rather than a specific instance type — which keeps the discount when you change families or sizes. Spot Instances sell spare AWS capacity at up to ~90% off, with one catch: AWS can reclaim the capacity with a two-minute warning. That catch defines the use case — fault-tolerant batch jobs, CI "},{"anchor":"what-you-actually-pay-for","text":"pipelines, anything that checkpoints and restarts. Dedicated Hosts reserve whole physical servers: the answer for bring-your-own-license deals, per-socket licensing, or compliance regimes that demand single-tenancy. Capacity Reservations hold AZ capacity at On-Demand price with no discount at all — purely a guarantee for critical events. Recap table use after reading, not instead of it : Option Commitment Discount vs On-Demand Best for --- --- --- --- On-Demand None — pay by second Baseline 0% Spiky, unpredictable, short-lived Reserved RI 1 or 3 years, steady state Up to ~72% Databases, baseline fleet Savings Plans Commit $/hour for 1–3 yr Similar to RI, more flexible RI savings without pinning instance type Spot None — spare, interruptible Up to ~90% Batch, fault-tolerant, flexible timing Dedicated Hosts Per-host reservation Separate pricing BYOL, per-socket licensing, compliance Capacity Reservations Per-AZ hold None On-Demand price Guarantee capacity for critical events Reasoning pattern: can it be interrupted? → Spot. Predictable around the clock? → Reserved / Savings. Unknown or spiky? → On-Demand. Must be guaranteed to exist? → Capacity Reservation. Two traps live here. First"},{"anchor":"2-ec2-pricing-the-exam-s-favorite-example","text":", Reserved Instances are a billing discount, not a capacity lock — nothing about an RI guarantees the hardware exists when you need it; that is what Capacity Reservations are for. Second, Spot is never the answer for production workloads that cannot tolerate interruption, no matter how attractive the discount. Mnemonic: O · R · S · S · D · C → On-Demand · Reserved · Savings · Spot · Dedicated · Capacity Reservation. The Free Tier Always Free portions, 12-months Free like t2.micro and starter S3, plus trials matters for labs, but remember caps apply — it is not unlimited, and the exam occasionally tests that boundary. --- 3. Total Cost of Ownership — The Honest Comparison TCO is the exam's way of asking: did you count the costs you can't see? An on-premises server's price tag is a fraction of its lifetime cost. The rest hides in facilities and labor: servers plus depreciation and refresh cycles, racks, power, cooling, physical space, network hardware and transit, firewalls, storage arrays and SANs, backups, the IT labor of racking, patching, and tickets, software licenses and support contracts. AWS folds nearly all of that into its per-unit price — which is why a naive \"server vs EC"},{"anchor":"3-total-cost-of-ownership-the-honest-comparison","text":"2 hour\" comparison always understates the cloud advantage. Two calculators serve two moments. The Pricing Calculator estimates a planned AWS architecture before you build — service by service, Region-aware. The TCO Calculator compares that plan against staying on-premises, surfacing the hidden costs and projecting across typically three years. Calculator = estimate of the future; the bill, Cost Explorer, and CUR below = record of the present and past. Mnemonic TCO inputs : S · C · L · N · B · I → Server · Cooling · License · Network · Backup · Idle capacity. --- 4. AWS Organizations — Many Accounts, One Control Plane Real AWS estates do not live in one account. Best practice is many accounts — development, test, staging, production, per-workload, per-team — grouped into Organizational Units OUs and governed centrally. AWS Organizations is the free service that makes this manageable: a management account pays one consolidated bill for all member accounts, combined usage pushes the organization into better volume tiers, and policies flow downward through the OU tree: The load-bearing concept is the Service Control Policy SCP . An SCP sets the maximum an account can do — a ceiling, a "},{"anchor":"4-aws-organizations-many-accounts-one-control-plane","text":"guardrail. It never grants anything: some IAM policy inside the account must still allow the action. The exam's favorite formulation: \"SCPs restrict — IAM grants.\" An account with no explicit SCP restriction and no IAM allow still cannot act, and an IAM allow blocked by an SCP also cannot act. Both gates must pass. Beyond billing and SCPs, Organizations carries tag policies standardizing cost-allocation tags across accounts so spend reports slice cleanly and backup policies centralized backup governance . The through-line: define once at the OU, inherit everywhere below. --- 5. Billing & Cost Management — The Suite Four tools plus two helpers, each answering a different verb. Learn the verb, and the tool follows: AWS Bills is the invoice itself — month-to-date and itemized, by service and linked account. Where finance looks first. Cost Explorer charts and forecasts spend: past twelve months visualized, twelve months projected, filterable by service, account, tag, and Region — the tool for \"why did spend move.\" AWS Budgets watches thresholds: set a cost, usage, or RI budget and get email or SNS alerts when actual or forecasted spend crosses it. Critical nuance: budgets alert ; they "},{"anchor":"5-billing-amp-cost-management-the-suite","text":"never stop spend. Cost and Usage Report CUR is the raw material: hourly line items per resource per tag, delivered as CSV to S3 for querying in Athena, Redshift, or QuickSight. FinOps teams live here. Anomaly Detection adds machine learning over the spend stream, alerting on unusual patterns without manual thresholds. And the Billing Dashboard is the top-line glance — service totals and month-over-month trend. Pick by verb: \"show my invoice\" → Bills. \"Chart and forecast\" → Cost Explorer. \"Alert me at $X\" → Budgets. \"Analyze every line item\" → CUR. Tags deserve their own paragraph because they power half the suite. Tags are key/value labels Env=Prod , Team=Data attached to resources. Activated as cost allocation tags , they become the dimensions Cost Explorer and CUR slice by — team, project, environment. Untagged resources are invisible to chargeback; tagging discipline is a cost-management practice, not decoration. Mnemonic: B · B · C · B · C · A → Billing · Bills · Cost Explorer · Budgets · CUR · Anomaly. --- 6. Support Plans — Which Level When Four tiers, and the exam tests the thresholds — especially response times and the Technical Account Manager. Basic is free and universal:"},{"anchor":"6-support-plans-which-level-when","text":" account and billing help, documentation, forums — but no technical support cases at all. Developer ~$29/mo adds business-hours email with general guidance; it is for experimenting, explicitly not production. Business ~$100/mo plus usage is the production floor: 24×7 phone/chat/email, full Trusted Advisor, and sub-one-hour response on impaired production systems. Enterprise On-Ramp ~$5,500/mo covers production plus some mission-critical needs with 15-minute response on business-critical events. Enterprise ~$15,000/mo is mission-critical: a dedicated Technical Account Manager TAM — your named AWS advocate — 15-minute response, Concierge support, event management, full Trusted Advisor. Mnemonic: B · D · B · E · E → Basic · Developer · Business · Enterprise On-Ramp · Enterprise. Triggers: \"15-minute, business-critical\" → Enterprise. \"24×7 phone/chat for production\" → Business. \"Business-hours email, testing\" → Developer. \"Billing only\" → Basic. TAM always means Enterprise. Dollar figures are approximate and region-dependent; the exam tests capabilities and response times, not prices. IEM Infrastructure Event Management — short-term AWS engineers embedded for planned events like launch"},{"anchor":"7-knowledge-check","text":"es — rides on the Enterprise tiers. Decision pattern: production with SLA requirements → Business minimum. Mission-critical → Enterprise. Testing only → Developer. --- 7. Knowledge Check 1. Q: A startup runs spiky, unpredictable batch jobs that can restart safely. Cheapest EC2 option, and why? A: Spot Instances — up to ~90% off spare capacity, and the workload's restart-safety absorbs the reclaim risk. 2. Q: Finance wants an alert at 80% of the monthly budget. Which tool, and what won't it do? A: AWS Budgets. It alerts via email/SNS; it will not stop or cap spend. 3. Q: Can an SCP grant an S3 permission that no IAM policy allows? A: No — SCPs only restrict. An IAM policy inside the account must still grant the action; both gates must pass. 4. Q: Is inbound data transfer to AWS charged? A: No — inbound is free; outbound is metered and tiered. 5. Q: A company needs 15-minute response for business-critical outages plus a named AWS contact. Which plan, and what is the contact called? A: Enterprise; the contact is the Technical Account Manager TAM . --- 8. Key Takeaways - Pay only for consumption; commit RI/Savings for steady state; Spot for interruptible; On-Demand for the unknown; Cap"},{"anchor":"8-key-takeaways","text":"acity Reservations guarantee. - RI is a billing discount, not a capacity lock. Spot never suits interruption-intolerant production. - TCO counts hidden on-prem costs; calculators estimate the future, bills and CUR record the past. - Organizations = many accounts, one bill, OUs, SCP guardrails. SCPs restrict; IAM grants. - Bills invoice · Explorer forecast · Budgets alert, never block · CUR raw . Tags power the slicing. - Support scales Basic → Developer → Business → Enterprise, with TAM at the top. --- 9. Glossary - Pay-as-you-go — consumption billing, no upfront. - Reserved Instance RI — 1–3 yr commit, up to ~72% off. - Savings Plan — $/hour commit, flexible across instance family. - Spot Instance — spare capacity, up to ~90% off, reclaimable 2-min notice . - Dedicated Host — physical server for BYOL/compliance. - Capacity Reservation — guaranteed AZ capacity at On-Demand price. - Free Tier — Always Free + 12-month + trial allowances capped . - TCO — Total Cost of Ownership. - Pricing / TCO Calculator — future estimate vs on-prem comparison. - AWS Organizations — multi-account management + consolidated billing. - OU — Organizational Unit account group . - SCP — Service Control Pol"},{"anchor":"9-glossary","text":"icy OU-level guardrail, restrict-only . - Bills / Cost Explorer / Budgets / CUR — invoice / visualize+forecast / threshold alerts / raw line items. - Cost allocation tags — tags activated for billing reports. - Anomaly Detection — ML spend-spike alerts. - TAM — Technical Account Manager Enterprise . - IEM — Infrastructure Event Management. --- 10. Additional Resources - AWS Pricing https://aws.amazon.com/pricing/ - AWS Pricing Calculator https://calculator.aws/ - AWS Cost Management https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/ - AWS Organizations https://aws.amazon.com/organizations/ - Organizations User Guide https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_introduction.html - AWS Billing User Guide https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/billing-what-is.html - Cost & Usage Report https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cur/latest/userguide/what-is-cur.html - Cost Explorer https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/aws-cost-explorer/ - AWS Budgets https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/aws-budgets/ - AWS Support Plans https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/plans/"}]},"aws-03-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-3-global-infrastructure":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-3-global-infrastructure-overview","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module3-GlobalInfra-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS_Module3_GlobalInfrastructureOverview_Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 3: Global Infrastructure Overview Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS_Module3_GlobalInfrastructureOverview_Notes.md --- 1. Infrastructure Hierarchy — How AWS Physically Nests AWS infrastructure is a nesting doll, and every question about placement reduces to knowing which layer contains which: A Region is a geographic area — Northern Virginia, London, São Paulo — fully independent, containing two or more Availability Zones. There are 30+. An Availability Zone is one or more discrete data centers with independent power, cooling, networking, and connectivity, stitched to its sibling AZs by low-latency private fiber. There are 100+. A data center is the physical building with the racks. Edge locations 600+ across 90+ cities sit outside this nesting: they are CDN caches at the network frontier, not places you deploy servers. Mnemonic: C · R · A · D · E → Cloud → Region → AZ → Data Center"},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":" → Edge. The single most important consequence: failure domains follow the nesting. An AZ failure is contained because sibling AZs have independent power and network. A Region failure is contained because Regions share nothing. This is why deployment guidance always says \"across AZs\" for ordinary resilience and \"across Regions\" only for disaster-grade scenarios. One more fact that surprises newcomers: edge locations cannot run EC2. They serve CloudFront and Route 53 plus S3 Transfer Acceleration and Shield . They exist for delivery speed, never for compute. Any exam option placing workloads \"in an edge location\" is wrong on arrival. --- 2. Region Selection — Four Criteria in Order Choosing a Region is a decision procedure, and the criteria have a priority order the exam respects. First, compliance and data residency : GDPR, ITAR, HIPAA, FedRAMP, and sovereign clouds GovCloud for US public sector, the China regions operated by local partners under Chinese law can legally require data to stay in a jurisdiction. Law beats everything. Second, latency and proximity : pick closest to the users — the round-trip gap between Northern Virginia and Ireland is on the order of 80ms, which decid"},{"anchor":"1-infrastructure-hierarchy-how-aws-physically-nests","text":"es real-time applications outright. Third, service availability : newer services launch in a few regions first us-east-1 almost always earliest , so the Region must actually offer what the architecture needs. Fourth, pricing : the same service can cost ~30% more in São Paulo than in Northern Virginia. Special-purpose infrastructure extends the map without changing the model: Local Zones put AWS capacity inside specific metro areas for single-digit-millisecond needs, Wavelength embeds it in 5G carrier networks, and Outposts ships AWS hardware into your own data center. All three answer \"the Region is too far\" without abandoning AWS APIs. --- 3. Global vs Regional vs AZ Scope The console's Region selector is the visible symptom of scope. Global services ignore it — one logical endpoint and configuration covering all Regions automatically. Regional services are configured per Region and isolated between Regions; switching the selector shows a different set of resources. Zonal AZ-scoped resources live inside a single AZ. Scope Meaning Examples know cold --- --- --- Global One endpoint, available everywhere IAM, Route 53, CloudFront, WAF, Shield, Organizations, Artifact Regional Scoped "},{"anchor":"2-region-selection-four-criteria-in-order","text":"to one Region; selected on creation EC2, Lambda, RDS, VPC, DynamoDB, ELB, S3 buckets, ECS, EKS Zonal Exists in a specific AZ Subnet, EC2 instance, EBS volume, ENI, EFS mount target Mnemonic: G · R · Z → Global · Regional · Zonal. Three facts do heavy lifting across the whole course. First, subnets live in one AZ while VPCs live in one Region spanning all its AZs through those subnets — the Module 5 preview that explains half of all networking questions. Second, S3 bucket names are globally unique, but bucket data lives in the Region you pick at creation — \"global name, regional data.\" Third, AZ identifiers are randomized per account : your us-east-1a may be my us-east-1b, which is why architectures say \"two AZs\" rather than naming letters. --- 4. Edge Locations + CloudFront — How Delivery Works Follow a request and the edge model clicks into place. A user in Tokyo asks for content: the request hits the nearest edge location first. On a cache hit , the edge answers in milliseconds and the origin never hears about it. On a miss , the request climbs one tier to Origin Shield and the Regional Edge Cache — intermediate layers whose job is absorbing misses so the origin serves as little "},{"anchor":"3-global-vs-regional-vs-az-scope","text":"as possible — and only then reaches the origin itself: an S3 bucket, EC2 fleet, load balancer, or custom URL. The supporting cast: signed URLs grant temporary access to private content through the CDN, and Lambda@Edge runs small code at edges for personalization rewrites, headers, A/B variants . Route 53's anycast network answers DNS from the nearest edge, which is why DNS itself benefits from the same geography. Exam favorite, stated plainly: \"low-latency content delivery\" or \"cache close to users\" always resolves to edge locations via CloudFront . --- 5. Resilience Vocabulary — HA vs FT vs DR The exam deliberately blurs these three; keep them separated by asking what failure each survives. High Availability survives component failure — the pattern is deploying across at least two AZs in one Region load balancer plus Auto Scaling group spanning AZs , so one data-center event does not take the workload down. Fault Tolerance is strictly stronger: the system operates without interruption despite failure, typically active-active with state replication — no failover pause at all. Disaster Recovery survives Region-level events through cross-Region replication, and it is specified by two"},{"anchor":"4-edge-locations-cloudfront-how-delivery-works","text":" objectives: RTO Recovery Time Objective — how fast to be back and RPO Recovery Point Objective — how much data loss is tolerable, which sets backup frequency . DR pattern When Trade-off --- --- --- Single Region, Multi-AZ Most workloads HA in-region; whole-region risk remains Multi-Region active-passive DR requirement Warm standby in a second region Multi-Region active-active Global users, RTO≈0 Highest complexity + cost Pilot light Minimal DR footprint Core always running; scale up on failover Rule of thumb: AZs protect against data-center failure. Regions protect against large-scale disaster. Edge has nothing to do with durability — it is delivery speed, and any option mixing the two is a distractor. --- 6. Console Walkthrough — Mental Model The lab activity is really four facts wearing a click-through disguise. First, the Services menu groups by category: IAM sits under Security, Identity & Compliance; VPC sits under Networking & Content Delivery — placements the exam retests see Module 1, §4 . Second, the Region switch in the top-right changes which regional resources the console shows; global services like IAM look identical regardless. Third, VPC vs subnet : the VPC is the R"},{"anchor":"5-resilience-vocabulary-ha-vs-ft-vs-dr","text":"egional container, subnets are its AZ-scoped slices, and an EC2 instance launches into a subnet — therefore into an AZ. Fourth, the lab answers fall out directly: subnet → AZ, VPC → Region, IAM and Route 53 global, EC2 and Lambda Regional. --- 7. Service Categories Map Category Core services --- --- Compute EC2, Lambda, ECS, Beanstalk Storage S3, EBS, EFS, Glacier Database RDS, DynamoDB, Redshift Networking VPC, Route 53, CloudFront Security & IAM IAM, KMS, WAF Management CloudWatch, CloudTrail --- 8. Knowledge Check 1. Q: Which infrastructure component does CloudFront use for low-latency delivery, and why not a Region? A: Edge locations — they cache content near users; Regions host compute, not delivery caches. 2. Q: A subnet in eu-west-2a — Region or AZ scoped, and why? A: AZ. Subnets are slices of a VPC pinned to exactly one AZ. 3. Q: Which are global among EC2, IAM, Lambda, Route 53? A: IAM and Route 53. EC2 and Lambda are Regional. 4. Q: HA vs FT — which is stronger, and what distinguishes them? A: Fault tolerance: it requires no interruption , while HA only requires surviving the failure failover pause allowed . 5. Q: Customer data must stay in the EU for legal reasons. Which"},{"anchor":"6-console-walkthrough-mental-model","text":" Region selection criterion dominates, and what beats price? A: Compliance / data residency — law outranks latency, availability, and price. --- 9. Key Takeaways - Region = geography latency, residency, cost ; AZ = isolated DCs joined by private fiber; Edge = delivery cache, never compute. - VPC spans a Region; subnet, instance, and volume live in one AZ; IAM is global. - HA ≥2 AZs vs FT no interruption vs DR cross-Region, specified by RTO/RPO . - Region selection order: compliance → latency → availability → price. - Edge locations never run EC2; \"global name, regional data\" for S3. --- 10. Glossary - Region — geographic cluster of 2+ AZs. - Availability Zone AZ — isolated data center s in a Region. - Edge Location / PoP — CDN cache endpoint. - Regional Edge Cache — intermediate CloudFront tier. - Origin / Origin Shield — content source / extra cache tier. - Multi-AZ / Multi-Region — distribution patterns for HA / DR. - Local Zone — AWS capacity inside a specific metro. - Wavelength — AWS infrastructure in 5G carrier networks. - Outposts — AWS hardware in your data center. - GovCloud — isolated US public-sector region. - RTO / RPO — recovery time / point objectives. - HA / FT / DR "},{"anchor":"7-service-categories-map","text":"— availability, tolerance, recovery. --- 11. Additional Resources - AWS Global Infrastructure https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/ - AWS Regions https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regions/ - Availability Zones https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/availability-zones/ - Regional Services List https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/global-infrastructure/regional-product-services/ - Amazon CloudFront https://aws.amazon.com/cloudfront/ - AWS Products https://aws.amazon.com/products/"}]},"aws-04-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-4-cloud-security":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-4-cloud-security","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module4-CloudSecurity-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS_Module4_CloudSecurity_Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 4: Cloud Security Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS_Module4_CloudSecurity_Notes.md --- 1. Shared Responsibility — The One Model to Memorize Security in AWS is always shared, and the single most examined idea in this module is where the split falls. Two short phrases carry it: AWS: \"Security OF the cloud.\" Everything you never see: physical data centers and hardware, the host operating system and virtualization layer, the global network of Regions and AZs, patching of managed services the RDS database engine, the Lambda runtime , and the availability and durability of the platform itself. Customer: \"Security IN the cloud.\" Everything you touch or configure: your data and its encryption at rest, in transit, and in use; your IAM users, groups, roles, and policies; guest OS patching on resources you manage; security group and NACL rules; application code and its dependencies; client-side data. The "},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"pattern to internalize rather than memorize rows: the more you configure, the more you own. On EC2 IaaS you own the guest OS upward. On RDS managed AWS patches the database engine while you keep IAM, networking, encryption configuration, and data. On Lambda serverless AWS owns runtime, scaling, and patching while you keep the function code, its IAM role, and its data. On S3 AWS owns durability and infrastructure while you keep bucket and IAM policies, encryption choices, versioning, and the data itself. And your data is always yours — no service tier transfers that. Mnemonic: AWS = \"of\" · Customer = \"in\". The classic trap, stated plainly: \"AWS is responsible for security\" is half-true. AWS owns OF — physical, host, hypervisor. You always own your data, your IAM, and any OS or network controls on resources you manage. --- 2. IAM — The Gate to Everything Identity and Access Management is the gate every AWS API call passes through first. Three facts frame the whole section: IAM is global a user created in one Region exists everywhere , evaluation is deny-first an explicit deny beats any number of allows , and everything is an API call so controlling IAM controls the account . Four ent"},{"anchor":"1-shared-responsibility-the-one-model-to-memorize","text":"ities, four distinct jobs. A user is a long-term identity for a person or an application, carrying durable credentials — give each human their own user, never share. A group bundles users so a policy attached once flows to all members; groups organize, they cannot be assumed . A role is assumed temporarily through the Security Token Service STS for a limited session: EC2 instances reaching S3, Lambda execution, cross-account access, federated single sign-on. No long-term credentials exist for a role, which is exactly why roles are safer. A policy is the JSON document Effect + Action + Resource attached to users, groups, or roles that states what is allowed or denied. Policies come in flavors worth distinguishing. Identity-based policies attach to users, groups, or roles. Resource-based policies live inline on the resource itself — S3 buckets, SNS topics, SQS queues. AWS managed policies like ReadOnlyAccess are pre-built starting points. Customer managed policies are yours: versioned, reusable, narrowable. Inline policies are embedded directly in a single identity. Above them all sit Service Control Policies SCPs from Organizations Module 2 : they filter the maximum an account's pol"},{"anchor":"2-iam-the-gate-to-everything","text":"icies can grant, and they never grant anything themselves. Credentials, by use Console passwords sign humans into the console — pair them with MFA and a real password policy. Access keys drive the CLI, SDKs, and API calls — rotate them, never hard-code or commit them, and prefer roles wherever a machine not a person needs access. MFA devices virtual or hardware protect sign-in and privileged actions; required for root and admins. Key pairs split SSH/RDP access: the public key stays with AWS, and the private key's secrecy is entirely your problem. STS session tokens are what assuming a role returns: temporary, auto-expiring, the mechanism that makes roles work. Principles that win marks Least privilege — grant only what is needed today, expand deliberately later. Prefer roles over long-term keys for services and cross-account paths. Explicit deny wins over any allow, which is how break-glass denials and guardrails stay reliable. Root is not for daily use — ever. --- 3. Hardening a New Account — Do This First, In This Order The module teaches account hardening as a sequence, and the order is the lesson — each step protects the next: 1. Lock the root user. Enable MFA on root, set a st"},{"anchor":"credentials-by-use","text":"rong password, delete any root access keys, store credentials offline. Root is unlimited and irreplaceable; it must also be almost never used. 2. Create an admin principal. An IAM user or role or SSO identity with AdministratorAccess, MFA enabled — this becomes the daily driver going forward. 3. Enforce MFA broadly. Every IAM user with console access, especially anyone holding sensitive permissions. Consider MFA conditions on privileged API calls too. 4. Apply least-privilege policies. AdministratorAccess stops at the admin principal. Normal users start from AWS managed policies and narrow with custom policies; assign through groups so permissions stay reviewable. 5. Turn on visibility. CloudTrail in all Regions writing to encrypted S3 the audit trail of who did what , Config configuration history plus rules evaluating what changed , GuardDuty threat detection for what looks malicious . 6. Organize and guard. Multi-account means Organizations plus SCPs plus consolidated CloudTrail — and consistent tagging, which is governance as much as cost control Module 2 . 7. Run Access Analyzer to surface unintended resource access the policies above missed. The trap embedded in step one: root"},{"anchor":"principles-that-win-marks","text":" access keys must be deleted , never used for programmatic access. Code and scripts get IAM users or, better, roles — root credentials touch nothing routine. --- 4. Protecting Data — Three States, Three Toolkits Data needs different protection depending on where it is. At rest — disks, snapshots, backups — the threat is theft or leakage of the stored object, and the answer is server-side encryption: SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, or SSE-C for S3 objects; EBS and RDS encryption; KMS Customer Master Keys, AWS-managed or customer-managed, underneath nearly all of it. In transit — bytes on the wire — the threat is interception, and the answer is TLS/HTTPS certificates from ACM, terminated at load balancers, CloudFront, or API Gateway , VPNs, Direct Connect with MACsec, and S3 endpoint policies keeping traffic off the public internet. In use — data being processed — the threat is exposure during computation, and the answer is CloudHSM dedicated hardware security modules , Nitro Enclaves, or encrypting client-side before upload so AWS never sees plaintext. S3 encryption options, compared by key holder SSE-S3 AES-256 keeps keys with AWS: the default, zero setup, right when control requirements are ligh"},{"anchor":"3-hardening-a-new-account-do-this-first-in-this-order","text":"t. SSE-KMS routes through KMS with an AWS-managed CMK: same convenience plus CloudTrail-audited key usage, right when you must prove who decrypted what. SSE-C lets you bring your own key per request while AWS performs the encryption: right when policy demands customer-held keys but operations stay server-side. Client-side encryption never lets AWS see plaintext at all: right for the strictest regimes, at the cost of owning the whole key lifecycle. Mnemonic: S3 · KMS · C · C → SSE-S3 · SSE-KMS · SSE-C · Client-side. EBS encryption AES-256 at rest, KMS-managed, transparent to EC2 and RDS encryption at rest via KMS plus in transit via TLS, enabled at creation follow the same pattern: declare it, KMS enforces it, the service absorbs the complexity. --- 5. KMS, Secrets, and Certificates — Know Which Solves What KMS is the center of gravity: create, rotate, and control encryption keys in one place, integrated with S3, EBS, RDS, and nearly everything else. The exam's phrasing — \"every encrypted service calls KMS\" — is close to literally true. Secrets Manager stores database passwords and API keys with built-in rotation ; Parameter Store Systems Manager stores hierarchical key/value config"},{"anchor":"4-protecting-data-three-states-three-toolkits","text":"uration with a free tier but no auto-rotation. The decision between them is one question: does the secret need rotation? Rotating → Secrets Manager. Static → Parameter Store. CloudHSM is the escalation beyond KMS: dedicated hardware security modules at FIPS 140-2 Level 3, for when shared-tenant key management is not enough. ACM provisions and auto-renews TLS certificates for load balancers, CloudFront, and API Gateway — terminate TLS at the edge, keep encryption inside the VPC. --- 6. Threat Detection & Security Services — One-Liners That Decide Scenarios The exam tests which service fits the scenario , not deep configuration. Learn each as a single sentence. CloudTrail records every API call: who did what, when — the audit trail. Config records what changed : resource configuration history plus rules evaluating compliance over time. GuardDuty detects what looks malicious : machine-learning threat detection over API calls, network flow, and DNS. Security Hub is the central posture dashboard aggregating findings across accounts. Inspector scans EC2 instances and container images for known vulnerabilities. Macie finds sensitive data PII, credentials sitting in S3. WAF is the Layer-7 "},{"anchor":"s3-encryption-options-compared-by-key-holder","text":"web application firewall in front of CloudFront and load balancers. Shield is DDoS protection — Standard free and automatic, Advanced paid with response-team support. The visibility trio compresses to: Trail who · Config what changed · GuardDuty malicious . When a question asks for detection, start there. --- 7. Compliance — Reports, Evidence, and Programs Compliance on AWS has three layers. Programs are the certifications AWS holds: ISO 27001/27017/27018 globally, SOC 1/2/3, PCI DSS Level 1 for card payments, HIPAA-eligible services for US healthcare, FedRAMP for US federal, GDPR alignment for EU data, GovCloud as an isolated region for US public sector. Artifact is the self-service portal where you download those reports for auditors — SOC, ISO, PCI — on demand, no ticket needed. Audit Manager produces continuous audit evidence for regulated workloads, and Config enforces configuration-compliance rules over time. Exam shorthand: \"need the SOC 2 report\" → Artifact. \"See every API call\" → CloudTrail. \"Temporary S3 access for EC2\" → IAM Role, never access keys. \"Who patches the EC2 guest OS?\" → customer. \"The host or hypervisor?\" → AWS. --- 8. Knowledge Check 1. Q: Who patches the h"},{"anchor":"5-kms-secrets-and-certificates-know-which-solves-what","text":"ypervisor under your EC2 instance, and which half of the model is that? A: AWS — Security OF the cloud infrastructure you never see . 2. Q: An S3 bucket policy versus an IAM user policy — which is resource-based, and why does it matter? A: The bucket policy: it travels with the resource and can grant cross-account access without touching the other account's IAM. 3. Q: Why are roles preferred over access keys for Lambda execution? A: Roles yield temporary STS credentials that auto-expire and rotate — no long-term secret exists to leak from code or logs. 4. Q: First three steps securing a new account, in order? A: MFA on root plus deleting root keys → admin principal with MFA → MFA everywhere plus least-privilege policies. Order matters because each step protects the next. 5. Q: Data must be encrypted before it reaches AWS at all. Which option, and what does AWS never see? A: Client-side encryption — AWS never sees plaintext; you own the full key lifecycle. --- 9. Key Takeaways - OF = AWS physical, host, hypervisor . IN = you data, IAM, OS, encryption . The line moves with managed-ness: EC2 you own more, Lambda AWS owns more. - IAM: users long-term · groups bundle · roles temporary S"},{"anchor":"6-threat-detection-amp-security-services-one-liners-that-decide-scenarios","text":"TS · policies JSON. Least privilege, MFA, roles over keys, explicit deny wins. Global. - Harden in order: MFA root → admin principal → MFA all → least privilege → CloudTrail / Config / GuardDuty → Organizations + SCPs. - Data: at rest KMS/SSE · in transit TLS/ACM/VPN · in use CloudHSM . Rotation need decides Secrets Manager vs Parameter Store. - Artifact for reports; Trail/Config/GuardDuty for visibility; Hub/Macie/ Inspector/WAF/Shield as scenario one-liners. --- 10. Glossary - Root account — signup identity; unlimited, restricted use. - IAM — Identity and Access Management global service . - User / Group / Role / Policy — long-term identity / bundle / assumed temporary identity / JSON permission document. - STS — Security Token Service temporary credentials . - SSO — Single Sign-On one login across accounts . - MFA — Multi-Factor Authentication. - KMS / CMK — Key Management Service / Customer Master Key. - SSE-S3 / SSE-KMS / SSE-C — S3 encryption variants by key holder. - TLS / ACM — transit encryption / certificate manager. - VPN / Direct Connect — encrypted tunnel / dedicated fiber. - CloudHSM — dedicated HSM FIPS 140-2 Level 3 . - Nitro Enclaves — isolated compute for sensitiv"},{"anchor":"7-compliance-reports-evidence-and-programs","text":"e processing. - CloudTrail / Config / GuardDuty — audit / history / threats. - Security Hub / Macie / Inspector — posture / PII discovery / vulns. - WAF / Shield — Layer-7 firewall / DDoS protection. - Artifact / Audit Manager — compliance reports / audit evidence. - Secrets Manager / Parameter Store — secrets with / without rotation. --- 11. Additional Resources - Shared Responsibility Model https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/ - IAM User Guide https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html - IAM Best Practices https://aws.amazon.com/iam/features/credentials/best-practices/ - CloudTrail https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/ - Config https://aws.amazon.com/config/ - GuardDuty https://aws.amazon.com/guardduty/ - Security Hub https://aws.amazon.com/security-hub/ - KMS https://aws.amazon.com/kms/ - Secrets Manager https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/ - Artifact https://aws.amazon.com/artifact/ - Compliance Programs https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/"}]},"aws-05-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-5-networking-and-conten":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-5-networking-and-content-delivery","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module5-Networking-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS-Module5-Networking-Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 5: Networking and Content Delivery Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS-Module5-Networking-Notes.md --- 1. VPC Architecture — Your Private Network, Layer by Layer A Virtual Private Cloud is your isolated network inside AWS, and the way to learn it is top-down, because every component's scope follows from its position: The VPC itself is Regional: one container spanning every AZ of its Region. Inside it, subnets are AZ-scoped slices — each subnet lives in exactly one AZ, which is why multi-AZ design means multiple subnets. A route table attached to each subnet decides where its traffic goes, and that decision is what makes a subnet \"public\" or \"private\": a route to an Internet Gateway IGW — the VPC's door to the internet — means public; a route to a NAT Gateway itself sitting in a public subnet means private subnets can initiate outbound traffic, like software updates, without ever accepting inbound conne"},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"ctions from the internet. Beyond the single VPC, three connectivity pieces complete the picture. VPC peering is a direct one-to-one link between two VPCs — and crucially not transitive : peering A→B and B→C does not connect A→C. Transit Gateway is the hub-and-spoke answer when many VPCs and on-prem networks must interconnect without a mesh of peerings. VPC endpoints let traffic to AWS services S3, DynamoDB stay on the private AWS network instead of traversing the internet at all. Component What it does --- --- VPC Isolated virtual network; Regional, spans all AZs Subnet IP range in exactly one AZ Route Table Per-subnet traffic rules; defines public vs private Internet Gateway IGW VPC-attached internet entry/exit NAT Gateway Outbound-only internet for private subnets VPC Peering 1-to-1 inter-VPC link no transit Transit Gateway Hub for many VPCs + on-prem VPC Endpoint Private service link, no internet --- 2. Security Groups vs NACLs — Two Firewalls, Different Jobs Newcomers conflate these because both filter traffic. They differ in three independent dimensions, and the exam tests each pairing. Scope: security groups attach to ENIs instances ; NACLs attach to subnets — so a security g"},{"anchor":"1-vpc-architecture-your-private-network-layer-by-layer","text":"roup protects what , a NACL protects where . State: security groups are stateful — allow an inbound request and the return traffic flows automatically; NACLs are stateless — every direction needs its own explicit rule, including the ephemeral-port return path. Rules: security groups express allow only default deny inbound ; NACLs express allow and deny , evaluated by rule number, lowest first. Security Group Network ACL --- --- --- Scope ENI / instance Subnet State Stateful — return auto-allowed Stateless — return needs explicit rule Rules Allow only Allow + Deny numbered, lowest first Default Deny all inbound Allow all Association Many groups per ENI 1 NACL per subnet at a time Layer L4 + L5 L4 only The association rules follow from the design: subnets with no explicit NACL use the default; one NACL may cover many subnets, but a subnet sits in exactly one NACL at a time, and re-associating silently removes the previous one. Mnemonic: SG = \"Stateful, Good allow \" · NACL = \"Network, All rules allow+deny , stateless\". --- 3. Route 53 Routing Policies — Directing DNS Answers Route 53 is AWS's authoritative DNS, and its routing policies decide which answer a DNS query receives. Each po"},{"anchor":"2-security-groups-vs-nacls-two-firewalls-different-jobs","text":"licy answers a different traffic question. Simple round-robins across records for a single resource. Weighted splits by assigned weight 0–255 : two records weighted 3 and 1 send 75%/25% — the mechanism behind A/B tests and canary rollouts. Latency-based measures real network latency and answers with the lowest-latency Region: the multi-Region default. Geolocation routes by the user's continent or country — content localization, language selection, rights-restricted distribution. Geoproximity routes by the resource's location with an adjustable bias — shifting load between regions. Failover watches health checks and flips from primary to standby: active-passive DR at the DNS layer. Multivalue answer returns up to eight healthy records chosen at random — DNS-level distribution that is explicitly not a load balancer. IP-based routes on client CIDR blocks for network-tuned delivery. Policy Routes by Use case --- --- --- Simple Round-robin Single resource Weighted Weight 0–255 A/B testing, canary Latency Lowest measured latency Multi-Region apps Geolocation User location Localize, restrict Geoproximity Resource location + bias Shift load between regions Failover Health check Active-pass"},{"anchor":"3-route-53-routing-policies-directing-dns-answers","text":"ive DR Multivalue answer Up to 8 healthy, random DNS distribution not an ELB IP-based Client CIDR Network-tuned delivery The highest-value distinction, because the names invite confusion: Geolocation looks at the user; Geoproximity looks at the resource. Say it once more when revising — it is the single most mixed-up pair in this module. Mnemonic: S · W · L · G · P · F · M → Simple · Weighted · Latency · Geolocation · Geoproximity · Failover · Multivalue. --- 4. CloudFront — Caching the World Closer CloudFront is AWS's content delivery network: a global cache layer that puts bytes near users so origins serve as little as possible. Follow one request and the whole service makes sense. A user asks for content; the nearest edge location checks its cache. On a hit , it answers in milliseconds and the origin never hears about it. On a miss , the request climbs to Origin Shield and the Regional Edge Cache — intermediate tiers whose entire job is absorbing misses — and only then reaches the origin : an S3 bucket, EC2 fleet, load balancer, or custom URL. Two features extend the model. Signed URLs grant temporary access to otherwise-private content through the CDN — paywalled video, expirin"},{"anchor":"4-cloudfront-caching-the-world-closer","text":"g downloads. Lambda@Edge runs small functions at edge locations for personalization: rewrites, header manipulation, A/B variants computed where the user is, not where the origin lives. Exam favorite, stated plainly: \"low-latency content delivery\" or \"cache close to users\" always resolves to CloudFront plus edge locations . --- 5. Hybrid Connectivity — Reaching Back On-Premises Almost no enterprise is cloud-only on day one, so AWS offers two primitives for the on-premises link with opposite trade-offs. Site-to-Site VPN is an IPsec tunnel over the public internet: encrypted by construction, cheap, and up in minutes — the default when security matters more than raw throughput. Direct Connect is dedicated fiber 1–100 Gbps from your facility into AWS: consistent latency, predictable bandwidth, private path — the default when throughput and stability dominate. Combine them VPN over DX when both matter. Transit Gateway then multiplexes: one hub connecting many VPCs plus VPN and Direct Connect attachments, instead of a tangle of individual links. Decision: need encryption → VPN. Need dedicated throughput → Direct Connect. Many networks → Transit Gateway hub. Three traps cluster here. Direc"},{"anchor":"5-hybrid-connectivity-reaching-back-on-premises","text":"t Connect alone is private but not encrypted — encryption requires the VPN overlay, and options claiming otherwise are distractors. VPC peering is never transitive. NAT Gateways serve outbound only — no inbound path from the internet exists through NAT, which is precisely the point of private subnets. --- 6. Knowledge Check 1. Q: Which AWS networking service creates a virtual network, and why not the other options? A: Amazon VPC. AWS Config is resource inventory, Route 53 is DNS, Direct Connect is a dedicated link — none creates networks. 2. Q: An inbound security-group rule allows port 443. Is the return traffic allowed? What about under a NACL? A: Yes under the security group stateful . Under a NACL, only with an explicit outbound rule covering the ephemeral return path stateless . 3. Q: Split traffic 50/50 between two endpoints for an A/B test. Which Route 53 policy and configuration? A: Weighted routing with equal weights. 4. Q: Restrict video content to users in one country. Which policy, and why not Geoproximity? A: Geolocation — it routes on user location. Geoproximity routes on resource location. 5. Q: A private subnet needs OS updates but must accept no inbound internet tr"},{"anchor":"6-knowledge-check","text":"affic. Which component, placed where? A: A NAT Gateway in a public subnet, with the private route table pointing outbound through it. --- 7. Key Takeaways - VPC spans a Region; subnets pin to one AZ; instances attach via subnets. IGW = public exit, NAT = outbound-only for private subnets. - Security Groups stateful, allow-only, ENI vs NACLs stateless, allow+deny, subnet, numbered rules . - Route 53 policies answer different questions; Geolocation = user side, Geoproximity = resource side; multivalue DNS is not a load balancer. - CloudFront + edge locations = low latency; Shield and Regional Edge Caches absorb misses before the origin. - VPN = encrypted over internet; Direct Connect = dedicated fiber, not encrypted alone; Transit Gateway = the hub; peering never transits. --- 8. Glossary - VPC — Virtual Private Cloud; Regional isolated network. - Subnet — AZ-scoped IP range inside a VPC. - Route Table — per-subnet traffic rules. - IGW — Internet Gateway public exit . - NAT Gateway — outbound-only internet for private subnets. - Security Group — ENI firewall stateful, allow-only . - NACL — subnet firewall stateless, allow/deny, numbered . - VPC Peering — 1-to-1 inter-VPC link no tran"},{"anchor":"7-key-takeaways","text":"sit . - Transit Gateway — hub for VPC + on-prem networks. - VPC Endpoint — private AWS-service link, no internet. - Route 53 — authoritative DNS + health checks. - CloudFront — global CDN over edge locations. - Origin / Origin Shield — content source / extra cache tier. - Edge Location — CDN cache endpoint. - Signed URL — temporary private-content access. - Lambda@Edge — edge-executed personalization code. - Direct Connect — dedicated fiber to AWS. - Site-to-Site VPN — encrypted IPsec tunnel over internet. --- 9. Additional Resources - Amazon VPC User Guide https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html - Network ACLs https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-network-acls.html - Security Groups https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/VPC_SecurityGroups.html - VPC Peering https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-peering.html - Transit Gateway https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/tgw/what-is-transit-gateway.html - Route 53 Routing Policy https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy.html - Amazon CloudFront https://aws.amazon.com/cloudfront/ - Direct Connect https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnec"},{"anchor":"8-glossary","text":"t/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html - Site-to-Site VPN https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/s2vpn/Welcome.html - VPC Connectivity Options whitepaper https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-vpc-connectivity-options/introduction.html - One to Many: Evolving VPC Design https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/one-to-many-evolving-vpc-design/"}]},"aws-06-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-6-compute":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-6-compute","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module6-Compute-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS-Module6-Compute-Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 6: Compute Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS-Module6-Compute-Notes.md --- 1. The Compute Spectrum — Control vs Management Every compute service answers the same question: how much of the stack do you want to operate? EC2 IaaS virtual machines gives maximum control — OS, runtime, patching, networking are yours — at maximum operational cost. Containers ECS, EKS, Fargate keep your application and its image while an orchestrator handles placement and scaling. Lambda FaaS keeps only function code; AWS owns everything including scaling to zero. Beanstalk PaaS keeps application code plus version while AWS provisions the standard web stack underneath. Lightsail bundles simple fixed-price virtual servers for small sites and dev/test. The economic logic: more managed means higher price per unit but lower total cost of ownership, because operations labor disappears. The exam frames choices as workload shapes — match "},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"the shape to the service, not the brand. Service Model You manage Use it for --- --- --- --- EC2 IaaS — VMs OS, runtime, app, patching, networking Max control, custom stacks, lift-and-shift ECS / EKS Containers orchestrator App + container image Microservices, portable workloads Fargate Serverless containers App + container image No cluster/node management Lambda FaaS — functions Function code Event-driven, spiky, short-lived Beanstalk PaaS — web apps App code + version Fast deploy without infra design Lightsail VPS bundles App Simple sites, dev/test, fixed price --- 2. Amazon EC2 Core — The Instance and Its Entourage An EC2 instance never travels alone; launching one assembles a small constellation. The AMI Amazon Machine Image is the template — OS plus applications baked in. The instance type e.g. m6i.large is the hardware profile: family letter, generation, size. The instance is the running VM itself. Around it gather the companions. An EBS volume provides persistent block storage. A security group firewalls the elastic network interface. A key pair gates SSH/RDP login public key with AWS, private key guarded by you . An Elastic IP gives a static public IPv4. User data is a boot"},{"anchor":"1-the-compute-spectrum-control-vs-management","text":" script executed at first launch — the hook for self-configuring fleets. An IAM role never hardcoded access keys grants the instance permission to call AWS APIs. Tags carry the key/value metadata that billing and automation slice by. The naming convention repays attention: in m6i.large , m is the family, 6i the generation, large the size — and each size step roughly doubles vCPUs and RAM. Questions giving two type names are usually testing whether you can read this encoding. --- 3. Instance Type Families — Letters That Mean Workloads The family letter encodes the optimization, and the optimization encodes the workload. T instances are burstable: they accumulate CPU credits at idle and spend them in bursts — right for dev/test and small services with peaky days. M is general purpose, the default when nothing special applies. C is compute-optimized: batch processing, gaming servers, ML inference — CPU-bound work. R and X are memory-optimized: in-memory caches like Redis and Memcached, Spark — RAM-bound work. P and G carry GPUs: ML training, graphics, video transcoding. I and D optimize storage I/O: NoSQL databases, Hadoop, log processing. H serves tightly coupled high-performance com"},{"anchor":"2-amazon-ec2-core-the-instance-and-its-entourage","text":"puting; Z serves high-frequency, single-threaded performance like trading engines. Family Optimized for Typical use --- --- --- M · T General · Burstable CPU credits Web servers, dev/test, microservices C Compute-optimized Batch, gaming, ML inference R · X Memory-optimized In-memory caches, Spark P · G GPU / accelerated ML training, graphics, transcoding I · D Storage I/O NoSQL, Hadoop, log processing H High-performance compute HPC, parallel jobs Z High frequency Trading, single-threaded perf Mnemonic: T · M · C · R · P · I → Burstable · General · Compute · Memory · GPU · Storage I/O. --- 4. EC2 Pricing Models — Matching Money to Shape Revisit Module 2's economics with EC2 specifics, because this is where the exam lives. On-Demand no commitment, pay by the second is for the unknown — spiky, unpredictable, short-lived. Reserved Instances one- or three-year promise of steady usage, up to ~72% off are for the known — databases, baseline fleets. Savings Plans dollars-per-hour promise, similar discount keep the savings when you change families or sizes. Spot Instances spare capacity, up to ~90% off, reclaimable on two minutes' notice are for the interruptible — fault-tolerant batch, CI "},{"anchor":"3-instance-type-families-letters-that-mean-workloads","text":"pipelines that checkpoint. Dedicated Hosts whole physical servers are for the constrained — BYOL deals, per-socket licensing, single-tenancy compliance. Capacity Reservations AZ hold at On-Demand price, zero discount are for the unmissable — guaranteed existence for critical events. Option Commitment Discount Best for --- --- --- --- On-Demand None Baseline Unknown / spiky Reserved RI 1–3 yr Up to ~72% Steady-state, predictable Savings Plans 1–3 yr $/hour Up to ~72% Flexible across family Spot None interruptible Up to ~90% Fault-tolerant batch / CI Dedicated Hosts Per-host Separate pricing BYOL, compliance Capacity Reservations Per-AZ hold None Guarantee capacity The reasoning pattern is a decision tree: can it be interrupted → Spot. Predictable around the clock → Reserved/Savings. Unknown → On-Demand. Must provably exist → Capacity Reservation. And the two traps: Spot is never for interruption-intolerant production regardless of discount, and an RI is a billing discount, not a capacity lock — guaranteeing hardware is the Capacity Reservation's separate job. Mnemonic: O · R · S · S · D · C → On-Demand · Reserved · Savings · Spot · Dedicated · Capacity Reservation. --- 5. Containers"},{"anchor":"4-ec2-pricing-models-matching-money-to-shape","text":" — ECS, EKS, Fargate, ECR Containers package application plus dependencies into a portable image; the orchestrator decides where images run and how they scale. AWS offers two orchestrators and one way to avoid operating either. ECS is the AWS-native Docker orchestrator — deepest AWS integration, simplest when no Kubernetes investment exists. EKS is managed upstream Kubernetes — the control plane AWS operates, the standard APIs your team already knows, the right call when the organization has standardized on k8s. Fargate is the serverless container engine working with both : no EC2 nodes to manage, pay per task or pod. ECR Elastic Container Registry is the private image repository feeding all three. Two decisions, in order: already standardizing on Kubernetes → EKS, otherwise ECS. Then: willing to manage EC2 worker nodes → EC2 launch type; prefer none → Fargate. The \"cluster of managed servers\" phrasing in the objectives means the control plane plus worker nodes of ECS/EKS — and Fargate's value proposition is deleting the workers from your responsibilities. --- 6. AWS Lambda — Events In, Results Out Lambda executes function code without servers existing in your mental model: an even"},{"anchor":"5-containers-ecs-eks-fargate-ecr","text":"t arrives API Gateway request, S3 upload, SQS message, EventBridge schedule , the function runs, AWS scales concurrency automatically, and you pay per request plus GB-seconds of execution. The limits define the use cases, so learn them as boundaries. Fifteen minutes maximum per invocation — anything longer belongs on EC2, containers, or Batch. Memory 128 MB to 10 GB CPU scales with memory . Packaging as ZIP or container image up to 10 GB. Concurrency defaulting to 1,000 raiseable . Runtimes spanning Node, Python, Java, Go, .NET, Ruby, plus custom. Within those walls: event-driven glue, spiky unpredictable traffic, schedulers, chatbots. Outside them: always-on steady traffic EC2 is cheaper per unit at sustained load , stateful apps needing sticky sessions, latency-critical real-time paths. --- 7. Elastic Beanstalk — PaaS for Web Apps Beanstalk answers \"I have a web app, I don't want to design infrastructure.\" Upload code; Beanstalk provisions the standard stack — EC2, load balancer, Auto Scaling group, RDS, security groups, monitoring — and you control the application code plus version, instance type, and scaling rules. Platforms cover Java, .NET, Node, Python, Ruby, Go, PHP, and Do"},{"anchor":"6-aws-lambda-events-in-results-out","text":"cker. And the pricing fact the exam checks: Beanstalk itself is free — you pay only for the underlying AWS resources it creates. Contrast with CloudFormation : Beanstalk deploys opinionated web stacks from code; CloudFormation provisions arbitrary AWS infrastructure from JSON/YAML templates infrastructure as code . Fast standard app → Beanstalk. Anything custom or codified → CloudFormation. --- 8. EC2 vs RDS — The Managed-Service Trade-off, Concretely The activity comparing databases on EC2 versus RDS teaches the general managed-service lesson through one example. Self-managed on EC2 means you patch the OS and the database engine, script your own backups, build your own high availability and read replicas — total control, total burden. RDS means AWS patches OS and engine on a configurable window, automates backups with point-in-time recovery, and offers Multi-AZ failover and read replicas essentially as options — less control engine-level only , vastly less undifferentiated work. Every \"EC2 vs managed\" question in the course replays this table with different service names. Aspect Self-managed on EC2 Amazon RDS --- --- --- OS patching You AWS DB patching You AWS window Backups You s"},{"anchor":"7-elastic-beanstalk-paas-for-web-apps","text":"cript it Automated + point-in-time HA You build it Multi-AZ one click Read replicas You build it One click Control Total Engine-level only --- 9. Knowledge Check 1. Q: Fault-tolerant nightly batch, minimize cost. Which EC2 option and why? A: Spot — the workload's restart-safety absorbs reclaim risk, capturing up to ~90% off. 2. Q: Steady-state database fleet for three years. Which option? A: Reserved Instances or Savings Plans — predictable baseline earns the commitment discount ~72% . 3. Q: Team standardized on Kubernetes wants an AWS-managed control plane. A: EKS. No k8s investment → ECS; no nodes at all → Fargate. 4. Q: S3 uploads trigger two-minute image processing, traffic spiky. Which service and why does it fit? A: Lambda — event-driven, well under the 15-minute ceiling, spiky traffic matches pay-per-execution. 5. Q: Deploy a standard web app fast without designing infrastructure. What does the deployment service itself cost? A: Beanstalk is free; you pay only for the underlying EC2/ELB/RDS it provisions. --- 10. Key Takeaways - Compute spectrum trades control for management: EC2 → containers → Lambda/Beanstalk → Lightsail. Match workload shape, not brand. - EC2 instances tr"},{"anchor":"8-ec2-vs-rds-the-managed-service-trade-off-concretely","text":"avel with AMI, type family.generation.size , EBS, SG, key pair, EIP, user data, IAM role, tags. - Family letters encode workloads: T/M/C/R/P-G/I-D +H, +Z . - Spot interruptible · Reserved/Savings steady · On-Demand unknown · Capacity Reservation guarantee . RI discounts; it does not reserve hardware. - ECS native · EKS managed k8s · Fargate no nodes · ECR registry . - Lambda: events in, 15-minute ceiling, pay per ms. Beanstalk: free PaaS over standard AWS infrastructure. --- 11. Glossary - AMI — Amazon Machine Image instance template . - ENI — Elastic Network Interface virtual NIC . - EBS — persistent block storage for EC2. - EIP — Elastic IP static public IPv4 . - User data — first-launch boot script. - Placement group — cluster / spread / partition proximity control. - ASG — Auto Scaling Group metric-driven instance count . - ELB / ALB — Elastic / Application Load Balancer. - ECS / EKS / Fargate / ECR — container orchestrators, serverless container engine, image registry. - Lambda — function-as-a-service, serverless, pay-per-ms. - Beanstalk — PaaS for standard web apps free service . - CloudFormation — infrastructure as code JSON/YAML . - Lightsail — simple fixed-price VPS. - Spo"},{"anchor":"9-knowledge-check","text":"t / RI / Savings Plan — interruptible / commitment / $/hour EC2 pricing. - Capacity Reservation / Dedicated Host — guaranteed AZ capacity / single-tenant physical server. --- 12. Additional Resources - Amazon EC2 https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/ - EC2 Instance Types https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance-types/ - EC2 Pricing https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/pricing/ - EC2 User Guide https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/concepts.html - Amazon ECS https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/ - Amazon EKS https://aws.amazon.com/eks/ - AWS Fargate https://aws.amazon.com/fargate/ - AWS Lambda https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/ - Elastic Beanstalk https://aws.amazon.com/elasticbeanstalk/ - Lightsail https://aws.amazon.com/lightsail/ - CloudFormation https://aws.amazon.com/cloudformation/"}]},"aws-07-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-7-storage":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-7-storage","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\study-notes\\Module7-Storage-StudyNotes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations companion: modules md files/AWS-Module7-Storage-Notes.md /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 7: Storage Study Notes Companion to: modules md files/AWS-Module7-Storage-Notes.md --- 1. Storage at a Glance — Four Services, One Decision Cloud storage beats on-premises on reliability, scalability, and security — but only if you pick the right service, because each answers a different access pattern. Big-data analytics, warehouses, IoT ingestion, databases, and backup/archive pipelines all sit on one of four foundations. Learn the decision first, the details second: block EBS for one server's disks, object S3 for internet-scale data, file EFS for shared filesystems, archive Glacier for cold retention. EBS S3 EFS Glacier --- --- --- --- --- Type Block Object File NFS Object archive Attached via 1 EC2 in 1 AZ HTTP / SDK Many EC2, multi-AZ HTTP via S3 Best for Boot volumes, DBs Backups, static assets, data lakes Shared app data, content repos Cold archives, compliance Latency Sub-ms ms ms min–hr Pricing Per GB provisio"},{"anchor":"study-notes","text":"ned Per GB + requests Per GB stored Per GB + retrieval Cross-AZ No snapshot to copy Yes replication Yes native Yes CRR Mnemonic: B · O · F · A → Block EBS · Object S3 · File EFS · Archive Glacier . --- 2. Amazon EBS — Disks for EC2 Elastic Block Store is block-level storage: raw volumes that behave like physical disks, attached to one EC2 instance in one Availability Zone, persisting independently of the instance's life. Four volume types cover the performance spectrum. gp3/gp2 general-purpose SSD are the default for boot volumes and most workloads — gp3 additionally separates IOPS from throughput provisioning. io2/io1 provisioned-IOPS SSD serve demanding databases SAP, Oracle, large MongoDB where IOPS must be guaranteed, not hoped for. st1 throughput-optimized HDD serves sequential big-data and log processing like Kafka. sc1 cold HDD is the cheapest per gigabyte for infrequently touched data. Type Use Notes --- --- --- gp3 / gp2 General-purpose SSD Boot + most workloads; gp3 splits IOPS/throughput io2 / io1 Provisioned IOPS SSD High-performance DBs st1 Throughput HDD Big data, logs, Kafka sc1 Cold HDD Infrequent access, lowest cost Snapshots are the second half of EBS: point-in-ti"},{"anchor":"1-storage-at-a-glance-four-services-one-decision","text":"me copies stored in S3, used to copy volumes across AZs and Regions and to restore after deletion. Because EBS is AZ-scoped, the snapshot-then-recreate dance is the answer to every \"move this volume elsewhere\" question. The module's lab walks exactly this loop: create a volume, attach it to EC2, snapshot it, create a new volume from the snapshot. --- 3. Amazon S3 — Object Storage at Internet Scale S3 stores objects — each a bundle of data plus metadata plus a key — inside buckets globally unique names, Region-scoped homes , from zero bytes to five terabytes per object, accessed over HTTP/S via console, CLI, SDK, or REST. Around that simple model orbit the features that make S3 the center of AWS data architectures: eleven nines of durability 99.999999999% , versioning keeping multiple variants alive, lifecycle rules auto-transitioning objects between storage classes, replication CRR cross-region, SRR same-region copying buckets, static website hosting , and event notifications firing Lambda, SQS, or SNS on every put. Concept Detail --- --- Bucket Container; name globally unique; data in one Region Object Data + metadata + key; 0 B – 5 TB Key Unique identifier within a bucket Version"},{"anchor":"2-amazon-ebs-disks-for-ec2","text":"ing Multiple variants retained Lifecycle rule Automatic class transitions Replication CRR / SRR to another bucket Storage classes — pay for the access pattern you have Seven classes, one principle: hotter data costs more per gigabyte, colder data costs more per retrieval. Standard serves frequently accessed data with millisecond retrieval and no minimum. Intelligent-Tiering serves unknown or shifting patterns, moving objects between tiers automatically. Standard-IA serves infrequent data that still needs millisecond retrieval 30-day minimum . One Zone-IA drops a replica for non-critical infrequent data in a single AZ. Glacier Instant Retrieval archives with millisecond access 90-day minimum . Glacier Flexible Retrieval archives with minutes-to-hours retrieval. Glacier Deep Archive keeps compliance data for 7–10 years at the lowest price with 12+ hour retrieval 180-day minimum . Class For Min storage Retrieval --- --- --- --- S3 Standard Frequently accessed None ms Intelligent-Tiering Unknown / changing None ms Standard-IA Infrequent, ms needed 30 days ms One Zone-IA Infrequent, single AZ 30 days ms Glacier Instant Retrieval Archive, ms needed 90 days ms Glacier Flexible Retrieval A"},{"anchor":"3-amazon-s3-object-storage-at-internet-scale","text":"rchive, slow OK 90 days min–hr Glacier Deep Archive 7–10 yr compliance 180 days 12+ hr Decision chain: accessed daily → Standard. Pattern unknown → Intelligent- Tiering. Rarely touched but millisecond-capable → Standard-IA or Glacier Instant. True archive → Flexible or Deep Archive by retrieval deadline. Three traps guard this section. S3 is object storage, not a filesystem — no POSIX API, no in-place edits new upload = new version ; shared mounted filesystems mean EFS. Glacier is an S3 storage class , not a separate service — you still use S3 APIs. And bucket names are global while bucket data is Regional. --- 4. Amazon EFS — One Filesystem, Thousands of Clients Elastic File System is shared NFSv4 storage for Linux: thousands of EC2 instances across many Availability Zones mounting the same filesystem concurrently. Capacity grows automatically into petabytes with pay-per-use billing, throughput scales with stored data, and lifecycle policies slide cold files into Infrequent Access. Entry points are mount targets , one per VPC subnet. Use it for shared application data, content repositories, and lift-and-shift Linux file servers — and remember the platform boundary: EFS is NFS, the"},{"anchor":"storage-classes-pay-for-the-access-pattern-you-have","text":"refore Linux; Windows file shares on AWS mean FSx for Windows File Server FSx also covers Lustre for HPC . --- 5. Amazon S3 Glacier — Cold Storage With Retrieval Tiers Because Glacier lives inside S3 as storage classes, \"retrieval\" is really a scheduling choice with three speeds. Expedited 1–5 minutes recovers urgent archives. Standard 3–5 hours is the default bulk path. Bulk 5–12 hours is cheapest for large batches. Deep Archive sits beneath all three as the lowest-cost class for decade-scale regulatory and compliance retention — medical records being the canonical example. Mnemonic: E · S · B → Expedited minutes · Standard hours · Bulk longest, cheapest . --- 6. Hybrid & Transfer — Getting Data In and Out Not all data starts in AWS. Storage Gateway bridges on-premises environments to S3 in file, volume, and tape modes — the hybrid answer. Snowball and Snowmobile move petabytes offline: ship the ruggedized disk array, or the truck, when networks cannot. DataSync migrates and synchronizes online between on-premises and AWS storage. Transfer Family speaks SFTP/FTPS/FTP directly into S3 and EFS for legacy clients. --- 7. Knowledge Check 1. Q: Hundreds of EC2 instances across many AZs"},{"anchor":"4-amazon-efs-one-filesystem-thousands-of-clients","text":" need one shared filesystem. Which service, and why not the alternatives? A: EFS — the only option mounting as shared POSIX storage across many instances multi-AZ. EBS is single-instance/single-AZ; S3 is object storage with no filesystem API; Glacier is cold archive. 2. Q: Backups touched twice a year, millisecond retrieval unneeded, keep ten years, minimize cost. Which class? A: Glacier Deep Archive — lowest storage price, 12+ hour retrieval fits, 180-day minimum satisfied. 3. Q: Move an EBS volume to another AZ. Exact mechanism? A: Snapshot the volume stored in S3 , then create a new volume from that snapshot in the target AZ. 4. Q: Access pattern unknown, want automatic cost optimization without managing transitions. Which class? A: S3 Intelligent-Tiering — monitors and moves objects between tiers. 5. Q: Windows file shares on AWS — EFS? A: No: EFS is NFS/Linux-only. Use FSx for Windows File Server. --- 8. Key Takeaways - EBS = block, single AZ and single EC2; snapshots in S3 are the copy and restore mechanism; gp3 default, io2 for demanding databases. - S3 = object, eleven nines, seven classes keyed to access pattern; objects immutable, names global, data Regional. - EFS = shar"},{"anchor":"5-amazon-s3-glacier-cold-storage-with-retrieval-tiers","text":"ed NFS, multi-AZ concurrent mounts, Linux only FSx for Windows . - Glacier = S3 archive classes with Expedited/Standard/Bulk retrieval; Deep Archive for decade compliance. - Hybrid: Storage Gateway online bridge , Snowball/Snowmobile offline bulk , DataSync online sync , Transfer Family legacy protocols . - Pick by access pattern plus structure — price follows the pattern. --- 9. Glossary - EBS — Elastic Block Store EC2 volumes . - Snapshot — point-in-time EBS backup stored in S3. - S3 — Simple Storage Service object storage . - Bucket / Object / Key — container / item / identifier. - Versioning — retained object variants. - Lifecycle rule — automatic storage-class transitions. - Replication CRR/SRR — cross- / same-region bucket copy. - Intelligent-Tiering — automatic access-tier moves. - EFS / Mount target — shared NFS / per-subnet entry point. - NFS — Network File System Linux sharing protocol . - FSx — managed Windows / Lustre file systems. - Glacier — cold S3 archive classes. - Expedited / Standard / Bulk — Glacier retrieval speeds. - Deep Archive — lowest-cost decade retention class. - Storage Gateway — hybrid on-prem↔S3 bridge. - Snowball / Snowmobile — offline petabyte trans"},{"anchor":"6-hybrid-amp-transfer-getting-data-in-and-out","text":"port. - DataSync / Transfer Family — online sync / legacy file protocols. --- 10. Additional Resources - Amazon EBS https://aws.amazon.com/ebs/ - Amazon S3 https://aws.amazon.com/s3/ - S3 Storage Classes https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/ - Amazon EFS https://aws.amazon.com/efs/ - Amazon FSx https://aws.amazon.com/fsx/ - S3 Glacier https://aws.amazon.com/glacier/ - Storage Gateway https://aws.amazon.com/storagegateway/ - Snowball https://aws.amazon.com/snowball/"}]},"aws-08-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-8-databases":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-8-databases","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\modules md files\\AWS_Module8_Databases_Notes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations Note: source covers a middle section of the module RDS HA, replicas, pricing, Lab 5 . Earlier/later module content not in source — marked partial. /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 8: Databases © Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved. Coverage note: This chapter covers a middle section of the module — Amazon RDS high availability, read replicas, use cases, billing factors, and the start of Lab 5. Earlier and later module content isn't included in the source. --- High Availability with Multi-AZ Deployment One of the most powerful Amazon RDS features is configuring a database instance for high availability with a Multi-AZ deployment . How it works: - Once Multi-AZ is configured, Amazon RDS automatically creates a standby copy of the database instance in a different Availability Zone, within the same VPC. - After seeding the standby copy, transactions are synchronously replicated to it. Architecture: Public subnet app tier, EC2 + two private subnets across two Availability Zones"},{"anchor":"high-availability-with-multi-az-deployment","text":" — one holding the primary RDS instance, the other holding the standby RDS instance, connected via synchronous replication. Benefits: - Enhances availability during planned system maintenance - Protects against database instance failure - Protects against Availability Zone disruption --- Amazon RDS Read Replicas Features: - Offers asynchronous replication - Can be promoted to primary if needed requires manual action, due to the async nature of replication Functionality: - Best for read-heavy database workloads - Offloads read queries from the primary instance Supported engines: MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL, and Amazon Aurora. How it works: Updates to the source primary database instance are asynchronously copied to the read replica. Applications can route read queries to the replica, reducing load on the primary and letting you scale out read capacity beyond a single instance's limits. Cross-Region read replicas: Read replicas can be created in a different Region than the primary — useful for disaster recovery, or for reducing latency by serving reads from a replica closer to the end user. --- Amazon RDS Use Cases Use case Why RDS fits --- --- Web and mobile applications High through"},{"anchor":"amazon-rds-read-replicas","text":"put, massive storage scalability, high availability Ecommerce applications Low-cost database, data security, fully managed solution Mobile and online games Rapidly grow capacity, automatic scaling, database monitoring - Web/mobile apps: RDS has no licensing constraints, which fits the variable usage patterns typical of these applications. - Ecommerce: RDS offers a flexible, secure, low-cost database solution for online sales and retail, for businesses of any size. - Games: RDS manages the database infrastructure, so developers don't need to worry about provisioning, scaling, or monitoring database servers — important given the high throughput/availability needs of gaming platforms. --- Amazon RDS Pricing Factors Clock-hour billing & database characteristics - Clock-hour billing: resources incur charges while running — from launch until the instance is terminated. - Database characteristics affecting cost: engine , size , and memory class i.e., the physical capacity of the database . DB purchase type & multiple DB instances - On-Demand Instances: pay for compute capacity by the hour, no minimum commitment. - Reserved Instances: low, one-time upfront payment to reserve an instance fo"},{"anchor":"amazon-rds-use-cases","text":"r a 1-year or 3-year term — results in a significant discount on the hourly usage rate. - Number of DB instances: you can provision multiple DB instances to handle peak loads. Storage - Provisioned storage: - No additional charge for backup storage up to 100% of provisioned storage on an active database instance. - Once the instance is terminated, backup storage is billed per GB/month. - Additional storage: backup storage beyond the provisioned amount is billed per GB/month. Deployment type & data transfer - Requests: the number of input/output requests made to the database is a cost factor. - Deployment type: storage and I/O charges vary depending on whether you deploy to: - A single Availability Zone like a standalone data center , or - Multiple Availability Zones like a secondary data center, for enhanced availability/durability - Data transfer: - Inbound data transfer: free - Outbound data transfer: tiered charges --- Recorded Demo: Amazon RDS Console Watch the \"Console Demonstration – RDS\" video in the Module 8 section of the course to see how to: - Configure an Amazon RDS installation running the MySQL database engine - Connect to the database using a MySQL client If you can'"},{"anchor":"amazon-rds-pricing-factors","text":"t locate the video, reach out to your educator. --- Lab 5: Build Your DB Server and Interact with Your DB Using an App Scenario This lab shows how to use an AWS managed database instance to meet a relational database need. With Amazon RDS you can: set up, operate, and scale a relational database in the cloud with cost-efficient, resizable capacity — while AWS manages the time-consuming administration tasks, freeing you to focus on your application and business. Supported engines 6 : Amazon Aurora, Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB. Multi-AZ in this lab: Provisioning a Multi-AZ DB instance automatically creates a primary DB instance and synchronously replicates data to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone — well suited to production database workloads. Lab architecture: - VPC: 10.0.0.0/16 - Public subnet 1: 10.0.0.0/24 Availability Zone A - Public subnet 2: 10.0.2.0/24 Availability Zone B - Private subnet 1: 10.0.1.0/24 Availability Zone A - Private subnet 2: 10.0.3.0/24 Availability Zone B - Internet gateway, NAT gateway, security group, and a web server tier Lab Objectives After completing this lab, you should be able to: 1. Launch an Amazon R"},{"anchor":"clock-hour-billing-amp-database-characteristics","text":"DS DB instance with high availability 2. Configure the DB instance to permit connections from your web server 3. Open a web application and interact with your database --- AWS Training and Certification — Module 8: Databases"}]},"aws-09-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-9-cloud-architecture":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-9-cloud-architecture","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\modules md files\\AWS_Module9_CloudArchitecture_Notes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations Note: source covers a middle section Reliability tail, Performance Efficiency, activity, Cost Optimization opening . Other pillars not in source — marked partial. /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 9: Cloud Architecture © Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved. Coverage note: This covers a middle section of the module — the second half of the Reliability pillar, the full Performance Efficiency pillar, an activity breakout, and the start of the Cost Optimization pillar AWS Well-Architected Framework . Other pillars/sections aren't included in the source. --- Reliability Pillar Focus: Recover from failure and mitigate disruption. Design Principles 1. Automatically recover from failure — Monitor systems for key performance indicators and configure automated recovery when a threshold is breached. Enables automatic notification, failure-tracking, and recovery processes that work around or repair the failure. 2. Test recovery procedures — Test how systems fail and validate re"},{"anchor":"reliability-pillar","text":"covery procedures. Use automation to simulate failures or recreate past failure scenarios, exposing failure pathways you can fix before a real incident. 3. Scale horizontally to increase aggregate workload availability — Replace one large resource with multiple smaller resources, distributing requests across them to reduce the impact of any single point of failure. 4. Stop guessing capacity — Monitor demand and usage, and automate adding/removing resources to maintain the optimal level to meet demand. 5. Manage change in automation — Use automation to make and manage infrastructure changes. Reliability Questions by Best Practice Area Foundations - How do you manage service quotas and constraints? - How do you plan your network topology? Workload architecture - How do you design your workload service architecture? - How do you design interactions in a distributed system to prevent failure? - How do you design interactions in a distributed system to mitigate or withstand failures? Change management - How do you monitor workload resources? - How do you design your workload to adapt to changes in demand? - How do you implement change? Failure management - How do you back up data? - How"},{"anchor":"design-principles","text":" do you use fault isolation to protect your workload? - How do you design your workload to withstand component failures? - How do you test reliability? - How do you plan for disaster recovery? To achieve reliability, a system needs a well-planned foundation with monitoring in place, mechanisms to handle changes in demand or requirements, and a design that detects failure and automatically heals itself. --- Performance Efficiency Pillar Focus: Use resources sparingly. The Performance Efficiency pillar is about using IT and computing resources efficiently to meet system requirements — and maintaining that efficiency as demand changes or technologies evolve. Key topics: - Selecting the right resource types and sizes based on workload requirements - Monitoring performance - Making informed decisions to maintain efficiency as business needs evolve Design Principles 1. Democratize advanced technologies — Consume technologies NoSQL databases, media transcoding, machine learning, etc. as a service rather than needing in-house expertise. Lets teams focus on product development instead of resource provisioning and management. 2. Go global in minutes — Deploy systems across multiple AWS Regio"},{"anchor":"reliability-questions-by-best-practice-area","text":"ns for lower latency and a better customer experience, at minimal cost. 3. Use serverless architectures — Removes the operational burden of running/maintaining servers, and can lower transactional costs since managed services operate at cloud scale. 4. Experiment more often — Run comparative testing of different instance types, storage options, or configurations. 5. Consider mechanical sympathy — Use the technology approach that best aligns with your goal — e.g., factor in data access patterns when choosing database or storage approaches. Performance Efficiency Questions by Best Practice Area Selection - How do you select the best performing architecture? - How do you select your compute solution? - How do you select your storage solution? - How do you select your database solution? - How do you configure your networking solution? Review - How do you evolve your workload to take advantage of new releases? Monitoring - How do you monitor your resources to ensure they are performing? Tradeoffs - How do you use tradeoffs to improve performance? Use data to design and build a high-performance architecture — gather data across the whole architecture, from high-level design down to resou"},{"anchor":"performance-efficiency-pillar","text":"rce type/configuration selection. Review choices periodically to take advantage of new AWS services, monitor to catch deviations from expected performance, and use tradeoffs compression, caching, relaxing consistency requirements to improve performance. --- Activity Breakout 1 Using the full AnyCompany architecture see background/architecture notes and optionally the AWS Well-Architected Framework whitepaper https://d1.awsstatic.com/whitepapers/architecture/AWS_Well-Architected_Framework.pdf : 1. Review three Performance Efficiency questions from the Well-Architected Framework: - PERF 1: How do you select the best performing architecture? - PERF 2: How do you select your compute solution? - PERF 4: How do you select your database solution? 2. For each question, identify AnyCompany's current state and its final target state . 3. Agree as a group on the top improvement AnyCompany should make. --- Cost Optimization Pillar Focus: Eliminate unneeded expense. Key topics: - Understanding and controlling where money is being spent - Selecting the most appropriate and right number of resource types - Analyzing spend over time - Scaling to meet business needs without overspending Design Prin"},{"anchor":"design-principles-2","text":"ciples 1. Implement Cloud Financial Management — Invest in cloud financial management and cost optimization to achieve financial success and accelerate value realization in the cloud. Build capability through knowledge-building, programs, resources, and processes. 2. Adopt a consumption model — Pay only for the computing resources you need; scale usage up or down based on actual business requirements rather than elaborate forecasting. 3. Measure overall efficiency — Measure business output against the cost of delivering it, to understand the gains from increasing output and reducing costs. 4. Stop spending money on undifferentiated heavy lifting — AWS handles racking, stacking, and powering servers, freeing you to focus on customers and business projects instead of IT infrastructure. 5. Analyze and attribute expenditure — The cloud makes it easier to accurately identify usage/costs and attribute them to individual workload owners — helping measure ROI and giving owners the chance to optimize and reduce their own costs. --- AWS Training and Certification — Module 9: Cloud Architecture"}]},"aws-10-10-modules-deep-dive-why-when-how-4-stage-evolution":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"10-modules-deep-dive-why-when-how-4-stage-evolution","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\10-modules-deep-dive.md 2026-09-08 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate→Advanced Note: Obsidian frontmatter tags/level/status/source converted to this provenance block. /} 10 — Modules Deep Dive — Why, When, How + 4-Stage Evolution One-line: a module = a reusable parameterized folder main.tf + variables.tf + outputs.tf ; you call it via module \"name\" { source = \"...\" } to stop copy-pasting resources. 1. Why Use Modules — The Real Reason Without modules every environment repeats the same 30 lines. Copy-paste → drift → a typo in prod that wasn't in dev. With modules you get 4 things: 1. DRY Don't Repeat Yourself : write the resource ONCE, call it 10 times with different inputs. 2. Consistency: every VPC/EC2/S3 built the same way — same tags, same defaults, same security baseline. 3. Encapsulation: caller only sees variables inputs and outputs exports — internals hidden, like a function. 4. Versioning & Testing: pin version = \"~ 5.1\" or Git ?ref=v1.2.0 ; test the module once, reuse everywhere. Analogy: resource = single LEGO brick, module = pre-built LEGO wall. You don't"},{"anchor":"1-why-use-modules-the-real-reason","text":" rebuild the wall each time. 2. When TO Use Modules vs When NOT To Use a module when... Don't use a module when... --- --- Same resource pattern repeated ≥2 times 3 EC2s, 3 envs, 10 teams One-off resource — a single S3 bucket that will never be reused Multi-env dev/staging/prod with identical shape, different sizes Simple learning lab / one-shot terraform apply — overhead benefit Team-shared standard VPC, EC2 baseline, S3+encryption Logic is trivial 1 resource, 5 lines — a module adds indirection Need version pinning & independent release cycle The abstraction hides more than it helps 2 variables for 1 resource Composition: VPC module + EC2 module + ALB module = full stack Early prototype — extract module AFTER second copy, not before Rule of Three Rule of Three: first copy → copy-paste, second copy → consider, third copy → extract module. ⚠️ Gotcha — premature abstraction: creating a module for a single aws_s3_bucket with 1 variable saves nothing but adds source = \"./modules/s3-bucket\" indirection. Cost value. 3. How to Create a Terraform Project Using Modules — Structure 3.1 Minimal reusable project layout 3.2 Module authoring rules 5 rules 1. No provider block inside reusable mo"},{"anchor":"2-when-to-use-modules-vs-when-not-to","text":"dules — inherit from caller or use providers = { aws = aws.eu } at call site . 2. No hardcoded values — every name, CIDR, type comes via variable . 3. All inputs validated: validation { condition = length var.bucket_name 3 } 4. All exports via output — caller never reaches into module.xxx.aws_s3_bucket.this internals. 5. Pin versions: module versions.tf declares required_providers ; caller pins version = \"~ 5.0\" registry or ?ref=v1.0.0 Git . 3.3 Consume patterns — source types --- 4. The Task — Same Job, 4 Evolutions Task: create 3 S3 buckets for app logs: myapp-logs-dev , myapp-logs-staging , myapp-logs-prod , each with Environment tag + force_destroy = true lab-safe . Region us-east-1 . We'll evolve one step at a time. All 4 stages live under source Terraform/level2/modules-evolution/ — each is a runnable terraform init/plan root. --- Stage 1 — WITHOUT variables, WITHOUT loops, WITHOUT modules raw copy-paste Path: level2/modules-evolution/stage1-no-vars-no-loop-no-module/ Terminal: Pain: change ManagedBy tag? Edit 3 places. Add a 4th env? Copy-paste a 4th block. --- Stage 2 — WITH variables, WITHOUT loops, WITHOUT modules Path: stage2-vars-no-loop-no-module/ — values extracted, b"},{"anchor":"3-how-to-create-a-terraform-project-using-modules-structure","text":"ut repetition remains. Terminal: Win: change prefix once → all buckets rename. Still pain: 3 blocks for 3 buckets — doesn't scale to 20. ⚠️ Gotcha: interpolating ${var.bucket_prefix}-dev in 3 places is better than hardcoding, but still violates DRY on resource structure. --- Stage 3 — WITH variables AND loops for_each , WITHOUT modules Path: stage3-vars-loop-no-module/ — one resource block creates N buckets. Win: add \"qa\" to var.environments → 4th bucket, zero new blocks. each.key = \"dev\" etc., stable addressing unlike count index . ⚠️ Gotcha — count vs for_each : use for_each for named envs rename-safe ; count renumbers on delete web 1 becomes web 0 → recreation . --- Stage 4 — WITH variables AND loops AND modules production Path: stage4-vars-loop-module/ + modules/s3-bucket/ Module — modules/s3-bucket/main.tf reusable, no provider, no hardcodes : Root — calls module with for_each : Wins: swap myapp-logs → payments-logs in one variable; add encryption/versioning inside the module once → all 3 buckets inherit it; publish module to Git/Registry and every team reuses it. ⚠️ Module gotchas: - No provider inside module — caller owns region/account. - for_each on a module creates module"},{"anchor":"3-1-minimal-reusable-project-layout","text":".logs \"dev\" addresses — outputs become maps, not lists. - Changing for_each key \"dev\" → \"development\" forces destroy+create key is identity . --- 5. Gotchas Checklist - Module without variables.tf validation → callers pass empty strings → cryptic AWS errors. - Hardcoded region inside module → breaks multi-region use. - Using count for env-named resources → rename causes recreation; prefer for_each . - Forgetting ?ref= on Git source → main moves → non-reproducible applies. - Outputting secrets without sensitive = true → leaks in terraform output and logs. 6. Exam / Interview Close Q: Why not always use modules? A: Abstraction cost; Rule of Three — extract after 2nd duplication. Q: count vs for_each ? A: count = index 0 , fragile on delete; for_each = key \"dev\" , stable. Q: How to version a module? A: Registry version = \"~ 1.2\" or Git source = \"github.com/org/repo//modules/x?ref=v1.2.0\" . Next: run terraform plan in each stage folder to see the delta, then extract your own VPC/EC2 module. --- Appendix — Raw Capture Log - 2026-09-08 User brief: why use modules, how to create project using modules, when to use / when not, + 4-stage task evolution — captured and built."}]},"aws-11-aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-10-automatic-scaling-an":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-academy-cloud-foundations-module-10-automatic-scaling-and-monitoring","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\AWS Master Folder\\aws\\modules md files\\AWS_Module10_AutoScalingMonitoring_Notes.md Course metadata: course=aws AWS Academy Cloud Foundations Note: source covers Section 2 CloudWatch in full plus the opening of Section 3 Auto Scaling why . Other sections not in source — marked partial. /} AWS Academy Cloud Foundations — Module 10: Automatic Scaling and Monitoring © Amazon Web Services, Inc. or its affiliates. All rights reserved. Coverage note: This covers Section 2 Amazon CloudWatch in full, plus the opening of Section 3 Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling — why scaling matters . Other sections aren't included in the source. --- Section 2: Amazon CloudWatch Monitoring AWS Resources To use AWS efficiently, you need insight into your resources — for example: - When should you launch more Amazon EC2 instances? - Is your application's performance or availability affected by insufficient capacity? - How much of your infrastructure is actually being used? Amazon CloudWatch is how you capture this information. What CloudWatch Does CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service built for DevOps engineers, developers, site reliability engineers SREs "},{"anchor":"section-2-amazon-cloudwatch","text":", and IT managers. It monitors your AWS resources — and the applications running on them — in real time . Monitors: - AWS resources - Applications that run on AWS Collects and tracks: - Standard metrics - Custom metrics Alarms: - Send notifications to an Amazon SNS topic - Perform Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling or Amazon EC2 actions Events: - Define rules that match changes in your AWS environment and route those events to one or more target functions/streams for processing Metrics and Alarms A metric is a variable you can measure for a resource or application e.g. CPU utilization, ELB request latency, DynamoDB table throughput, SQS queue length, even your AWS bill charges . You can create an alarm on any CloudWatch metric — standard or custom — to automatically: - Send a notification to an Amazon SNS topic, or - Trigger an EC2 Auto Scaling or EC2 action CloudWatch Events CloudWatch Events lets you define rules matching incoming events changes in your AWS environment and route them to targets for processing. Targets can include: - Amazon EC2 instances - AWS Lambda functions - Kinesis streams - Amazon ECS tasks - Step Functions state machines - Amazon SNS topics - Amazon SQS queues - Built"},{"anchor":"monitoring-aws-resources","text":"-in targets CloudWatch Events becomes aware of operational changes as they happen and can respond by sending messages, activating functions, making changes, or capturing state information. Pricing CloudWatch gives you system-wide visibility into resource utilization, application performance, and operational health. No upfront commitment or minimum fee — you pay only for what you use, billed at the end of the month. --- CloudWatch Alarms — Deep Dive A CloudWatch alarm watches a single metric, or the result of a math expression based on metrics. Alarms can be based on: - Static threshold - Anomaly detection - Metric math expression For a static threshold alarm, the alarm enters the ALARM state when the metric breaches the threshold for a specified number of evaluation periods. You must specify: Setting Description --- --- Namespace Container for the metric, e.g. AWS/EC2 Metric The variable to measure, e.g. CPU Utilization Statistic Average, sum, minimum, maximum, sample count, or a percentile predefined or custom Period The evaluation period — each period aggregates into one data point Conditions Whether the metric is Greater, Greater or Equal, Lower or Equal, or Lower than a thresho"},{"anchor":"what-cloudwatch-does","text":"ld, plus the threshold value itself Additional configuration How many data points within the evaluation period must be breached to trigger the alarm, and how missing data should be treated Actions Send a notification to an SNS topic, or trigger an EC2 Auto Scaling / EC2 action 📚 More detail: Using Alarms — AWS Documentation https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html --- Section 2 Key Takeaways - Amazon CloudWatch helps you monitor your AWS resources — and the applications running on them — in real time. - CloudWatch enables you to: - Collect and track standard and custom metrics. - Set alarms to automatically send notifications to SNS topics, or trigger EC2 Auto Scaling / EC2 actions, based on a metric or expression's value relative to a threshold over time. - Define rules that match changes in your AWS environment and route events to targets for processing. --- Section 3: Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling When running applications on AWS, your architecture needs to scale to handle changes in demand. This section covers how to automatically scale EC2 instances with Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling . Why Is Scaling Important? Scaling is the ability to incr"},{"anchor":"metrics-and-alarms","text":"ease or decrease your application's compute capacity. Consider a workload with varying resource requirements across the week — say, peak demand on Wednesday and lowest demand on Sunday. There are two naive options: 1. Provision for peak demand always i.e., enough capacity for Wednesday, every day → resources sit underutilized most of the week → costs aren't optimized. 2. Provision for lower capacity to save costs → the app is under-capacity on high-demand days → risk of underperformance or even unavailability if the capacity problem isn't solved. Auto Scaling solves this by dynamically matching provisioned capacity to actual demand — covered in the sections that follow. --- AWS Training and Certification — Module 10: Auto Scaling and Monitoring"}]},"aws-12-terraform-vs-cloudformation-expanded":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-vs-cloudformation-expanded","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\03-terraform-vs-cloudformation.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner /} Terraform vs CloudFormation — Expanded Same job IaC , opposite bets: multi-cloud standard vs AWS-native . 1. Mental model analogy - Terraform = unlocked phone + any carrier. More setup state, backends , works everywhere. - CloudFormation = carrier-locked phone, free in box. Zero setup, only one network, deep integration voicemail, billing all native . Pick unlocked when you travel clouds; pick locked when you live in one building forever. 2. Terraform with 2 examples HashiCorp IBM , HCL, 3000+ providers, explicit tfstate , init→plan→apply . 3. CloudFormation with 2 examples Built into AWS, YAML/JSON templates → stacks , AWS stores state, auto-rollback, free. 4. Full mini-stack side-by-side VPC + EC2 Terraform HCL, references with . : CloudFormation YAML, references with Ref/ GetAtt : Same 3 resources. Terraform ~15 lines + refs auto-order; CFN ~15 lines YAML + intrinsic functions. Difference explodes at 50 resources: Terraform modules compress, CFN goes verbose 500-resource/stack limit fo"},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-analogy","text":"rces nested stacks . 5. Lifecycle: plan vs change set, rollback, drift Step Terraform CloudFormation --- --- --- Preview terraform plan local, seconds, +/~/− Change Set server-side, slower, console list Execute apply you watch log create/update-stack events tab streams Fail stops, state may be partial → fix + re-apply auto-rollback to last good safer default Drift plan shows diff Drift detection button + detect-stack-drift API Multi-account folders/workspaces/Terragrunt StackSets deploy 1 template to 100 accounts — best in class Terraform partial-failure example: 3 VMs, 2nd fails → 1st exists, 3rd pending. Fix AMI, re-apply, converges. CFN: same failure → whole stack rolls back, nothing left half-built great for compliance, slower to iterate . 6. CDK layer both have one - AWS CDK TS → synthesizes CFN: real loops/ifs, cdk diff ≈ plan, cdk deploy ≈ apply. - CDKTF TS/Python → synthesizes Terraform: same idea, multi-cloud. Rule: hate DSLs → Pulumi/CDK/CDKTF; want max jobs → plain HCL/YAML first. 7. Together + migration bridge Common split: CFN/Control Tower for org baseline SSO, audit, guardrails + Terraform for team workloads. 8. Decision flowchart 9. Interview Q&A expanded - One-line"},{"anchor":"2-terraform-with-2-examples","text":" diff? Terraform multi-cloud + self-state + best plan; CFN AWS-only + AWS-state + auto-rollback. - Why does TF need state, CFN not? TF is external, tracks IDs itself; CFN runs inside AWS, uses stack DB. - Stack vs Change Set? Deployed instance vs preview of update ≈ plan . - StackSets? One template → many accounts/regions org-wide baselines . - CFN limit? 500 resources/stack → nested stacks. - Which first? Terraform concepts transfer to CFN in a day . --- Next: Why Terraform /courses/terraform/level1/why-terraform/ → then State."}]},"aws-13-aws-certified-cloud-practitioner-slides-v44":{"chunks":[{"page":1,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com https://links.dat acumulus.com/ aws - cert - cloud - practitioner - pt - coupon https://links.datacumulus.com/aws - cert - cloud - practitioner - pt - coupon https://links.da tacumulus.com /aws - certified - practitioner - coupon https://links.data cumulus.com/aw s - certified - practitioner - coupon https://links.datacumulus.com/aw s - certified - practitioner - coupon AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner By Stéphane Maarek EXTRA PRACTICE EXAMS COURSE"},{"page":2,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Disclaimer: These slides are copyrighted and strictly for personal use only • This document is reserved for people enrolled into the AWS Cer tified Cloud Practitioner Cour se • Please do not share this document , it is intended for per sonal use and exam preparation only, thank you. • Best of luck for the exam and happy learning!"},{"page":3,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ta b l e o f C o n t e n t s • What is Cloud Computing? • AWS Identity & Access Management • Amazon EC2 • Amazon EC2 Instance Storage • Elastic Load Balancing & Auto Scaling Group • Amazon S3 • Databases & Analytics • Other Compute Services • Deploying & Managing Infrastructure at Scale • Global Infrastructure • Cloud Integration"},{"page":4,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ta b l e o f C o n t e n t s • Cloud Monitoring • Amazon VPC • Security & Compliance • Machine Learning • Account Management, Billing, & Support • Advanced Identity • Other AWS Services • AWS Architecting & EcoSystem • Exam Preparation • Congratulations"},{"page":5,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cer tified Cloud Practitioner Course CLF - C02"},{"page":6,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Welcome! We’re starting in 5 minutes • We’re going to prepare for the Cloud Practitioner exam – CLF - C02 • It’s a challenging certification, so this course will be long and interesting • Basic IT knowledge is helpful, but I will explain everything • We will cover over 40 AWS ser vices (out of the 200+ in AWS) • AWS / IT Beginner s welcome! (but take your time, it’s not a race) • Learn by doing – key learning technique! This course mixes both theory & hands on"},{"page":7,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Sample question: Certified Cloud Practitioner <= we will learn <= correct answer <= we will learn <= distractor (over 200 services in AWS) • A) AWS Storage Gateway • B) AWS Database Migration Service • C) Amazon EC2 • D) Amazon AppStream 2.0 • https://explore.skillbuilder.aws/learn/course/external/view/elearning/14050/aws - certified - cloud - practitioner - official - practice - question - set - clf - c02 - english Which AWS service would simplify the migration of a database to AWS?"},{"page":8,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com About me • I’m Stephane! • 11x AWS Certified • Wor ked with AWS many year s: built websites, apps, streaming platfor ms • Veteran Instructor on AWS (Certifications, CloudFormation, Lambda, EC2...) • Yo u c a n f i n d m e o n • LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/stephanemaarek • Instagram: https://Instagram.com/stephanemaarek • Medium: https://medium.com/@stephane.maarek • Tw i t t e r : https://twitter.com/stephanemaarek • GitHub: https://github.com/simplesteph"},{"page":9,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Yo u r A W S C e r t i f i c a t i o n j o u r n e y Foundational Learn AWS Cloud fundamentals and core concepts. No prior experience needed. Associate Validate core AWS skills. Prior cloud or IT experience recommended. Professional Master advanced AWS architecture and solutions. 2+ years AWS experience required. Specialty Demonstrate expertise in specific AWS technologies and services."},{"page":10,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Estimated Cost for this Course • AWS will give you $100 of free credits when you create your account (can go up to $200) • Creating resources in the cloud will cost money but they will use the credits you’ll have (if not I’ll let you know) • Yo u c a n r e a d m o r e a b o u t t h e AWS Free Tier at: https://aws.amazon.com/free/"},{"page":11,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Udemy Tips"},{"page":12,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What is Cloud Computing Section"},{"page":13,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com How websites work Server network Clients have IP addresses Servers have IP addresses Client"},{"page":14,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Just like when you’re sending post mail!"},{"page":15,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What is a server composed of? • Compute: CPU • Memory: RAM • Storage: Data • Database: Store data in a structured way • Network: Routers, switch, DNS server + ="},{"page":16,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IT Terminology • Network: cables, routers and servers connected with each other • Router : A networking device that fo r wards data packets between computer networks. They know where to send your packets on the internet! • Switch: Ta k e s a p a c k e t a n d s e n d i t t o t h e c o r r e c t s e r v e r / c l i e n t o n y o u r n e t w o r k Router Switch"},{"page":17,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Tr a d i t i o n a l l y, h o w t o b u i l d i n f r a s t r u c t u r e Data center Home or Garage Office"},{"page":18,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Problems with traditional IT approach • Pay for the rent for the data center • Pay for power supply, cooling, maintenance • Adding and replacing hardware takes time • Scaling is limited • Hire 24/7 team to monitor the infrastructure • How to deal with disasters? (earthquake, power shutdown, fire...) • Can we externalize all this?"},{"page":19,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What is Cloud Computing? • Cloud computing is the on - demand delivery of compute power, database storage, applications, and other IT resources • Through a cloud services platform with pay - as - you - go pricing • Yo u c a n provision exactly the right type and size of computing resources you need • Yo u c a n a c c e s s a s m a n y r e s o u r c e s a s y o u n e e d , almost instantly • Simple way to access servers, storage, databases and a set of application services • Amazon Web Services owns and maintains the network - connected hardware required for these application services, while you provision and use what you need via a web application."},{"page":20,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com The Cloud Office"},{"page":21,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Yo u ’ v e b e e n u s i n g s o m e C l o u d s e r v i c e s Gmail • E - mail cloud service • Pay for ONLY your emails stored (no infrastructure, etc.) Dropbox • Cloud Storage Service • Originally built on AWS Netflix • Built on AWS • Video on Demand"},{"page":22,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com The Deployment Models of the Cloud Private Cloud: • Cloud services used by a single organization, not exposed to the public. • Complete control • Security for sensitive applications • Meet specific business needs Public Cloud: • Cloud resources owned and operated by a third - party cloud service provider delivered over the Internet. • Six Advantages of Cloud Computing Hybrid Cloud: • Keep some servers on premises and extend some capabilities to the Cloud • Control over sensitive assets in your private infrastructure • Flexibility and cost - effectiveness of the public cloud"},{"page":23,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com The Five Characteristics of Cloud Computing • On - demand self service: • Users can provision resources and use them without human interaction from the service provider • Broad network access: • Resources available over the network, and can be accessed by diverse client platforms • Multi - tenancy and resource pooling: • Multiple customers can share the same infrastructure and applications with security and privacy • Multiple customers are serviced from the same physical resources • Rapid elasticity and scalability: • Automatically and quickly acquire and dispose resources when needed • Quickly and easily scale based on demand • Measured service: • Usage is measured, users pay correctly for what they have used"},{"page":24,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Six Advantages of Cloud Computing • Tr a d e c a p i t a l e x p e n s e ( C A P E X ) f o r o p e r a t i o n a l e x p e n s e ( O P E X ) • Pay On - Demand: don’t own hardware • Reduced Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) & Operational Expense (OPEX) • Benefit from massive economies of scale • Prices are reduced as AWS is more efficient due to large scale • Stop guessing capacity • Scale based on actual measured usage • Increase speed and agility • Stop spending money running and maintaining data centers • Go global in minutes: leverage the AWS global infrastructure"},{"page":25,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Problems solved by the Cloud • Flexibility: change resource types when needed • Cost - Effectiveness: pay as you go, for what you use • Scalability: accommodate larger loads by making hardware stronger or adding additional nodes • Elasticity: ability to scale out and scale - in when needed • High - availability and fault - tolerance: build across data centers • Agility: rapidly develop, test and launch software applications"},{"page":26,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ty p e s o f C l o u d C o m p u t i n g • Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) • Provide building blocks for cloud IT • Provides networking, computers, data storage space • Highest level of flexibility • Easy parallel with traditional on - premises IT • Platform as a Service (PaaS) • Removes the need for your organization to manage the underlying infrastructure • Focus on the deployment and management of your applications • Software as a Service (SaaS) • Completed product that is run and managed by the service provider"},{"page":27,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Applications Data Runtime Middleware O/S Virtualization Servers Storage Networking Applications Data Runtime Middleware O/S Virtualization Servers Storage Networking Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) Applications Data Runtime Middleware O/S Virtualization Servers Storage Networking Applications Data Runtime Middleware O/S Virtualization Servers Storage Networking On - premises Platform as a Service (PaaS) Software as a Service (SaaS) Managed by you Managed by others"},{"page":28,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Example of Cloud Computing Types • Infrastructure as a Service: • Amazon EC2 (on AWS) • GCP, Azure, Rackspace, Digital Ocean, Linode • Platform as a Service: • Elastic Beanstalk (on AWS) • Heroku, Google App Engine (GCP), Windows Azure (Microsoft) • Software as a Service: • Many AWS services (ex: Rekognition for Machine Learning) • Google Apps (Gmail), Dropbox, Zoom"},{"page":29,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Pricing of the Cloud – Quick Overview • AWS has 3 pricing fundamentals, following the pay - as - you - go pricing model • Compute: • Pay for compute time • Storage: • Pay for data stored in the Cloud • Data transfer OUT of the Cloud: • Data transfer IN is free • Solves the expensive issue of traditional IT"},{"page":30,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Histor y 2002: Internally launched 2003: Amazon infrastructure is one of their core strength. Idea to market 2004: Launched publicly with SQS 2006: Re - launched publicly with SQS, S3 & EC2 2007: Launched in Europe"},{"page":31,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Number Facts • In 2023 , AWS had $ 90 billion in annual revenue • AWS accounts for 31 % of the market in Q1 2024 (Microsoft is 2nd with 2 5 %) • Pioneer and Leader of the AWS Cloud Mar ket for the 13 th consecutive year • Over 1,000,000 active users Gartner Magic Quadrant"},{"page":32,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Use Cases • AWS enables you to build sophisticated, scalable applications • Applicable to a diverse set of industries • Use cases include • Enterprise IT , Backup & Stor age , Big Data analytics • Web site hosting , Mobile & Social Apps • Gaming"},{"page":33,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Global Infrastr ucture • AWS Regions • AWS Availability Zones • AWS Data Center s • AWS Edge Locations / Points of Presence • https://infrastructure.aws/"},{"page":34,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Regions • AWS has Regions all around the world • Names can be us - east - 1, eu - west - 3... • A region is a cluster of data centers • Most AWS services are region - scoped https://aws.amazon.com/about - aws/global - infrastructure/"},{"page":35,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com How to choose an AWS Region? • Compliance with data governance and legal requirements : data never leaves a region without your explicit permission • Proximity to customers : reduced latency • Available ser vice s within a Region : new services and new features aren’t available in every Region • Pricing : pricing varies region to region and is transparent in the service pricing page If you need to launch a new application, where should you do it? ? ? ? ?"},{"page":36,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Availability Zones • Each region has many availability zones (usually 3, min is 3, max is 6). Example: • ap - southeast - 2a • ap - southeast - 2b • ap - southeast - 2c • Each availability zone (AZ) is one or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking, and connectivity • They’re separate from each other, so that they’re isolated from disasters • They’re connected with high bandwidth, ultra - low latency networking AWS Region Sydney: ap - southeast - 2 ap - southeast - 2a ap - southeast - 2b ap - southeast - 2c"},{"page":37,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Points of Presence (Edge Locations) • Amazon has 400+ Points of Presence (400+ Edge Locations & 10+ Regional Caches) in 90+ cities across 40+ countries • Content is delivered to end users with lower latency https://aws.amazon.com/cloudfront/features/"},{"page":38,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com To u r o f t h e A W S C o n s o l e • AWS has Global Ser vices: • Identity and Access Management (IAM) • Route 53 (DNS service) • CloudFront (Content Delivery Network) • WAF (Web Application Firewall) • Most AWS services are Region - scoped : • Amazon EC2 (Infrastructure as a Service) • Elastic Beanstalk (Platform as a Service) • Lambda (Function as a Service) • Rekognition (Software as a Service) • Region Table: https://aws.amazon.com/about - aws/global - infrastructure/regional - product - services"},{"page":39,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Shared Responsibility Model diagram https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared - responsibility - model/ AWS = RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE SECURITY OF THE CLOUD CUSTOMER = RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE SECURITY IN THE CLOUD"},{"page":40,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Acceptable Use Policy • https://aws.amazon.com/aup/ • No Illegal, Harmful, or Offensive Use or Content • No Security Violations • No Network Abuse • No E - Mail or Other Message Abuse"},{"page":41,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Section"},{"page":42,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM: Users & Groups • IAM = Identity and Access Management, Global service • Root account created by default, shouldn’t be used or shared • Users are people within your organization, and can be grouped • Groups only contain users, not other groups • Users don’t have to belong to a group, and user can belong to multiple groups Alice Bob Charles David Edward Group: Developers Group: Operations Group Audit Team Fred"},{"page":43,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM: Permissions • Users or Groups can be assigned JSON documents called policies • These policies define the permissions of the users • In AWS you apply the least privilege principle : don’t give more permissions than a user needs { \"Version\" : \"2012 - 10 - 17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : \"ec2:Describe*\" , \"Resource\" : \"*\" }, { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : \" elasticloadbalancing:Describe *\" , \"Resource\" : \"*\" }, { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \" cloudwatch:ListMetrics \" , \" cloudwatch:GetMetricStatistics \" , \" cloudwatch:Describe *\" ], \"Resource\" : \"*\" } ] }"},{"page":44,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Policies inheritance Alice Bob Charles David Edward Developers Operations Audit Team Fred inline"},{"page":45,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Policies Structure • Consists of • Ver sion: policy language version, always include “2012 - 10 - 17” • Id: an identifier for the policy (optional) • Statement: one or more individual statements (required) • Statements consists of • Sid: an identifier for the statement (optional) • Effect: whether the statement allows or denies access (Allow, Deny) • Principal: account/user/role to which this policy applied to • Action: list of actions this policy allows or denies • Resource: list of resources to which the actions applied to • Condition: conditions for when this policy is in effect (optional)"},{"page":46,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM – Password Policy • Strong passwords = higher security for your account • In AWS, you can setup a password policy: • Set a minimum password length • Require specific character types: • including uppercase letters • lowercase letters • numbers • non - alphanumeric characters • Allow all IAM users to change their own passwords • Require users to change their password after some time (password expiration) • Prevent password re - use"},{"page":47,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Multi Factor Authentication - MFA • Users have access to your account and can possibly change configurations or delete resources in your AWS account • Yo u w a n t t o p r o t e c t y o u r R o o t A c c o u n t s a n d I A M u s e r s • MFA = password you know + security device you own • Main benefit of MFA: if a password is stolen or hacked, the account is not compromised Alice + Password => Successful login"},{"page":48,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com MFA devices options in AWS Virtual MFA device Google Authenticator (phone only) Authy (phone only) Universal 2nd Factor (U2F) Security Key YubiKey by Yubico (3 rd party) Support for multiple tokens on a single device. Support for multiple root and IAM users using a single security key"},{"page":49,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com MFA devices options in AWS Hardware Key Fob MFA Device Provided by Gemalto (3 rd party) Hardware Key Fob MFA Device for AWS GovCloud (US) Provided by SurePassID (3 rd party)"},{"page":50,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com How can users access AWS ? • To a c c e s s A W S , y o u h a v e t h r e e o p t i o n s : • AWS Management Console (protected by password + MFA) • AWS Command Line Interface (CLI): protected by access keys • AWS Software Developer Kit (SDK) - for code: protected by access keys • Access Keys are generated through the AWS Console • Users manage their own access keys • Access Keys are secret, just like a password. Don’t share them • Access Key ID ~= username • Secret Access Key ~= password"},{"page":51,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Example (Fake) Access Keys • Access key ID: AKIASK4E37PV4983d6C • Secret Access Key: AZPN3zojWozWCndIjhB0Unh8239a1bzbzO5fqqkZq • Remember : don’t share your access keys"},{"page":52,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What’s the AWS CLI? • A tool that enables you to interact with AWS services using commands in your command - line shell • Direct access to the public APIs of AWS services • Yo u c a n d e v e l o p s c r i p t s t o m a n a g e y o u r r e s o u r c e s • It’s open - source https://github.com/aws/aws - cli • Alternative to using AWS Management Console"},{"page":53,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What’s the AWS SDK? • AWS Software Development Kit (AWS SDK) • Language - specific APIs (set of libraries) • Enables you to access and manage AWS services programmatically • Embedded within your application • Supports • SDKs (JavaScript, Python, PHP, .NET, Ruby, Java, Go, Node.js, C++) • Mobile SDKs (Android, iOS, ...) • IoT Device SDKs (Embedded C, Arduino, ...) • Example: AWS CLI is built on AWS SDK for Python AWS SDK Your Application"},{"page":54,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Roles for Services • Some AWS service will need to perform actions on your behalf • To d o s o , w e w i l l a s s i g n permissions to AWS services with IAM Roles • Common roles: • EC2 Instance Roles • Lambda Function Roles • Roles for CloudFormation EC2 Instance (virtual server) IAM Role Access AWS"},{"page":55,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Security Tools • IAM Credentials Report (account - level) • a report that lists all your account's users and the status of their various credentials • IAM Access Advisor (user - level) • Access advisor shows the service permissions granted to a user and when those services were last accessed. • Yo u c a n u s e t h i s i n f o r m a t i o n t o r e v i s e y o u r p o l i c i e s ."},{"page":56,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Guidelines & Best Practices • Don’t use the root account except for AWS account setup • One physical user = One AWS user • Assign users to groups and assign permissions to groups • Create a strong password policy • Use and enforce the use of Multi Factor Authentication (MFA) • Create and use Roles for giving permissions to AWS services • Use Access Keys for Programmatic Access (CLI / SDK) • Audit permissions of your account using IAM Credentials Repor t & IAM Access Advisor • Never share IAM users & Access Keys"},{"page":57,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Shared Responsibility Model for IAM • Infrastructure (global network security) • Configuration and vulnerability analysis • Compliance validation You • Users, Groups, Roles, Policies management and monitoring • Enable MFA on all accounts • Rotate all your keys often • Use IAM tools to apply appropriate permissions • Analyze access patterns & review permissions"},{"page":58,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Section – Summary • Users: mapped to a physical user, has a password for AWS Console • Groups: contains users only • Policies: JSON document that outlines permissions for users or groups • Roles: for EC2 instances or AWS services • Security: MFA + Password Policy • AWS CLI: manage your AWS services using the command - line • AWS SDK: manage your AWS services using a programming language • Access Keys: access AWS using the CLI or SDK • Audit: IAM Credential Reports & IAM Access Advisor"},{"page":59,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Section"},{"page":60,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Amazon EC2 • EC2 is one of the most popular of AWS’ offering • EC2 = Elastic Compute Cloud = Infrastructure as a Service • It mainly consists in the capability of : • Renting virtual machines (EC2) • Storing data on virtual drives (EBS) • Distributing load across machines (ELB) • Scaling the services using an auto - scaling group (ASG) • Knowing EC2 is fundamental to understand how the Cloud works"}]},"aws-14-level-2-networking-deep-dive-tcp-udp-subnetting-vpc-routing":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-networking-deep-dive-tcp-udp-subnetting-vpc-routing-for-terraform","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\03-networking-deep-dive.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate /} Level 2 — Networking Deep Dive: TCP/UDP, Subnetting, VPC Routing for Terraform Everything you must know about TCP/UDP, IP subnetting, and routing to read AWS networking in Terraform and pass a network engineering interview . Every concept has a Terraform usage example and a \"why Terraform cares\" callout. 1. TCP vs UDP the protocol picture Aspect TCP Transmission Control Protocol UDP User Datagram Protocol --- --- --- Connection Connection-oriented 3-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK Connectionless fire-and-forget Reliability Guaranteed, ordered, error-checked, retransmits lost packets Best-effort, no guarantee, no order Speed Slower overhead Faster less overhead Header size 20–60 bytes 8 bytes Use cases HTTP/HTTPS, SSH, databases, file transfer DNS, video streaming, gaming, VoIP, metrics Flow control Yes sliding window, congestion control No When broken \"Connection refused\", \"timeout\", \"RST\" Silent loss Terraform aws_security_group ingress with protocol = \"tcp\" protocol = \"udp\" DNS resolver, "},{"anchor":"1-tcp-vs-udp-the-protocol-picture","text":"syslog, metrics Terraform port range from_port / to_port = same single value, OR a range 3000–3999 Same syntax Why Terraform cares: every aws_security_group and aws_lb_target_group rule needs from_port / to_port / protocol . Mismatched protocol = silent \"no connection\". Most common bug: opening TCP/80 when the server listens on UDP e.g., a custom metrics endpoint . 2. The 3-way handshake why \"connection refused\" means what it means Failure modes Terraform users hit: - Connection refused RST : server reached but no process listening on that port → wrong from_port in SG, or app crashed - Timeout no response : packet silently dropped → SG denied OR NACL denied OR route missing - TLS handshake error : wrong port OR wrong protocol OR expired cert - RST mid-stream : security group or NACL killed an established connection — rare but happens with state-tracking bugs NACLs are stateless; see §6 Why Terraform cares: \"I added the SG rule, why does the connection still fail?\" → check the NACL, the route table, the SG referencing another SG chained , and the health check on the ALB. The handshake model explains why a single missing rule = \"timeout\" silent , not \"refused\" loud . 3. IP addressing"},{"anchor":"2-the-3-way-handshake-why-connection-refused-means-what-it-means","text":" & CIDR the math behind every Terraform cidr_block What is an IP address? 32 bits IPv4 split into network prefix + host ID . 10.0.1.23 = 32-bit number; 10.0.0.0/16 says \"the first 16 bits identify the network, last 16 are for hosts.\" CIDR = Classless Inter-Domain Routing. The /N is the prefix length; more bits = smaller network. Every AWS resource that takes a CIDR uses this exact format: aws_vpc.cidr_block , aws_subnet.cidr_block , aws_route.cidr_block , aws_security_group.cidr_blocks . Quick decimal → binary the only math you need You don't compute this manually; Terraform's cidrsubnet and cidrhost do it. The mental model: /N = how many bits of network, the rest are host bits. Subnetting the operation Terraform does for you Given 10.0.0.0/16 , you split it into smaller networks: - 256 subnets of /24 each = 256 IPs, 251 usable - 4 subnets of /18 each = 16,384 IPs, 16,381 usable Formula: newbits = number of bits ADDED to the prefix ; subnets count = 2^newbits. Reserved IPs every AWS subnet loses 5 AWS reserves in every subnet : - .0 — network address - .1 — VPC router default gateway - .2 — DNS server when enable_dns_support = true - .3 — reserved for future - .255 or last IP — bro"},{"anchor":"3-ip-addressing-amp-cidr-the-math-behind-every-terraform-cidr-block","text":"adcast So a /24 = 256 - 5 = 251 usable IPs . /28 = 16 - 5 = 11 usable . Terraform can't change this; plan accordingly. IP ranges: which to pick for a VPC? RFC 1918 private ranges the only legal choice for a VPC unless you bring your own via BYOIP : - 10.0.0.0/8 — 16M IPs, the most common study default - 172.16.0.0/12 — 1M IPs AWS default VPC uses 172.31.0.0/16 - 192.168.0.0/16 — 65k IPs small networks, lab/dev Pick 10.x.0.0/16 e.g., 10.0.0.0/16 , 10.10.0.0/16 , 10.20.0.0/16 per env so you can peer/connect multiple VPCs without CIDR overlap. Why Terraform cares: cidrsubnet \"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, k returns the k-th /24. If you pass overlapping CIDRs across VPCs that peer, the peer fails. Plan with cidrsubnets and data \"aws_caller_identity\" to avoid overlap. 4. Routing the real reason SG isn't enough Security Groups are stateful; routing is what actually delivers packets to the right subnet. A packet from your laptop to an EC2 in a private subnet travels: The route table has rules: destination CIDR → target igw, nat, vpc peering, vpc endpoint . AWS picks the most specific match longest prefix . Route table selection rules: - Every VPC has a main route table auto-created - A subnet is assoc"},{"anchor":"what-is-an-ip-address","text":"iated with exactly one route table explicit OR the main - Routes within a table are evaluated by longest prefix match most specific wins Why Terraform cares: SG allows traffic on port 80, but if the route table has no 0.0.0.0/0 → igw for that subnet, packets go nowhere. \"Why can't my EC2 reach the internet?\" → 80% of the time, it's the route table, not the SG. 5. Subnet tiers — public vs private vs database Tier Route table has SG from internet Who lives here --- --- --- --- Public 0.0.0.0/0 → igw or NAT for outbound map_public_ip_on_launch = true ALB, NAT gateway, bastion host, VPN Private 0.0.0.0/0 → nat or no default route No, must come through a public load balancer App servers, ECS tasks Database No default route or local-only No, no NAT RDS, ElastiCache, internal services Why Terraform cares: subnet tier is a Terraform code pattern, not a magic tag. You enforce it by: 1. Route table association which RT the subnet uses 2. map_public_ip_on_launch = true only on public subnets — the launch-time behavior 3. Security groups DB SGs allow only app SG as source, not 0.0.0.0/0 6. NACL vs Security Group the stateful vs stateless question Security Group SG Network ACL NACL --- --- --- "},{"anchor":"quick-decimal-binary-the-only-math-you-need","text":"Level Instance / ENI Subnet Stateful? Yes — return traffic auto-allowed No — both directions must be explicit Rules Allow only Allow AND deny numbered 1–32766 Default Allow all outbound, deny all inbound Allow all both directions Common use Default; almost all traffic control Block a known-bad IP range; rare Terraform aws_security_group + aws_security_group_rule aws_network_acl + aws_network_acl_rule NACL gotcha stateful vs stateless in action : you allow inbound TCP/443. NACL won't auto-allow the return traffic — you need a separate rule for ephemeral ports 1024–65535 . SGs don't have this problem. Why Terraform cares: most teams don't touch NACLs. But when debugging \"why is the connection dropping mid-stream\" or \"why does ping work but HTTP doesn't\", NACLs are often the culprit. Add NACL rules only after the SG is verified open. 7. DNS in a VPC why enable_dns_support and enable_dns_hostnames matter - enable_dns_support = true → VPC has DNS resolution at the .2 address of every subnet - enable_dns_hostnames = true → instances with public IPs get a public DNS name e.g., ec2-1-2-3-4.compute-1.amazonaws.com Why Terraform cares: if you turn off DNS, no service discovery, no Route 53 i"},{"anchor":"subnetting-the-operation-terraform-does-for-you","text":"nternal zones, no nslookup between instances. Almost every Terraform module assumes DNS is on. 8. Endpoints — skip the NAT for AWS services For private subnets talking to S3/DynamoDB, a NAT gateway costs money and adds latency. VPC endpoints keep traffic on the AWS backbone: Why Terraform cares: add a few lines, save NAT data charges, gain predictable latency. The endpoint service name format is com.amazonaws. . . 9. Peering and Transit Gateway multi-VPC - VPC peering: 1:1, no transitive routing, free per hour, no edge routing. Works between accounts. - Transit Gateway: hub for many VPCs and on-prem via VPN/DX . Paid per attachment + data. Scales past 3-4 VPCs. Why Terraform cares: once you have 1 VPC dev/staging/prod or multi-account , you hit peering limits quickly. Transit Gateway centralizes the topology; Terragrunt or Terraform modules make the per-VPC config reusable. 10. DNS over VPC peering the silent bug By default, DNS queries from VPC A to VPC B's Route 53 hosted zone do NOT resolve through the peering connection. You must enable it: Why Terraform cares: without this, your app in VPC A can't resolve a private hostname in VPC B — connection times out and the SG/route chec"},{"anchor":"reserved-ips-every-aws-subnet-loses-5","text":"k passes. This is the 1 \"DNS works locally but not across VPCs\" issue. 11. Port ranges you'll see in Terraform SG rules Port s Protocol Service Common Terraform use --- --- --- --- 22 TCP SSH Bastion, EC2 connect replace with SSM in prod 53 TCP+UDP DNS Route 53 resolvers, internal DNS 80, 443 TCP HTTP/HTTPS ALB ingress, CloudFront, API Gateway 123 UDP NTP Time sync default open in default SG 389, 636 TCP LDAP/LDAPS Directory services 5432 TCP PostgreSQL RDS ingress 3306 TCP MySQL RDS ingress 6379 TCP Redis ElastiCache ingress 8500 TCP Consul Service mesh 51820 UDP WireGuard VPN 1024–65535 TCP Ephemeral Required for NACL return traffic ; SG doesn't need Why Terraform cares: when you write from_port = 443, to_port = 443, protocol = \"tcp\" , that's the SG rule for an ALB or web server. Memorize the few you use, look up the rest in a chart. 12. The full mental model one picture Why Terraform cares: every resource you create aws_vpc , aws_subnet , aws_route_table , aws_route , aws_security_group , aws_network_acl maps to one of these boxes. Once you can name what each piece does, the rest is syntax. 13. Errors you'll hit and the fix Error Cause Fix --- --- --- cidr block conflicts with e"},{"anchor":"ip-ranges-which-to-pick-for-a-vpc","text":"xisting VPC overlapping CIDR across accounts/VPCs pick non-overlapping range; use data.aws_caller_identity to namespace InvalidSubnet: not enough IP addresses subnet too small for the resource count bump /28 to /24 or larger network connection refused wrong port OR app not listening OR SG allows different port curl -v from same subnet; aws ec2 describe-instances for ENI connection timeout route missing OR SG deny OR NACL deny walk route table; check SG; check NACL rules especially ephemeral ports Could not resolve host DNS off OR peering DNS option off enable_dns_support = true ; aws_vpc_peering_connection_options allow_remote_vpc_dns_resolution = true Instance reaches internet but can't reach S3 NAT down OR no S3 endpoint add aws_vpc_endpoint for S3 to the private route table cheaper than NAT Health check failed: 503 SG blocks ALB SG source, OR wrong port in target group SG rule: security_groups = aws_security_group.alb.id not cidr_blocks Operation timeout on apply dependency cycle rare with refs OR provider issue terraform plan -refresh-only ; check resource graph 14. Interview Q&A - TCP vs UDP? TCP = connection-oriented, reliable, ordered, slower HTTP, SSH, DB . UDP = connection"},{"anchor":"4-routing-the-real-reason-sg-isn-t-enough","text":"less, fast, no guarantee DNS, video, metrics . - 3-way handshake? SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK. Failures: RST refused means server reached but no process; timeout means packet dropped. - CIDR /24 in 10.0.0.0/16? cidrsubnet \"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 0 = 10.0.0.0/24 . 256 possible /24s; 251 usable per subnet 5 reserved . - Public vs private subnet — actual difference? Route table: public has 0.0.0.0/0 → igw ; private has 0.0.0.0/0 → nat or nothing . Plus map_public_ip_on_launch = true on public. - SG vs NACL? SG = stateful, instance-level, allow-only. NACL = stateless, subnet-level, allow + deny. SGs first; NACLs only when needed. - VPC endpoint vs NAT? NAT = generic internet egress paid data . Endpoint = direct to one AWS service, cheaper, lower latency. Use endpoints for S3/DynamoDB always. - DNS over peering broken? Enable allow_remote_vpc_dns_resolution = true on BOTH sides via aws_vpc_peering_connection_options . The 1 silent inter-VPC bug. - Reserved IPs in a subnet? 5: network .0 , VPC router .1 , DNS .2 , future .3 , broadcast last . Plan capacity accordingly. --- Next: Multi-Env /courses/terraform/level2/multi-env/ workspaces + per-env sizing deep-dive, or modules authoring."}]},"aws-15-state-commands-list-show-pull-mv-rm-import":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"state-commands-list-show-pull-mv-rm-import","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\22-state-commands.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner→Intermediate /} State Commands — list / show / pull / mv / rm / import Day 1 · Managing state from the CLI State commands read and reshape Terraform's memory WITHOUT touching real infrastructure. apply changes the cloud; these change the ledger. Every one of them is safe to rehearse on the emulator first. 1. Inspect read-only, run freely 2. Reshape refactor without re-creating Rename the tracking, not the resource: next plan shows zero changes because the cloud object never moved — only the ledger entry did. This is how you rename resources without downtime. rm stops managing the resource — the EC2 keeps running, Terraform just forgets it. Opposite of destroy which deletes the cloud object . Use when decommissioning from Terraform but keeping the machine. 3. Adopt bring click-ops under management Steps: write the resource block first matching the real object's config as closely as you can , import to bind the ID, then plan — and fix the config until the diff is empty. Import binds identity only; it never"},{"anchor":"1-inspect-read-only-run-freely","text":" generates configuration. Remember - ✓ Inspect: list what , show details , pull raw JSON backup . - ✓ Reshape: mv rename tracking, zero-downtime refactor , rm stop managing, object survives . - ✓ Adopt: write block → import → plan until clean. - ⚠ Gotcha: state rm feels like cleanup but orphans live infrastructure — the next engineer finds an untracked EC2 burning money. rm only when you mean \"keep it, unmanaged\" and say so in the commit message. - 🎓 Exam takeaway: \" rm vs destroy ?\" → rm forgets object lives , destroy deletes object dies . \"Rename without re-creating?\" → state mv ."}]},"aws-16-level-2-provision-the-onext-application-load-balancer-stack":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-provision-the-onext-application-load-balancer-stack","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\09-alb-walkthrough.md 2026-09-08 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Advanced type=hands-on walkthrough time=30min Floci / 60min AWS /} Level 2 — Provision the ONEXT Application Load Balancer Stack Hands-on walkthrough of the task: build a two-tier ALB infrastructure for the ONEXT project, both the internet-facing ALB and the internal ALB, with their listeners, target groups, attachments, security groups, and health checks — all as Terraform IaC, ready for the FLOCI pipeline. Companion to Workspaces /courses/terraform/level2/workspaces/ , count vs for_each /courses/terraform/level2/count-for-each/ , Outputs /courses/terraform/level2/outputs/ , Workspace Walkthrough /courses/terraform/level2/workspace-walkthrough/ . Estimated time: 30 minutes with Floci, 60 minutes on real AWS. --- 0. The task in one paragraph Provision a public Application Load Balancer in front of two frontend EC2 instances public subnets and an internal Application Load Balancer in front of two backend EC2 instances private subnets . Four custom security groups, two target groups with HTTP health checks, two li"},{"anchor":"0-the-task-in-one-paragraph","text":"steners, four target-group attachments, plus the surrounding VPC plumbing. Use Terraform 1.x with the hashicorp/aws ~ 5.0 provider, exactly the modern arguments documented at https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs . Drive dev/staging/prod with workspaces so one codebase ships three environments. 1. The resource map 2. The project layout File Why it's its own file ------------ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ versions.tf Lock Terraform & provider versions — protects the team from drift. providers.tf Floci endpoint switching lives here so the rest of the code never knows whether AWS is real or emulated. variables.tf Every input that the next platform engineer might tweak. locals.tf name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${terraform.workspace}\" so every resource names itself correctly. main.tf All resources — VPC plumbing + 4 SGs + EC2 + 2 ALBs + 2 TGs + 2 listeners + 4 TG attachments. outputs.tf DNS names and ARNs that downstream stacks DNS, autoscaling need. 3. versions.tf — the pin that makes it portable - ~ 5.0 means 5.x but not 6.0 — the operator can roll forward to bug-fix releases without re"},{"anchor":"1-the-resource-map","text":"-approval. - Commit the auto-generated .terraform.lock.hcl next to this file so a teammate's init downloads the same provider bytes. 4. providers.tf — Floci or real AWS with one variable The dynamic \"endpoints\" block is the trick — when var.floci_endpoint is null the block is empty and we hit real AWS; when it points at Floci we redirect every API the AWS provider might call ec2 , elb , elbv2 , iam , sts , s3 . The elbv2 line is mandatory for ALBs — the classic elb endpoint only serves CLBs, so a missing elbv2 line will cause aws_lb to silently call real AWS even with TF_VAR_floci_endpoint set a bug hit and fixed in the source vault . insecure = true plus the four skip_ flags are LocalStack/Floci-friendly defaults — they strip TLS, IMDS, account-ID and STS validation that's pointless when the API is a stub. 5. variables.tf — every knob Inputs the platform team can tune per workspace: Var Default Notes ------------------------- ------------------------ ---------------------------------------------- region us-east-1 One region per deploy. floci_endpoint null Set http://localhost:4566 to use Floci. project onext Resource-name prefix. vpc_cidr 10.30.0.0/16 public_subnets \"10.30.1.0/24\""},{"anchor":"2-the-project-layout","text":",\"10.30.2.0/24\" private_subnets \"10.30.10.0/24\",\"10.30.11.0/24\" frontend_instance_type t3.micro backend_instance_type t3.micro frontend_count 2 Used by count and by TG attachments. backend_count 2 health_check_path / Target ping path on the backend. health_check_matcher 200 Comma-separated list of acceptable HTTP codes. The Floci switch is a string not a bool because the AWS provider's endpoints { ... } block accepts an HTTP URL — a bool would force an extra indirection. 6. locals.tf — workspace-aware naming Every Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-vpc\" becomes onext-dev-vpc in dev, onext-staging-vpc in staging, onext-prod-vpc in prod. Same for ALB names, target group names, listener names — one codebase, three name spaces. 7. main.tf — the resources, in dependency order 7.1 Networking foundation always first data \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\" gives us real AZ names. Then aws_vpc , aws_internet_gateway , aws_subnet.public , aws_subnet.private , aws_route_table.public with the IGW default route , aws_route_table.private , and two aws_route_table_association blocks using count = length aws_subnet.public / length aws_subnet.private . That last bit is the lesson from count vs for_each"},{"anchor":"3-versions-tf-the-pin-that-makes-it-portable","text":" /courses/terraform/level2/count-for-each/ — count over a list makes the code shrink if you ever go from 2 AZs to 1 without touching the associations. 7.2 Security groups 4 Each SG gets its own resource so changes review cleanly: SG Ingress Why ------------------- ---------------------------------------------------- ---------------------------------------- public_alb TCP 80/443 from 0.0.0.0/0 Internet-facing load balancer. internal_alb TCP 80 from frontend_ec2 SG and VPC CIDR Defense-in-depth. frontend_ec2 TCP 80 from public_alb SG only ALB → EC2 path. backend_ec2 TCP 80 from internal_alb SG only Internal traffic only. The two SGs that reference each other frontend_ec2 ↔ public_alb , backend_ec2 ↔ internal_alb form a dependency chain Terraform resolves automatically — the SGs are created in topological order, no depends_on needed. 7.3 EC2 fleet 2 + 2 data \"aws_ami\" \"amazon_linux\" looks up the latest amzn2 AMI so the code doesn't pin an outdated AMI id. aws_instance.frontend and aws_instance.backend each use count = var.frontend_count / var.backend_count and a user_data script that installs Apache and serves a workspace-aware page — so the health-check / path returns HTTP 200 the mo"},{"anchor":"4-providers-tf-floci-or-real-aws-with-one-variable","text":"ment the instance boots. Spread them across AZs with subnet_id = aws_subnet.public count.index % length aws_subnet.public .id . Floci note: Floci's EC2 implementation returns instances in terminated state, which makes Terraform's state serializer crash. The IaC is correct and would work on real AWS; the run-alb.sh helper excludes the EC2 + TG-attach targets from the Floci apply so the rest of the stack can be verified end-to-end. 7.4 Public ALB The four resources: load balancer, target group, listener, attachment. That's the canonical ALB quartet — every Application Load Balancer stack in AWS needs at least these four blocks per tier. 7.5 Internal ALB same quartet, internal = true The internal block is the public block copied with internal = true , swapped security group, swapped subnets, swapped target group, swapped instance set. Name changes int vs pub keep the ALB names unique in AWS. 7.6 Health-check anatomy The numbers are tuned for a small backend. Rule of thumb: interval = 2 × timeout , healthy_threshold + unhealthy_threshold = 5 for fast convergence on small fleets. 8. outputs.tf — what the rest of the platform consumes Output Purpose -------------------------- -----------"},{"anchor":"5-variables-tf-every-knob","text":"----------------------------- vpc_id For peering/TGW/observability stacks. public_subnet_ids For ASGs, Lambda ENIs, etc. private_subnet_ids Same. public_alb_dns_name Route53 alias records, app config. public_alb_zone_id Required for Route53 alias to ALB. internal_alb_dns_name Internal service discovery. public_target_group_arn For autoscaling attachments. internal_target_group_arn Same. frontend_instance_ids For SSM/CloudWatch inventory. backend_instance_ids Same. 9. Workspaces drive dev/staging/prod Each workspace gets its own state file under terraform.tfstate.d/ /terraform.tfstate and its own resources in Floci 3 separate VPCs, 12 separate subnets, 12 separate SGs, 6 ALBs, 6 target groups, 6 listeners — all live in Floci . 10. Verifying the apply The script hits the Floci endpoint directly and dumps every VPC, subnet, security group, load balancer, target group, and listener it sees. Confirms Apply complete matches what's actually in the emulator. 11. Idempotence check Re-running terraform plan on a clean workspace should return \"No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.\" That's the proof the IaC is correct, not just one-shot. 12. Cross-reference with the AWS pr"},{"anchor":"6-locals-tf-workspace-aware-naming","text":"ovider docs Resource Doc anchor --------------------------------------- ----------------------------------------------------------- aws_lb registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs/resources/lb aws_lb_listener .../resources/lb_listener aws_lb_target_group .../resources/lb_target_group aws_lb_target_group_attachment .../resources/lb_target_group_attachment aws_security_group .../resources/security_group aws_vpc , aws_subnet , aws_route_table , aws_internet_gateway , aws_route_table_association .../resources/ for each aws_instance .../resources/instance data \"aws_ami\" .../data-sources/ami Every argument used in this guide internal , load_balancer_type , target_type , health_check { ... } , default_action { type = \"forward\" } , ingress { security_groups = ... } is from the 5.x docs — the task's hard requirement. 13. FLOCI pipeline integration The four .sh files run-floci.sh , floci-wrapper.sh at the repo root + run-alb.sh , verify-alb.sh here wire the project into the FLOCI pipeline: 1. CI sets TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 . 2. terraform fmt -check -recursive enforces formatting CI gate . 3. terraform init -input=false pulls the locked provider. 4. terra"},{"anchor":"7-main-tf-the-resources-in-dependency-order","text":"form validate fails the build on syntax errors. 5. For each workspace dev , staging , prod : - terraform workspace select $ws CI fails fast if missing . - ./run-alb.sh $ws applies with -auto-approve and the EC2 targets excluded Floci limitation . - ./verify-alb.sh queries Floci to prove the stack is live. - terraform plan runs again — must report No changes idempotence . The EC2/TG-attach blocks stay in main.tf so the same code ships to staging/prod on real AWS unchanged — Floci is for pipeline verification, not production. 14. Cleanup Real AWS teardown: drop the -target filters and let terraform destroy -auto-approve reap every resource including EC2s and TG attachments. --- 15. Cheat sheet — the FLOCI truth table Resource Floci behavior Real AWS --------------------------------- -------------------------------------- ------------ aws_vpc + subnets + routes + IGW full full aws_security_group full full aws_lb ALB full uses elbv2 endpoint full aws_lb_target_group full full aws_lb_listener full full aws_lb_target_group_attachment full but target instance is a stub full aws_instance returns terminated , crashes state write full data \"aws_ami\" returns the most-recent amzn2 stub full Fl"},{"anchor":"7-1-networking-foundation-always-first","text":"oci is complete for the ALB control plane and partial for EC2 . The IaC in this guide is complete for both — Floci verifies the control plane, real AWS ships the full stack. 16. Recap - 18 resource blocks in main.tf : VPC, IGW, 4 subnets, 2 RTs + 2 RT-associations, 4 SGs, 2 EC2 frontend + 2 EC2 backend , 2 ALBs, 2 target groups, 2 listeners, 2 TG attachments count-driven . - Every resource uses the modern AWS provider ~ 5.0 syntax — internal = false / true , load_balancer_type = \"application\" , target_type = \"instance\" , health_check { ... } , default_action { type = \"forward\" } , ingress { security_groups = ... } . - Workspaces isolate three environments from one codebase; verified by deploying all three to Floci. - Idempotent — re-running plan reports no changes. - FLOCI-ready — run-alb.sh + verify-alb.sh form the pipeline entry/exit gates; EC2 is excluded only because Floci's stub crashes Terraform state, not because of an IaC defect. That's the task done end-to-end: IaC authored, validated, applied to a real ish AWS-compatible emulator across three workspaces, verified live, and reproducible from a fresh clone with three shell commands."}]},"aws-17-aws-provider-setup-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"aws-provider-setup-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\07-aws-provider-setup.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner→Intermediate /} AWS Provider Setup — Complete Guide One-line: the provider \"aws\" block plus credentials that never touch code = Terraform talking to your account. Get auth right once, every guide after this just works. 1. Minimal setup study lab, 2 files Notice: no keys in code. Credentials come from outside below . That's the 1 rule. 2. How credentials reach the provider priority order AWS provider checks, in order — first hit wins: Option A — env vars quick local test Windows git-bash : same export works in your terminal; PowerShell uses $env:AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=\"...\" . Option B — named profile daily driver Better: omit profile from code, set AWS_PROFILE=study in shell — same code works for everyone. Option C — SSO team standard, no long-lived keys Option D — assume role dev → prod separation You log in as dev, Terraform elevates to a role in target account: Blast-radius win: your user has zero direct permissions; every action is logged as the role. Option E — OIDC in CI no stored secrets at all GitH"},{"anchor":"1-minimal-setup-study-lab-2-files","text":"ub Actions mints a short token, AWS trusts it: 3. Regions + multi-region alias pattern Rules: every resource uses default unless provider = aws.eu . Modules accept providers via providers = { aws = aws.eu } later topic . 4. Useful provider-level settings with examples 5. Verify it's working before writing real infra 6. Errors you'll hit symptom → fix Error Cause Fix --- --- --- No valid credential sources found nothing exported, no profile aws sts get-caller-identity first; set AWS_PROFILE or env vars ExpiredToken / SSO expired temporary creds timed out aws sso login --profile X , re-run AccessDenied ... AssumeRole user can't take the role check trust policy + external_id , test with aws sts assume-role wrong account surprise keys point at prod, not lab add allowed_account_ids , verify get-caller-identity before apply keys in state/git hardcoded in provider block remove, rotate the key NOW, use env/profile/SSO region required no region anywhere set in block or AWS_REGION BucketAlreadyExists name taken globally rename not an auth bug, but you'll see it right after setup 7. Security rules non-negotiable 1. Never access_key in .tf — state files + git history keep secrets forever. 2. ."},{"anchor":"2-how-credentials-reach-the-provider-priority-order","text":"gitignore must cover .tfstate , .terraform/ , .tfvars with real keys. 3. Prefer SSO/roles/OIDC over long-lived keys; rotate what remains. 4. allowed_account_ids in every provider block prod-safety seatbelt . 5. Least privilege: lab user gets scoped policy, CI role gets per-repo trust — never AdministratorAccess for study. 8. Interview Q&A - Where do AWS creds for Terraform come from? Env → shared file/profile → SSO/role/instance-profile/OIDC; never hardcoded. - What does allowed_account_ids do? Fails fast if creds point at the wrong account. - Default vs aliased provider? Default serves all resources; alias adds a second region/account selected per-resource with provider = aws.eu . - How does CI auth without keys? OIDC: cloud trusts the pipeline's short-lived token, assumes a role. - Keys leaked to git — now what? Remove from code, rotate/revoke immediately, purge history, move to SSO/env. --- Next: S3 remote backend + locking your state leaves your laptop , or Variables/Outputs deep-dive."}]},"aws-18-ec2-security-groups-lab-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"ec2-security-groups-lab-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\13-ec2-security-groups-lab.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate type=hands-on lab /} EC2 + Security Groups Lab — Complete Guide One-line: launch a real VM with Terraform, fence it with a security group, prove it works with curl — first against Floci free , then the identical code against AWS. 1. Mental model - AMI = frozen OS image Ubuntu 22.04 . Instance type = size t3.micro = 2 vCPU, 1 GB — free-tier . - Security group = firewall attached to the instance stateful: return traffic auto-allowed . NACL = firewall on the subnet stateless, both directions explicit — SGs first, NACLs later. - user_data = bootstrap script run once at first boot install nginx, write page . 2. Minimal example Floci first 3. Security group rules that matter Block Fields Notes --- --- --- ingress from/to port, protocol, cidr/Ipv6/sg-source one per opening; description mandatory in teams audit \"why is 22 open?\" egress same shape default SG allows all outbound — keep it for updates unless compliance says otherwise protocol \"-1\" all protocols/ports egress shortcut; never for ingress "},{"anchor":"1-mental-model","text":"from 0.0.0.0/0 source = another SG security_groups = aws_security_group.alb.id ALB→EC2 pattern: instances accept 80 ONLY from the load balancer, world never touches them SSH rule for real life: cidr_blocks = \"203.0.113.7/32\" your IP , never 0.0.0.0/0 . Better: no SSH at all — SSM Session Manager AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore role, no open port . 4. Same code on real AWS the swap 1. Delete the endpoints { } block + the three skip_ lines. 2. Auth via profile/SSO export AWS_PROFILE=study , verify aws sts get-caller-identity . 3. terraform init -reconfigure && terraform apply — identical plan shape, real VM, real bill ~$0.01/hr micro — destroy after . 5. Day-2 ops checklist - ✅ data.aws_ami with most_recent never hardcode AMI IDs — they rot per region . - ✅ prevent_destroy on anything holding data; instances themselves stay replaceable immutable pattern, see IaC /courses/terraform/level1/iac/ . - ✅ Separate SGs per tier alb-sg → web-sg → db-sg , chained by SG reference, not CIDR. - ✅ Flow Logs on + SSM instead of SSH. - ✅ EBS encrypted = true , delete_on_termination understood root dies with VM by default; data volumes persist . 6. Errors you'll hit Error Cause Fix --- --- --- InvalidAM"},{"anchor":"2-minimal-example-floci-first","text":"IID.NotFound hardcoded AMI from another region use data.aws_ami per region InvalidGroup.NotFound / SG in wrong VPC SG references another VPC's group same vpc_id everywhere timeout waiting for SSH / user_data didn't run no IGW route / no public IP / SG blocks 22 public subnet + map_public_ip + SG ingress + check /var/log/cloud-init-output.log curl hangs SG blocks 80 or nginx failed SG ingress 80 + systemctl status nginx via SSM InsufficientInstanceCapacity AZ out of micros another AZ or type Floci: instance \"runs\" but no real SSH emulation — EC2 is container-backed verify via state/outputs, save real-SSH practice for AWS 7. Interview Q&A - SG vs NACL? SG = stateful, instance-level, allow-only. NACL = stateless, subnet-level, allow+deny with numbered rules. - Why SG-reference instead of CIDR between tiers? Survives IP changes; least-privilege by identity, not address. - Why data.aws_ami over hardcoded ID? IDs differ per region and rot; data source always resolves fresh. - user_data vs provisioner? user_data = native cloud-init bootstrap visible, retryable ; provisioners = last resort see Terraform vs Ansible /courses/terraform/level1/vs-ansible/ . - How kill SSH entirely? SSM Session"},{"anchor":"3-security-group-rules-that-matter","text":" Manager + no 22 ingress + instance role. --- Next: ALB in front target groups, listeners, health checks , or variables/outputs/modules deep-dive."}]},"aws-19-how-terraform-works-core-vs-plugins-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"how-terraform-works-core-vs-plugins-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\06-how-terraform-works-core-plugins.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner→Intermediate /} How Terraform Works: Core vs Plugins — Complete Guide One-line: Core reads your code and builds a graph; plugins do the dirty work talking to AWS/Azure/etc. Core is the brain, providers are the hands. 1. The 30-second picture - Core one binary, terraform : parses HCL, builds dependency graph, plans diff, calls plugins, saves state. - Plugins separate binaries: providers + provisioners : each knows ONE API family hashicorp/aws , hashicorp/local , random , etc. . Downloaded by init into .terraform/ . - Communication: Core ↔ plugins over gRPC local RPC . You never see it; TF_LOG=DEBUG does. 2. The full run, step by step with real commands Step 1 — init plugin install - Reads required_version + required_providers in versions.tf . - Downloads exact provider binaries + hashes into .terraform/ + writes .terraform.lock.hcl . - No cloud calls. Think: npm install / pip install for infra. Step 2 — validate + fmt core only Step 3 — plan core + plugins read 1. Core parses all .tf → d"},{"anchor":"1-the-30-second-picture","text":"esired state. 2. Core loads terraform.tfstate → known state. 3. Core calls each provider's Read/Refresh : \"does this ID still exist? what are its live attrs?\" 4. Core diffs desired vs known vs real → +/~/− plan. No writes. Step 4 — apply core orders, plugins write 1. Core builds dependency graph edges from resource.A.x references . 2. Independent nodes run parallel default 10, -parallelism=n . 3. For each change core calls provider Create/Update/Delete → waits → saves new attrs to state. 4. Writes terraform.tfstate + backup . Done. 3. Core deep-dive what the brain does Core job Example --- --- Parse HCL + variables + tfvars var.env → \"prod\" Build graph subnet → vpc , vm → subnet auto from references Order + parallelize VPC first, then 3 subnets concurrently Diff plan code says v2, state says v1 → ~ update Manage state + lock S3 + DynamoDB lock, one writer Manage workspaces/backends dev vs prod isolation Call plugins, never APIs directly core never holds AWS keys logic — provider does Graph example auto-ordered, you wrote no order : 3b. You don't write in order — the graph does Yes, exactly. Write resources in ANY order in the files — Terraform figures out the real creation order fr"},{"anchor":"2-the-full-run-step-by-step-with-real-commands","text":"om references , not file position. Proof — shuffled but identical result: Apply order is always: vpc → subnet → vm , because core builds edges from aws_X.y.attr references. File order is irrelevant — even across different .tf files in the folder. Two kinds of dependencies: Rule: use references 95% of the time; depends_on only when the dependency is invisible to Terraform external side effects, eventual-consistency APIs . And in the infra parallelism : independent branches run concurrently — 3 subnets/AZs or 50 VMs with no shared refs are created in parallel default 10 at a time, -parallelism=n , dependents wait. That's the \"speed\" from Why Terraform: correct order + max parallelism, automatic. When it breaks know the trap : - Hidden dep with no reference and no depends_on → race, flaky apply classic: IAM policy attach vs EC2 assuming the role — add depends_on . - Circular refs a needs b , b needs a → Error: Cycle: — split or use data source for one side. - Debug: terraform graph -type=plan dot -Tpng g.png , or TF_LOG=DEBUG to watch ordering. 4. Plugins deep-dive providers + provisioners Providers the hands — 99% of plugin talk Each provider = one plugin binary exposing resource typ"},{"anchor":"step-1-init-plugin-install","text":"es + data sources: - hashicorp/aws → aws_instance , aws_s3_bucket , data.aws_ami - hashicorp/local → local_file lab-friendly, no cloud - hashicorp/random → random_password caution: new value = diff Provider configuration keys/region is separate from resources: Resources vs Data sources critical distinction : Provisioners avoid — the escape hatch local-exec / remote-exec / file run scripts during create/destroy. Break idempotency, invisible to state, fail CI. Use cloud-init / user_data , Packer, or Ansible instead. Only legit: bootstrap that nothing else can do. 5. State's role in the machine tiny preview State = core's memory: { code address → real ID + attrs } . - No state → core can't plan would duplicate everything . - Corrupt/lost state → import or refresh surgery full topic later . 6. Debug like a pro 3 tools 7. Common failures core vs plugin — know whom to blame Symptom Culprit Fix --- --- --- Failed to query providers / checksum mismatch init/plugin download init -upgrade , check network/registry Missing required argument / type error core validation your code read error's address, fix type BucketAlreadyExists / InvalidAMI plugin → API rejection rename / fix value, provider "},{"anchor":"step-2-validate-fmt-core-only","text":"is messenger Plan always diffs timestamp , unpinned random your code defeats core diff remove timestamp, use keepers inconsistent result after apply provider bug pin/revert provider, retry, file issue 8. Interview Q&A - Core vs provider? Core parses/graphs/plans/states; providers talk to APIs via gRPC. - What does init do? Installs provider binaries + lock file; no infra changes. - Resource vs data source? Managed lifecycle vs read-only lookup. - How does Terraform order creates? Dependency graph from references + depends_on when hidden . - Where do credentials live? Provider config/env, never in code. - Why avoid provisioners? Non-idempotent, un-tracked, better: cloud-init/Packer/Ansible. --- Next: State deep-dive backends, locking, workspaces, import/mv ."}]},"aws-20-static-credentials-vs-env-vars-vs-aws-cli-config-complete-gu":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"static-credentials-vs-env-vars-vs-aws-cli-config-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\08-static-vs-env-vs-cli-config.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner Note: source example keys AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE, wJalrXUtnFEMI... are AWS documentation placeholders, not real credentials. /} Static Credentials vs Env Vars vs AWS CLI Config — Complete Guide Same goal — get an access key into the AWS provider — but three very different ways, with different safety, portability, and team-fit. Master all three before touching anything. 1. Mental model the one picture Rule of thumb: lab = env or profile, team = SSO, CI = OIDC, never hardcode. 2. Static credentials the dangerous one What it means: long-lived access key + secret you put somewhere Terraform can see . Even if you delete it later, state + git history still have it . The key is now a permanent liability; rotate it. When it's acceptable: zero. The \"it's just for the lab\" exception is what causes real breaches. If you must test a one-shot, use env vars and revoke the key the same day. Self-test — find any leaks in your repo before you do anything else 3. Env vars the local default AWS provider checks t"},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-the-one-picture","text":"he standard env vars first in this order : AWS_PROFILE → AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY → AWS_SESSION_TOKEN → AWS_REGION . Source wins, then fall-through. Linux / macOS bash, zsh Persist in ~/.bashrc / ~/.zshrc lab only or use a .env file excluded from git . Windows PowerShell Windows git-bash / MSYS Same export syntax as Linux — works in your terminal. Path translation rules still apply for native tools. Quick test from any shell Pros: zero files, instant revoke by unset , great for CI secrets and make /wrapper scripts. Cons: shell-only, easy to forget, no profile separation, no SSO/MFA built in. 4. AWS CLI config the human default Two files in ~/.aws/ , never in your repo: File Holds Format --- --- --- ~/.aws/credentials access keys per profile study \\naws_access_key_id=...\\naws_secret_access_key=... ~/.aws/config region, output, role assumption, SSO settings per-profile and default One-time setup the aws configure walkthrough This creates both files. Resulting files: Terraform picks it up Assume role via config no hardcoded arn in code Pros: profiles per env dev/staging/prod , clean separation, built-in role + SSO, IDE-friendly, one place to rotate. Cons: per-machine "},{"anchor":"2-static-credentials-the-dangerous-one","text":"setup won't travel to CI , still long-lived keys unless you combine with SSO/roles. 5. Side-by-side comparison use this in interviews Static in code Env vars AWS CLI config profiles --- --- --- --- Where keys live .tf state+git shell env ~/.aws/credentials Shareable yes, badly via CI secret store per-user only Per-env separation none edit code hand-set per shell one profile per env Rotate cost grep history export again edit one file Risk if laptop stolen keys in git history n/a shell chmod 600 the file Works in CI? no state+history yes best only if file mounted Works with SSO/MFA? no no yes config + token Best for nothing lab + CI secrets humans daily-driving Interview verdict \"never\" \"good for ad-hoc\" \"standard for teams\" 6. Choose fast the flowchart 7. Live demo 5 commands, no secrets leaked 8. Security rules memorize 1. Zero keys in code or git. Period. 2. .tfstate , .terraform/ , .tfvars in .gitignore before first commit. 3. chmod 600 ~/.aws/credentials CLI does this on most OSes; verify on Windows . 4. Rotate the moment a key hits a public place — aws iam delete-access-key + new one. 5. Prefer IAM roles + STS short-lived creds SSO / assume-role / instance profile over static k"},{"anchor":"self-test-find-any-leaks-in-your-repo-before-you-do-anything-else","text":"eys. 6. Add allowed_account_ids in the provider block as a final seatbelt. 9. Interview Q&A - Where does the AWS provider look for creds first? env → shared file → SSO/role/instance profile → OIDC; never hardcoded. - Static vs env vs config? Static = keys in .tf never ; env = shell vars ad-hoc, CI ; config = ~/.aws/credentials with profiles team daily driver . - Why no keys in code? Leaks to state + git history; rotation cost grows over time. - How to use a different account per workspace? Named profile + AWS_PROFILE per shell, or aliased providers with assume_role in code. - What if a key leaks to GitHub? Treat as compromised: rotate + delete, purge history, alert security, audit aws cloudtrail for misuse. - Best CI auth? OIDC — no stored secret, short-lived token assumed into a per-pipeline role. --- Next: S3 remote backend + DynamoDB lock so state leaves your laptop and the team is safe , or SSO setup walkthrough."}]},"aws-21-terraform-aws-vpc-resource-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-aws-vpc-resource-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\09-aws-vpc-resource.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate Note: source had two \" 11\" sections; second renumbered to 12 during migration. /} Terraform aws_vpc Resource — Complete Guide One-line: aws_vpc is the network boundary Terraform creates — a logically isolated virtual network in AWS where every other AWS resource subnets, instances, RDS lives. One per environment; everything else hangs off it. 1. Mental model the 3 pictures - Building: VPC = the plot of land. Subnets = buildings on that land. Route tables = roads. Internet/NAT gateway = gates. - CIDR: your private IP range, e.g. 10.0.0.0/16 = 65,536 IPs. AWS reserves 5 per subnet, so plan /16 for VPC, /24 per subnet. - Layer: every EC2/RDS/ALB must launch in a VPC. No VPC → no real AWS infra default VPC exists but never use it for new work . 2. Minimal example runnable 3. The 20 attributes that matter with examples + when to use Identity & CIDR always set Attribute Type What it does Example --- --- --- --- cidr_block string Primary IPv4 CIDR \"10.0.0.0/16\" ipv4_ipam_pool_id string Use AWS IPAM-manage"},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-the-3-pictures","text":"d pool large orgs \"ipam-pool-...\" ipv4_netmask_length int Netmask inside IPAM pool 24 instance_tenancy string default shared HW or dedicated single-tenant, $$$ \"default\" tags map Always tag: Name , Env , Owner Name = \"study-vpc\" IPv6 optional, dual-stack Attribute Notes --- --- assign_generated_ipv6_cidr_block = true AWS auto-assigns a /56 from Amazon's pool ipv6_cidr_block Set explicitly if not using generated ipv6_cidr_blocks IPv6 associations advanced DNS almost always on, default already on Attribute Default Notes --- --- --- enable_dns_support true VPC has DNS. Turn off only for isolated networks. enable_dns_hostnames false Public IPs get public DNS. Set true if you have an IGW + public subnets. Modern AWS niceties use when applicable Attribute Effect --- --- enable_network_address_usage_metrics CloudWatch metric for IPs used in pool — useful for IPAM planning enable_flow_log or use the aws_flow_log resource VPC traffic logs to CloudWatch/S3 — security baseline What's gone in AWS provider v5 - tags is now a top-level map replaces the Tags list on resources . Use tags = {} . 4. Real-world 3-tier VPC the pro pattern Production-shaped — but the registry module is what teams actua"},{"anchor":"2-minimal-example-runnable","text":"lly use: Option A: hand-rolled you see every piece Option B: the registry module 10 lines, battle-tested Rule: use the module in any real project. Hand-rolled only when learning or you need a non-standard layout. 5. CIDR math the part everyone trips on cidrsubnet prefix, newbits, netnum . Newbits = the difference 16→24 = 8 . Netnum = which /24 within the /16. AWS reserves 5 IPs per subnet network, +1, +2, +3, broadcast . So a /24 = 251 usable, a /28 = 11 usable. 6. Referencing the VPC the payoff VPC ID flows automatically via the dependency graph — no need to pass it by hand through every resource. 7. Day-2 ops: flow logs, peering, endpoints 8. Errors you'll hit and fixes Error Cause Fix --- --- --- The CIDR is invalid wrong format use 10.0.0.0/16 style, no host bits set cidr block conflicts with existing VPC overlapping CIDR in same account/region pick a non-overlapping range, or use IPAM You must specify a CIDR block forgot cidr_block required attribute address range in use subnet inside VPC overlaps with another recompute cidrsubnet netnums cannot delete vpc because it has dependencies subnets/IGW/etc. still exist destroy in reverse: endpoints → nat → igw → subnets → route-table"},{"anchor":"3-the-20-attributes-that-matter-with-examples-when-to-use","text":"s → sg → vpc Tenancy 'dedicated' unsupported in this AZ chosen AZ doesn't support dedicated HW switch back to default or pick different AZs 9. State after apply what AWS knows 10. How to recover a VPC deleted or orphaned First, the hard truth: AWS has no \"undelete VPC\". Once deleted and its dependencies released , the ID is gone forever. Recovery = recreate , and how painful that is depends on what you still have: What survived Recovery path Pain --- --- --- Code + state intact terraform apply recreates it new ID low — update references, re-attach Code intact, state lost recreate state via import , then apply medium Neither code nor state, VPC still exists import it into management medium Nothing, VPC gone rewrite code, apply fresh high — new IDs everywhere Case A — VPC deleted, code + state exist common lab accident Then fix everything that referenced the old ID: peering connections, RAM shares, VPN attachments, route53 zones, and any hardcoded IDs in other workspaces. This is why cross-VPC references should use data sources/variables, not pasted IDs. Case B — state lost, VPC still exists recover management Import maps the real VPC into state without recreating it. Repeat for subn"},{"anchor":"identity-amp-cidr-always-set","text":"ets/route tables if their states are also gone, or terraform refresh -driven plan will want to recreate them which fails — CIDRs already in use . Case C — nothing survived, VPC gone rebuild Rewrite from sections 2–4 above or the registry module , pick the same CIDR if peers/VPNs expect it, apply , then rebuild attachments in dependency order: subnets → route tables → IGW/NAT → endpoints → peerings → VPN. Prevention worth more than any recovery Plus: remote state with versioning S3 versioning ON — restore any past state in 1 click , allowed_account_ids , separate prod state, nightly plan -detailed-exitcode drift alarm, and SCPs denying ec2:DeleteVpc in prod accounts. Deletion protection on RDS/ALB behind it too. Interview Q&A recovery - Can you undelete a VPC? No — recreate; IDs are never reused. - State says VPC exists but AWS deleted it? Next plan shows + create tainted/missing → recreate . Fix code refs to the new ID after apply. - AWS has the VPC but Terraform forgot it? terraform import , then align config until plan is clean. - How do you make VPC deletion hard? prevent_destroy , S3-versioned state, SCP deny, separate prod state, drift alarms. 11. Multi-VPC / multi-account pat"},{"anchor":"ipv6-optional-dual-stack","text":"tern - VPC peering: simple 1:1 link, no transit routing, no edge routing across regions. - Transit Gateway: hub for many VPCs, accounts, even on-prem via VPN/DX. The right answer past 3-4 VPCs. - Shared VPC RAM : one account owns the VPC, others share subnets — common in orgs with a network account. - VPC Lattice / PrivateLink: service-to-service across VPCs without full peering. 12. Interview Q&A - What is a VPC? A logically isolated virtual network in AWS with its own CIDR, route table, and gateways. - What CIDR should I pick? Private RFC1918 range: 10.0.0.0/16 is the common study default. /16 leaves room for ~250 subnets. - What gets reserved per subnet? 5 IPs network, +1, +2, +3, broadcast . So /24 = 251 usable. - Public vs private subnet? Public = route to IGW + map_public_ip_on_launch=true ; private = no IGW route, may have NAT. - NAT gateway for what? Outbound internet for private subnets; you don't want inbound from the internet to private hosts. - Default VPC — should I use it? No — it has 172.31.0.0/16 , all-public subnets, no logging. Always create your own for real work. - VPC peering vs Transit Gateway? Peering = 1:1, free per hour, no transitive routing. TGW = hub, pa"},{"anchor":"dns-almost-always-on-default-already-on","text":"id, scales to many VPCs and on-prem. - Why pin the registry module version? ~ 5.1 and lock file = reproducible, no surprise changes. --- Next: state backend S3 + DynamoDB so your team shares this VPC work safely, or Subnets/Route Tables deep-dive."}]},"aws-22-terraform-subnets-route-tables-igw-nat-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-subnets-route-tables-igw-amp-nat-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\10-subnets-routes-igw-nat.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate /} Terraform Subnets, Route Tables, IGW & NAT — Complete Guide VPC is the plot of land. Subnets divide it into AZs; route tables decide who can talk to whom; the Internet Gateway opens the public door; the NAT Gateway lets private subnets sneak out. 1. Mental model one picture Rule: 1 IGW per VPC , 1 NAT per AZ for HA or 1 shared for dev to save $ . 2. The 4 resources you'll touch every day 3. aws_subnet — the attributes that matter Attribute What it does Example --- --- --- vpc_id required, which VPC aws_vpc.main.id cidr_block required, /24 inside the VPC CIDR \"10.0.1.0/24\" availability_zone required, pick data.aws_availability_zones not hardcoded \"us-east-1a\" map_public_ip_on_launch auto-assign public IP to instances in public subnets true for public, omit/false for private tags always tag Name , Tier Name = \"public-us-east-1a\" Hidden gotcha: AWS reserves 5 IPs per subnet network, VPC+1 router, DNS, future, broadcast . A /24 gives 251 usable. 4. aws_internet_gateway IGW — the public door 1 "},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-one-picture","text":"per VPC , free, no attributes besides vpc_id and tags. No CIDR, no AZ — it's VPC-wide. A subnet is \"public\" only when its route table points 0.0.0.0/0 → igw . The map_public_ip flag is just a launch-time convenience. 5. aws_route_table + aws_route + aws_route_table_association - Route table = list of rules, belongs to a VPC, has many routes destination → target . - Association = link a subnet to a route table. Each subnet has exactly one route table. - Main route table = auto-created with every VPC; any subnet not explicitly associated uses it. Two syntaxes for aws_route : 6. NAT — the private subnet's \"outbound only\" door NAT Gateway = managed, AZ-bound, ~$32/mo + data. Lives in a public subnet ; private subnets route through it. Rule: 1 NAT per AZ for HA , or 1 shared NAT in dev to save cost. For prod HA 1 NAT per AZ → AZ failure doesn't take the whole region down : 7. Real-world 3-tier, all hand-rolled copy/run 8. Or use the registry module 10 lines vs 200 9. VPC Endpoints — free/private way to reach AWS services Skip NAT for S3/DynamoDB with gateway endpoints free : Interface endpoints cost ~$0.01/hr each for ECR, Secrets Manager, etc. 10. Errors you'll hit and fixes Error Caus"},{"anchor":"2-the-4-resources-you-ll-touch-every-day","text":"e Fix --- --- --- subnet CIDR overlaps with another math mistake use cidrsubnet with non-overlapping netnums value for 'cidr_block' is not a valid IPv4 CIDR host bits set in CIDR write 10.0.1.0/24 not 10.0.1.5/24 NoInternetForPrivateSubnet forgot NAT route add 0.0.0.0/0 → nat_gateway_id to private RT nat gateway must be in a public subnet NAT placed in private put it in a public subnet with IGW route cannot delete subnet: has dependencies ENIs, instances, RDS still attached destroy in reverse: rds → ec2 → nat → route-tables → subnet subnets must be in distinct AZs two subnets in same AZ for ALB spread subnets across 2+ AZs eip is already associated EIP attached to another resource disassociate, retry, or use a fresh EIP 11. Day-2 ops checklist - ✅ Flow logs on the VPC aws_flow_log resource → CloudWatch or S3 . - ✅ One NAT per AZ in prod HA , one in dev cost . - ✅ Tag every subnet with Tier public/private/db and AZ. - ✅ DB route table: NO default route. Ever. RDS shouldn't be talking to the internet. - ✅ Add Gateway VPC endpoints for S3 + DynamoDB → private traffic, no NAT charges. - ✅ aws_subnet × AZ count ≥ 2 for ALB/EKS; ≥ 3 for production RDS multi-AZ. 12. Interview Q&A - Public"},{"anchor":"3-aws-subnet-the-attributes-that-matter","text":" vs private subnet? Route table is the difference. Public = 0.0.0.0/0 → igw ; private = no IGW route, may have NAT for outbound. - What reserved in every subnet? 5 IPs network, +1, +2, +3, broadcast . /24 = 251 usable. - NAT Gateway vs NAT Instance? NAT GW = AWS-managed, AZ-bound, scales, ~$32/mo+data. NAT Instance = EC2 you manage legacy, don't use . - How make subnets HA across AZs? 2+ subnets per tier, one per AZ, route tables and NAT per AZ. - What is a route table association? The link between a subnet and a route table. Every subnet has exactly one. - Why a separate DB route table? Hard guarantee: DB subnets never get an internet route, even by accident. - S3 endpoint saves money how? S3 traffic stays inside AWS backbone, no NAT data processing charges. --- Next: EC2 instance + security groups, or EKS module walkthrough."}]},"aws-23-floci-local-aws-emulator-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"floci-local-aws-emulator-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\11-floci-local-aws-emulator.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner→Intermediate Note: source had two \" 8\" sections; second renumbered to 9 during migration. /} Floci — Local AWS Emulator — Complete Guide One-line: Floci is a free, open-source LocalStack alternative — a local AWS emulator in Docker. Point Terraform, the AWS CLI, or any SDK at http://localhost:4566 and practice real AWS workflows with zero account, zero cost, zero credentials to leak . - Repo: floci-io/floci MIT, ~23k stars — image floci/floci:latest , port 4566 - Family: floci-az Azure, :4577 , floci-gcp GCP, :4588 , floci-oci OCI, :4599 - Console: floci-ui AWS-Console-style UI · CLI: floci-cli floci start , floci env , floci doctor - Docs: floci.io · Community: Slack + GitHub Discussions 1. Why it exists the LocalStack story In 2026 LocalStack's Community edition was sunset — auth tokens for basic use, frozen security updates. Floci fills that gap: every emulated service free for everyone, forever, no tokens, no telemetry, no feature gates. Built with Quarkus Native, ~24ms startup, ~13 MiB idl"},{"anchor":"1-why-it-exists-the-localstack-story","text":"e. Why it matters for YOUR study: every guide so far provider setup, VPC, subnets can run against Floci locally — no AWS bill, no leaked keys, terraform destroy is instant. 2. What it emulates and how faithfully Application, data, eventing, identity, infra, billing services — S3, DynamoDB, SQS, SNS, IAM, STS, KMS, SSM, Secrets Manager, Step Functions, CloudFormation, EC2, ECS, EKS, Lambda, RDS, ElastiCache, MSK, Neptune, OpenSearch, CodeBuild and more. Full list: repo docs → services overview. Fidelity rule: real Docker where it matters — Lambda, RDS, Neptune, ElastiCache, MSK, ECS, EC2, EKS, OpenSearch, CodeBuild, Managed Flink run real container-backed execution, not shallow mocks. Validated by compatibility-tests/ suites: sdk-test-java/node/python/go/awscli + compat-terraform Terraform v1.10+ , compat-opentofu , compat-cdk . 3. Quick start 2 ways A — CLI fastest Any region works. Credentials can be any non-empty values. B — Docker Compose Migrating from LocalStack? Swap the image name, keep everything else. Old image hectorvent/floci no longer updates — use floci/floci:latest . 4. Terraform + Floci the important part Standard hashicorp/aws provider, endpoints overridden per serv"},{"anchor":"2-what-it-emulates-and-how-faithfully","text":"ice. From the official docs/getting-started/terraform.md : Key rule: every AWS service in your config needs its own endpoints { } line, or the provider talks to real AWS for that service. Full automated example: compatibility-tests/compat-terraform in the repo. 5. Emulated S3 backend + DynamoDB locking full local workflow 6. Floci vs LocalStack 30 seconds Floci LocalStack post-2026 --- --- --- License/cost MIT, all services free forever Community sunset; tokens + paid gates Startup/footprint ~24ms, ~13 MiB idle heavier JVM/Python Endpoint localhost:4566 same — drop-in localhost:4566 Migration swap image, keep going — Heavy services real Docker-backed real Docker-backed paid tiers for some Multi-cloud az/gcp/oci siblings, one port each AWS-focused 7. Floci CLI shortcuts verified against official README The CLI manages lifecycle, not resources — it starts/stops the emulator; aws /Terraform do the rest. Bare floci X = AWS default product ; others need the prefix. Daily 5 memorize Full lifecycle + debug Multi-cloud same verbs, prefixed Install pick one + upkeep CI pattern copy-paste Global flags all commands --endpoint default http://localhost:4566 , env FLOCI_ENDPOINT , --container de"},{"anchor":"3-quick-start-2-ways","text":"fault floci , -o text json yaml , -q , -v , --no-color , --profile ~/.floci/profiles/ .yaml . Port auto-detection: status/version/wait/env derive the endpoint from the container's port mapping. 8. Gotchas study-saver list 1. Missing endpoint line = real AWS call. If plan hangs on credentials or creates real resources, check endpoints { } . 2. Multi-container setups: set FLOCI_HOSTNAME=floci service name so returned URLs e.g. SQS QueueUrl resolve from your app container instead of localhost . 3. Credentials: any non-empty values work — unless you enable stricter per-service auth FLOCI_SERVICES_S3_ENFORCE_AUTH=true rejects unknown signed S3 keys . 4. Persistence: mount ./data:/app/data or state vanishes on restart. 5. Not everything is emulated — check the services overview before assuming VPC/EC2 basics yes, exotic features maybe not . 9. Interview Q&A - What is Floci? Free MIT local AWS emulator LocalStack alternative , Docker, port 4566. - How does Terraform use it? Standard AWS provider with per-service endpoints overrides + dummy creds + validation skips. - Why local emulation for IaC study? Zero cost, instant destroy, safe credential practice, CI-friendly compat-terraform suite"},{"anchor":"a-cli-fastest","text":" proves it . - Daily CLI? floci start → eval \"$ floci env \" → work → floci stop . Debug: doctor , status , logs ; CI: wait --timeout 60s ; reset labs: snapshot save/load . - Floci vs LocalStack? Drop-in same-port replacement; Floci fully free/MIT after Community sunset. - How does state work locally? Default local backend, or emulated S3 + DynamoDB lock table. - What's the multi-cloud story? Sibling emulators az/gcp/oci on ports 4577/4588/4599, plus floci-ui console and floci-cli . --- Next: EC2 + security groups lab run it against Floci first, AWS second , or floci-ui console tour."}]},"aws-24-terraform-state-backend-s3-dynamodb-locking-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-state-backend-s3-dynamodb-locking-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\12-state-backend-s3-dynamodb.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate /} Terraform State Backend: S3 + DynamoDB Locking — Complete Guide One-line: get terraform.tfstate off your laptop into versioned S3 with DynamoDB locking — so the team shares one truth, concurrent applies can't corrupt it, and any past state is one click away. 1. Why the 3 pains of local state Local state pain Backend fix --- --- Teammate applies from their laptop → your state is stale → resources duplicated or orphaned One shared state in S3, everyone reads/writes the same object Two apply at once → corrupt JSON, half-written resources DynamoDB lock: second apply waits or fails fast with Error acquiring state lock Laptop dies / file deleted → management history gone VPC still exists, Terraform forgot it — see VPC recovery /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ S3 versioning: restore any past state in 1 click 1b. State-file risks why the file itself is dangerous The state file is Terraform's memory — and it remembers everything , including things you'd never commit: Risk What happens Defense --- "},{"anchor":"1-why-the-3-pains-of-local-state","text":"--- --- Secrets in plaintext DB passwords, keys, tokens land in JSON password = \"P@ss...\" — anyone reading state owns your infra encrypt bucket SSE , tight IAM, never git-commit, prefer Vault/SSM data sources over inline secrets No lock → corruption two writes interleave → invalid JSON or half-recorded resources → next plan wants to recreate the world DynamoDB locking §3 ; never force-unlock while someone applies Stale state → wrong plan state says v1, reality is v3 manual clicks → plan proposes reverting legit changes or duplicating refresh -first workflow, drift alarms, no console clicks State loss = orphaned infra file gone → Terraform plans + create for things that exist → apply fails CIDR in use or duplicates billable resources S3 versioning + replication; import to re-adopt survivors VPC recovery /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ Blast radius one shared state for dev+prod → dev apply deletes prod separate key/bucket/account per env §4 State drift between backends two folders pointing at one key, or -reconfigure to the wrong bucket → teams overwrite each other one key per workspace, terraform init guard in CI verifying backend config Large state = slow plans 1000s of resources → "},{"anchor":"1b-state-file-risks-why-the-file-itself-is-dangerous","text":"every plan refreshes all → minutes per run, API throttling split states per service/env -target is a band-aid, splitting is the cure Memory hook: state is a password vault + map + diary in one file — encrypt it, lock it, version it, split it. 2. Minimum runnable setup study lab Bootstrap trick pro pattern : keep a tiny bootstrap/ folder whose ONLY job is creating bucket + table with local state, then never touch it again. Everything else uses the backend. 3. How locking works 30 seconds 1. apply starts → writes lock item LockID = \"bucket/key\" to DynamoDB with conditional-write fails if already there . 2. Second apply tries the same write → DynamoDB rejects → Error: Error acquiring the state lock with the lock ID + who holds it. 3. First apply finishes → deletes the lock item → queue clears. Stuck lock crash mid-apply ? Verify nobody is applying, then: What if 2 people apply at the same time? With S3 + DynamoDB backend the right setup : the second apply is blocked, nothing breaks. B waits or exits — no corruption, no half-state. A finishes, lock releases, B re-runs against A's fresh state and plans only the remaining diff. In CI this is why concurrent pipeline runs queue instead of "},{"anchor":"2-minimum-runnable-setup-study-lab","text":"clobbering prod. With local state / no lock the dangerous setup : both read the same starting state, both write at the end — last writer wins, first writer's resources become orphans: - A's VM exists in AWS but not in B's overwritten state file → Terraform forgot it → you pay for a ghost. - Interleaved writes can produce invalid JSON → state unparseable → manual surgery pull , hand-edit, push , pray . - Worst case: A deletes what B just created B's plan was computed against pre-A reality . Full scenario walkthrough minute by minute, no lock Setup: you and Sara share a folder with local state. State v1 tracks 1 VM web . Both of you add one resource each and apply at ~10:00. What happens next the bill arrives quietly : - terraform plan → No changes state matches code from Sara's view — db isn't in code OR state, so it's invisible . - db runs forever, unbilled-to-anyone's-dashboard, until someone spots it in the console or Cost Explorer. - Cleanup requires console deletion by hand or import it first, then destroy — the civilized path : Same scenario WITH the lock boring, safe : Nobody loses work, nothing orphans, plans were each computed against fresh reality. The 3 rules: 1. Remote b"},{"anchor":"3-how-locking-works-30-seconds","text":"ackend + lock table before the second human exists — not after the first incident. 2. Never force-unlock unless you've confirmed the holder is dead ask in chat, check CI runs — unlocking a LIVE apply recreates the corruption you installed the lock to prevent. 3. Serialize prod applies in CI concurrency groups / queue so humans never race the pipeline either. When does the lock engage, false locks, and unlocking Lock engages on every state-writing operation — apply , destroy , and init -migrate-state . Read-only commands plan , validate , show , output , refresh-only plan do NOT lock. So: seeing a lock error on a pure plan means something else is writing or a stale lock — see below . \"Locked for no reason\" — the 4 real causes all are stale locks, never random : Cause How to recognize --- --- Crashed apply Ctrl+C, laptop died, OOM, network drop mid-write error shows old timestamp + a machine nobody is on CI job killed timeout, runner evicted while applying holder is a pipeline run ID that's already red/cancelled Two backends sharing one key/table lock reappears right after unlock — someone else's automation keeps writing Manual DynamoDB edit / clock skew rare; lock item's Created tim"},{"anchor":"what-if-2-people-apply-at-the-same-time","text":"e is in the future or garbage Unlock procedure in order, no skipping : After unlock, always plan first. If the crashed apply died mid-write, some resources exist in AWS but not in state or vice versa — plan reveals them; fix with targeted apply or import before the real run. What changes where when you lock/unlock, and how others see it There is no lock file — the lock is a single row in DynamoDB , and the state is a versioned object in S3 . Two different stores, two different things changing: force-unlock does exactly one thing: deletes that DynamoDB row. It touches no AWS resources and no S3 object — that's why step 4 above plan-first is mandatory: the row is gone but a half-written world may remain. How others know 3 channels, automatic : 1. Lock error text — Who/Operation/Created printed on their failed apply. Always keep Who identifiable user@host , CI run URL — anonymous locks waste everyone's time. 2. Fresh state on next run — every plan / apply pulls the latest S3 version first, so your finished work appears in their diff automatically. 3. S3 version history — every state write is a new version; aws s3api list-object-versions shows who changed what when — the audit trail fo"},{"anchor":"full-scenario-walkthrough-minute-by-minute-no-lock","text":"r \"why did prod change at 10:02\". Never hand-delete the lock row — always use the command Tempting: open DynamoDB console, delete the lock item, done in 10 seconds. Don't. The command exists for reasons the console bypasses: Console delete wrong force-unlock right --- --- No confirmation of WHAT you're deleting wrong table? wrong key? prod vs dev? Targets the exact lock ID from the error, asks for confirmation No check whether the holder is alive — deletes live locks as happily as stale ones Prints Who/Operation/Created first so you verify death before releasing No audit of who removed it just an IAM user in CloudTrail noise The unlock is tied to your CLI run; pair it with a chat message \"unlocking xxx, holder dead, CI red\" Easy to delete the whole TABLE instead of one row it's happened Can't touch the table, only the row Only exception: Terraform itself can't reach the table creds broken, table policy changed — then console delete is the last resort, and you announce it to the team first, then immediately plan to prove health. 4. Per-env isolation 3 patterns Rule: pattern 1 for study, pattern 2 for anything with real users. Workspaces are fine but invisible — terraform workspace s"},{"anchor":"when-does-the-lock-engage-false-locks-and-unlocking","text":"how before every apply. 5. Local practice with Floci zero AWS cost Same backend, emulated Floci /courses/terraform/level1/floci/ : Do the whole lab migrate, lock contention with two shells, force-unlock against Floci first. 6. Day-2 ops checklist - ✅ S3 versioning ON restore , encryption ON , public access blocked , MFA-delete on prod bucket. - ✅ DynamoDB table PAY_PER_REQUEST locks are tiny, bursts rare . - ✅ allowed_account_ids in provider AWS provider setup /courses/terraform/level1/aws-provider-setup/ so dev creds can't write prod state. - ✅ State never in git .gitignore : .tfstate ; lock file .terraform.lock.hcl IS in git. - ✅ terraform plan -detailed-exitcode nightly → exit 2 = drift alert. - ✅ Bucket policy: only deploy roles/users can read/write; no human browsing prod state it contains secrets . 7. Errors you'll hit Error Cause Fix --- --- --- Backend initialization required added backend block, didn't re-init terraform init -migrate-state or -reconfigure to skip migration prompt Error acquiring state lock concurrent apply or crashed holder confirm idle → force-unlock AccessDenied on s3:GetObject creds lack bucket rights check IAM + bucket policy + allowed_account_ids Dyna"},{"anchor":"what-changes-where-when-you-lock-unlock-and-how-others-see-it","text":"moDB table not found table name/region mismatch create it in the SAME region as the bucket state snapshot was created by newer version teammate on newer CLI align required_version , upgrade together Two envs share one key copy-paste backend block unique key per env pattern 1 or bucket per env pattern 2 7b. What terraform init actually does with an S3 backend init does two jobs , and the backend is the second one people forget: First init with backend block local state → S3 : Fresh clone no local state, teammate/CI machine : What init verifies and its errors : Init step Failure Meaning --- --- --- S3 auth + HeadBucket AccessDenied / NoSuchBucket creds wrong or bucket doesn't exist yet bootstrap first, §2 DynamoDB DescribeTable ResourceNotFoundException lock table missing in that region State migration prompt answered \"no\" accidentally local and remote diverge — re-run with -migrate-state and answer yes Memory hook: init = install tools providers + plug in memory S3 . No backend → it only does job 1. Init rules + can you init before the bucket exists? No — with a backend \"s3\" block, init FAILS if the bucket or lock table doesn't exist yet. Init performs HeadBucket / DescribeTable dur"},{"anchor":"never-hand-delete-the-lock-row-always-use-the-command","text":"ing setup; missing bucket → NoSuchBucket , and it stops before installing anything usable. Initialization rules in one list: 1. Backend resources must exist BEFORE first init bootstrap folder with local state creates them — §2 . 2. Backend block can't use variables or locals — only literals that's why per-env means separate key values or -backend-config files . 3. One backend per folder; changing it needs -migrate-state move or -reconfigure forget . 4. init is safe to re-run anytime — it never touches real infra, only plugins + backend wiring. 8. Interview Q&A - Why remote state? Share, lock, version/restore, enable CI. - S3 + DynamoDB roles? S3 stores the JSON snapshots versioned, encrypted ; DynamoDB holds the mutex conditional-write lock row . - Lock stuck — now what? Verify no active apply, terraform force-unlock . - How isolate dev/prod state? Separate key small or separate bucket/account prod-grade ; never one key for both. - State contains secrets — implications? Encrypt bucket, block public, restrict IAM, never git-commit. - Migrate local → remote? Add backend \"s3\" , terraform init -migrate-state , verify plan = No changes. --- Next: EC2 + security groups lab Floci first, A"},{"anchor":"4-per-env-isolation-3-patterns","text":"WS second , or variables/outputs/modules deep-dive."}]},"aws-25-verified-terraform-apply-against-floci-proof-file":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"verified-terraform-apply-against-floci-proof-file","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\14-floci-verified-apply.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 type=execution proof Note: source header claims COMPLETED 2026-09-06 via WSL2 workaround, while body sections still read NOT EXECUTED/PENDING. Both states preserved verbatim — verification status UNCONFIRMED during migration. Re-run steps below to confirm. /} Verified terraform apply against Floci — Proof File Status: COMPLETED 2026-09-06 — executed inside WSL2 via wsl -e bash -lc . Floci binary floci CLI still NOT installed Windows curl SSL blocked; brew unavailable ; Docker Desktop daemon off. Workaround: pulled floci/floci:latest inside WSL2 Docker running → container floci-aws Floci v2.0.1 native started healthy on 4566. terraform init/plan/apply/destroy executed v1.16.1 . main.tf typo flocil_verify vs floci_verify fixed via sed . Full details in source notes folder completed copy preserved in original vault — archived, never deleted per owner rule . What this file proves when executed fill-in blanks for the user to complete Errors you'll see if something is wrong verified shapes from workflow, provider, floci gui"},{"anchor":"what-this-file-proves-when-executed-fill-in-blanks-for-the-user-to-complete","text":"des Command fails with... Cause Fix --- --- --- init → AccessDenied / hangs Floci not running / endpoint wrong floci start ; verify provider.tf endpoints plan hangs forever endpoint points at non-responsive port / wrong endpoint line check provider.tf endpoints; floci status apply creates real AWS resources unexpected bill endpoint line missing / using real profile confirm endpoints + dummy creds; never mix real profile apply succeeds but aws s3 ls empty created in Floci but list command hits wrong endpoint / wrong region match endpoint URL to provider.tf ; use same AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test env destroy → No changes but bucket exists state lost / backend mis-configured / two different backend files terraform import back; fix backend block Actual verification state: NOT EXECUTED - floci binary: Not Found see install in Floci /courses/terraform/level1/floci/ CLI section . - Port 4566: unreachable URLError . - live-verify-floci/ folder: exists with provider.tf + main.tf . - Proof file this file : created, tracking blanks. - Action required: install Floci brew / curl ... sh / iwr ... , run steps 0–6 above, replace NOT EXECUTED with COMPLETED , and include the plan and apply output lines. "},{"anchor":"errors-you-ll-see-if-something-is-wrong-verified-shapes-from-workflow-provider-f","text":"Reference commands copy-paste, one per line, verified syntax from core, provider, floci, state guides --- Created: 2026-09-06 Verified against: provider, vpc, floci, core/plugins, state guides Execution: PENDING requires Floci installation"}]},"aws-26-modules-author-publish-consume-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"modules-author-publish-consume-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\15-modules.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate /} Modules — Author, Publish, Consume — Complete Guide One-line: a module = reusable, parameterized folder; consume via source / version ; publish on registry public or Git private . The registry VPC module in VPC /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ and the inventory bridge in Terraform vs Ansible /courses/terraform/level1/vs-ansible/ are live examples. 1. Consume what VPC/workflow guides already show Source types: registry terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws , Git branch/tag/commit via ?ref= , local folder ./modules/ec2 , private registry enterprise . Only registry versions enforce version = \"~ X.Y\" ; Git sources must pin ?ref= manually for immutability. 2. Author structure No provider block inside reusable modules passed by caller ; see provider inheritance below. 3. Call + outputs + variables full chain 4. Provider inheritance no hardcoded provider in module Consumer passes providers explicitly: Rule: reusable modules = no provider block; dedicated single-region module = document the provider clearly. 5. Module ver"},{"anchor":"1-consume-what-vpc-workflow-guides-already-show","text":"sioning + registry quick Private: source = \"github.com/org/repo//modules/ec2?ref=v1.2.0\" tag = immutable . Public registry: requires manifest.json + open source; version = \"~ X.Y\" enforced automatically. For this study: the VPC registry module terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws , version \"~ 5.1\" is used in VPC /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ ; everything else is hand-rolled. Full module-authoring = next step variables + outputs mastery → package → registry . --- Next: verified-apply proof fill blanks or variables+outputs deep practice."}]},"aws-27-terraform-floci-visual-architecture-card":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-floci-visual-architecture-card","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\16-visual-architecture-card.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 type=summary card Note: Obsidian callout fences + wikilinks converted to sections + hub links during migration. /} Terraform / Floci — Visual Architecture Card Architecture — study environment This track runs on Floci free, MIT, floci/floci — LocalStack replacement, port 4566 for labs, with the identical code deployable to real AWS provider setup /courses/terraform/level1/aws-provider-setup/ . State lives in versioned S3 + DynamoDB lock state backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ . All content verified against official docs; verified apply /courses/terraform/level1/verified-apply/ holds execution blanks. Live labs — run now live-verify-floci/ — Floci provider.tf + main.tf . Commands Floci /courses/terraform/level1/floci/ : floci start → eval \"$ floci env \" → terraform init → plan → apply . No AWS account, no bill, no leaked keys. Clean state: terraform destroy . Proof file: verified apply /courses/terraform/level1/verified-apply/ fill blanks when executed . 15 guides — visual index Topic Key deliverable"},{"anchor":"architecture-study-environment","text":" --- --- --- 01 Infrastructure as Code /courses/terraform/level1/iac/ IaC, idempotence 02 Core Workflow /courses/terraform/level1/workflow/ HCL, versions, 3-state model 03 Terraform vs CloudFormation /courses/terraform/level1/vs-cloudformation/ Multi-cloud vs AWS 04 Why Terraform /courses/terraform/level1/why-terraform/ Hiring + reasons 05 Terraform vs Ansible /courses/terraform/level1/vs-ansible/ Stack, not swap 06 Core vs Plugins /courses/terraform/level1/how-it-works/ Core vs plugins 07 AWS Provider Setup /courses/terraform/level1/aws-provider-setup/ Auth patterns 08 Credential Hygiene /courses/terraform/level1/config-hygiene/ Key hygiene 09 VPC /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ VPC + registry 10 Subnets & Routing /courses/terraform/level1/subnets/ Subnets/NAT/IGW 11 Floci /courses/terraform/level1/floci/ Floci CLI 12 State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ Remote state + lock 13 EC2 Lab /courses/terraform/level1/ec2-lab/ EC2/SG lab 14 Verified Apply /courses/terraform/level1/verified-apply/ Proof file 15 Modules /courses/terraform/level1/modules/ Module authoring Plugins / visual tools active source vault Theme: Minimal kepano — clean typography, paper-style heading"},{"anchor":"live-labs-run-now","text":"s, dark/light. Plugins: dataview live tables + progress list · style-settings fonts/spacing · better-export-pdf PDF any guide · obsidian-git version control . Diagram: mermaid study-flow chart in index + excalidraw -ready folder. Live data: Dataview query in index pulls file list + tags + modified dates automatically. State / security model Remote backend S3 + DynamoDB : remote state state backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ , versioned bucket, encryption, prevent_destroy , per-env isolation key per env , force-unlock procedure, never hand-delete DynamoDB row. Auth provider setup /courses/terraform/level1/aws-provider-setup/ : AWS_PROFILE env vars SSO roles/OIDC. Keys never in .tf or .tfvars . allowed_account_ids seatbelt. Floci practice Floci /courses/terraform/level1/floci/ : zero cost, identical endpoint config provider.tf swaps by deleting endpoints + skips , S3 backend + DynamoDB lock emulated state backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ §5 . --- The 3-state mental model summary card — from Core Workflow Desired your .tf → diff → Known terraform.tfstate → refresh API → Real/current AWS/live . Plan compares all three: desired ≠ known → + / ~ ; known ≠ rea"},{"anchor":"15-guides-visual-index","text":"l → drift. Re-run clean = No changes . Memory hook: code = where to go; state = where you've been; refresh = where you are. --- Missing / next-up from source - excalidraw diagram: add a .excalidraw file showing the 3-tier network public → private → DB with icons. Can be embedded in index or Subnets /courses/terraform/level1/subnets/ . - Full execution: fill blanks in verified apply /courses/terraform/level1/verified-apply/ once floci binary installed and container running."}]},"aws-28-providers-iaas-paas-saas-catalog":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"providers-iaas-paas-saas-catalog","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\19-providers-catalog.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner /} Providers — IaaS, PaaS, SaaS Catalog Day 1 · who Terraform talks to A provider is responsible for understanding API interactions and exposing resources. Terraform Core never touches a cloud directly — it plans the graph, and provider plugins make the API calls. If it has an API, somebody has written or can write a provider for it. 1. The catalog what lives where Layer Examples You manage --- --- --- IaaS AWS, Azure, GCP, Alibaba VPCs, VMs, disks, load balancers PaaS Heroku, Render Apps, dynos, managed DBs SaaS Cloudflare, Datadog, GitHub DNS zones, monitors, repos One apply can touch all three layers at once e.g. AWS VPC + Cloudflare DNS + GitHub repo — that multi-API run is the point of being platform-agnostic see Why Terraform /courses/terraform/level1/why-terraform/ . 2. Declaring a provider block + version pin 3. Using two instances of one provider Resources default to the un-aliased block; provider = aws.eu opts into the second. Forgetting the alias is the classic multi-region bug — both VPCs "},{"anchor":"1-the-catalog-what-lives-where","text":"land in the same region and peering fails on overlap. Remember - ✓ Providers = API plugins IaaS/PaaS/SaaS ; Core plans, providers execute. - ✓ Pin with ~ in required_providers ; configure in provider blocks; alias for multi-region/account. - ⚠ Gotcha: static credentials inside a provider block end up in git. Use env vars or CLI config see AWS Provider Setup /courses/terraform/level1/aws-provider-setup/ — the block should carry region and aliases, never secrets. - 🎓 Exam takeaway: \"Core vs provider responsibility?\" → Core reads config, manages state, builds the graph; providers do auth + API calls see Core vs Plugins /courses/terraform/level1/how-it-works/ ."}]},"aws-29-capstone-lab-full-build-chain-step-by-step":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"capstone-lab-full-build-chain-step-by-step","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\26-capstone-hands-on-chain.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Intermediate type=hands-on capstone lab /} Capstone Lab — Full Build Chain, Step by Step Day 2 · Hands-on lab emulator-ready, AWS-identical minus endpoints Everything in this course, in one file, in order: Provider → VPC → 2 Subnets → 2 Instances per Subnet → Security Group HTTPS → Outputs private IPs → Provisioner → Destroy. Run it on the emulator free; the AWS swap is deleting 4 lines. 1. The full main.tf 8 resources, one file 2. Run it two tabs 3. Tear it down the chain in reverse Reverse-dependency order, confirmation prompted, state emptied. The lab is only finished when destroy is clean — leftover emulator state is how \"works on my machine\" starts. Remember - ✓ Chain: provider → VPC → subnets → SG → instances → outputs → provisioner → destroy. - ✓ for_each keys a1/a2/b1/b2 give per-instance identity; startswith routes to the right subnet. - ⚠ Gotcha: the local-exec writes ips.txt on YOUR machine — re-running apply without changes does NOT re-run it creation-only . Delete the file + taint to regener"},{"anchor":"1-the-full-main-tf-8-resources-one-file","text":"ate honestly. - 🎓 Exam takeaway: narrate this chain cold — \"provider auth, VPC, two subnets across AZs, SG with 443 in and all out, four instances via for_each, map output of private IPs, local-exec inventory, destroy in reverse.\" That paragraph passes any \"walk me through a project\" question. Resources — registry.terraform.io · developer.hashicorp.com/terraform · github.com/hashicorp/terraform"}]},"aws-30-terraform-core-workflow-write-code-version-apply-same-env-ex":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"terraform-core-workflow-write-code-version-apply-same-env-expanded","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level1\\02-write-version-apply-same-env.md 2026-09-06 Course metadata: course=terraform level=1 difficulty=Beginner→Intermediate /} Terraform Core Workflow: Write Code → Version → Apply → Same Env Expanded 4 topics combined: write HCL, pin versions , apply safely, guarantee same env . 1. The loop memorize this Analogy: init = download ingredients, validate = read recipe for typos, plan = show photo of dish before cooking, apply = cook. 2. Write code — HCL anatomy with 4 examples Files by convention: versions.tf pins , variables.tf inputs , main.tf resources , outputs.tf exports . Terraform loads all .tf in folder as one program. Example A — blocks, arguments, references Example B — variables no hardcoding Example C — tfvars per env the pro pattern Same code, different inputs = dev ≠ prod without duplicating code. Example D — meta-arguments count / for_each / depends_on Rule: prefer for_each over count for servers removing index 0 with count renames all; for_each removes one key cleanly . 3. Version — 3 levels + lock file with real outputs Operator Means Example Verdict --- --- --- --- ~ 5.0 allow 5.x, bl"},{"anchor":"1-the-loop-memorize-this","text":"ock 6.0 5.1→5.9 ok, 6.0 no ✅ default = 1.6, = 5.0 alone no cap 6.0 surprise breaks ❌ avoid Lock file — what it looks like: Teammate/CI runs init → gets byte-identical provider. Commands: 4. Apply — what happens + plan anatomy apply in 5 steps: refresh API read → diff code vs state vs real → propose +/~/- → execute graph order, parallel → persist state. The three states the whole mental model Every plan/apply is a 3-way comparison. Learn these names — interviews ask them directly: How core uses them: 1. Refresh — read REAL via APIs, compare vs KNOWN. Different? That's drift . 2. Diff — compare DESIRED vs KNOWN + refreshed REAL : in code but not state → + ; in both but differ → ~ ; in state but gone from code → - ; identical everywhere → no-op. 3. Propose → execute graph order → persist new KNOWN. Worked example all three disagree : Plan shows ~ instance_type = \"t3.large\" - \"t3.small\" — REAL → DESIRED. Applying reverts the click . That's drift correction in one line. Memory hook: code says where to go, state remembers where you've been, refresh checks where you are. Sample plan output — learn to read it: Symbols: + create, ~ update in-place, - destroy, -/+ destroy+recreate e.g. AMI c"},{"anchor":"2-write-code-hcl-anatomy-with-4-examples","text":"hange forces new VM , 0 — STOP and read every - line. Destroy is forever unless the resource is recreatable + stateless . Classic disasters: renamed resource Terraform sees delete+create , removed for_each key, changed force-new attribute. - 1 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy with a -/+ symbol next to one resource = replacement, not a bug: one dies, its successor is born. Drill do once : add a file, change its content, delete it — run plan after each step and watch which column moves. In CI, plan -detailed-exitcode turns this into gates: exit 0 = clean, 1 = error, 2 = diff pending. Renamed the resource? You destroyed it Terraform's identity for a resource is its address aws_instance.web , not its attributes. Change the local name and Terraform sees two strangers: Same config, but a new VM new ID, new IP and the old one terminated. Rename safely with a moved block no downtime, history kept : Edit vs replace vs destroy the full case table You changed... Plan shows AWS reality Danger --- --- --- --- Tag, description, content of a file ~ update in-place same ID, attrs patched low Instance type most AMIs types ~ update stop/start same ID, brief downtime medium AMI, subnet, AZ, bucket n"},{"anchor":"example-a-blocks-arguments-references","text":"ame, VPC CIDR -/+ destroy then create NEW ID, old deleted high — data/IPs lost unless handled Local name only address - + + looks like replace NEW resource, old terminated high — use moved {} Removed block / dropped for_each key / lowered count - destroy gone highest — verify every line Nothing re-run No changes untouched none Rule of thumb: ~ = edit, -/+ or - + + = death+rebirth new ID — update DNS, SG refs, outputs , lone - = gone forever. Any destroy anywhere → read every line before typing yes. Safe apply pattern always in prod : Flags: 5. Same env — 5 guarantees + drift demo Formula: same commit + same lock + same tfvars + per-env state + zero clicks = same env. Guarantee How Break symptom --- --- --- Same commit git rev-parse --short HEAD in pipeline log \"works on my machine\" Same providers lock file committed plan differs on CI vs laptop Same inputs -var-file=envs/ .tfvars prod got dev sizes Isolated state folder/workspace/backend per env dev apply killed prod Zero clicks IAM deny + nightly drift plan plan always shows surprise diff Drift demo lab do this once, remember forever : 6. Files: commit vs ignore with starter .gitignore 7. Errors you'll hit symptom → fix Error Caus"},{"anchor":"example-b-variables-no-hardcoding","text":"e Fix --- --- --- command not found not installed install, terraform version Failed to query providers offline / bad constraint fix ~ , init -upgrade Inconsistent validation var fails validation pass correct -var already exists S3 globally-unique name taken rename bucket state lock crash / concurrent run confirm idle → force-unlock No changes surprise wrong folder/var-file pwd , check -var-file Missing required argument forgot required var add to tfvars provider produced inconsistent result provider bug / computed race apply again, pin provider 8. Practice labs 3, in order 1. Clean run: init→validate→plan→apply→apply second = No changes . Change v1→v2, observe ~ . 2. Vars: add env var + 2 tfvars, plan each, compare outputs. 3. for_each: create 3 team files, remove one key, plan — only 1 destroyed then try same with count and see the mess . 9. Interview Q&A - init vs plan vs apply? Download → preview → execute+save. - Why plan -out + apply file? Apply exactly what was reviewed. - How guarantee same env? Commit + lock + tfvars + per-env state + no clicks. - count vs for_each? for_each for named resources safe removal . - Second apply No changes? Idempotent, no diff. - Lock file? Pins"},{"anchor":"example-c-tfvars-per-env-the-pro-pattern","text":" exact provider hashes for all machines. --- Next: State deep-dive remote backends, locking, workspaces ."}]},"aws-31-level-2-terraform-workspaces-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-terraform-workspaces-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\04-terraform-workspaces.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate /} Level 2 — Terraform Workspaces — Complete Guide Companion to Core Workflow /courses/terraform/level1/workflow/ , State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ , and Multi-Env /courses/terraform/level2/multi-env/ this file expands that stub . One state backend, multiple isolated state files, one set of code. Practice in 30 seconds, decide if you want it for production. 1. Mental model 30 seconds Think of workspaces as named sub-folders inside one state backend . The backend config stays the same; the key state path automatically gets a per-workspace prefix. Same code, same backend, different state. The \"default\" workspace exists for free and is just env/terraform.tfstate with no prefix. 2. Full command reference verbatim from CLI + verified behavior Workspace name rules: letters, digits, - , _ . No spaces, no slashes. Special: default is reserved the implicit workspace present when none is selected . 3. terraform.workspace — the in-code selector Inside HCL, the active workspace name "},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-30-seconds","text":"is available as terraform.workspace . Three patterns to use it: Pattern A — sizing per workspace the textbook pattern Pattern B — default + per-env overrides with a map Pattern C — toggle a resource per workspace 4. State isolation proof the test that wins arguments The same code applies per-workspace without collision. State in S3: The dependency graph in each workspace only knows its own resources. Cross-workspace references require terraform_remote_state or module outputs — workspaces alone do NOT share state. 5. Pros and cons the \"should I use this?\" decision Workspaces Separate folders + separate backends Single -var-file --- --- --- --- Code duplication None — one folder High — copy-paste or repo per env None — one folder State isolation Yes one file per env Yes full blast-radius isolation No — same state for all envs DANGEROUS Per-env backends No — same bucket/key prefix Yes — different accounts/regions/buckets N/A CI integration TF_WORKSPACE=prod terraform apply cd envs/prod && terraform apply terraform apply -var-file=envs/prod.tfvars Visibility of \"which env\" Hidden — workspace show before every apply Visible — current dir = current env Visible — file in command = current"},{"anchor":"2-full-command-reference-verbatim-from-cli-verified-behavior","text":" env Module reuse Easy same folder Easy Easy Cross-env drift Impossible different state Impossible Possible one state, different vars Blast radius Medium one bucket, one IAM, all envs Small per-env bucket + IAM + SCPs Big shared state = one bad apply nukes all Per-env provider aliases Same provider — no aliasing Different provider blocks provider \"aws\" { alias = \"prod\" region = \"...\" } Same provider, different var Learning curve Low 3 commands Medium folder structure + backend per folder Lowest Locking DynamoDB table = one lock for all envs per key One lock table per env per state One lock for all Best for solo/small teams, single AWS account, \"I just want dev/staging/prod\" large/regulated orgs, per-account isolation, blast-radius throwaway experiments only The interview answer: \"Workspaces are fine for one-account dev/staging/prod. Production-grade means per-environment state separate keys or buckets , per-account IAM, and CI gated by TF_WORKSPACE env var so humans don't type it.\" 6. CI integration the right way to use workspaces The classic mistake: humans typing terraform workspace select prod from their laptop. Lock that out. Have CI set TF_WORKSPACE and use a single set of .tf"},{"anchor":"3-terraform-workspace-the-in-code-selector","text":"vars files per env. The matrix runs dev and staging plans in parallel; prod only runs after human-approved merge to main. No laptop workspace select prod ever needed. 7. Cross-workspace data sharing Workspaces do not share state. Two patterns to read another workspace's outputs: Pattern A — terraform_remote_state deprecated but still common Caveats: - The source workspace name is baked into the key path ; renaming workspaces doesn't change state paths. - The reading workspace needs IAM access to the bucket. - terraform_remote_state is marked legacy; the recommended path is module outputs Pattern B , but state-from-state is still common in enterprise setups. Pattern B — module output the modern path A module in workspace A exposes outputs; another workspace's module block reads them. Modules can be local ./modules/... or registry. State is local to each workspace; the module is a source-code artifact, not a state artifact. Each workspace provisions its own copy of the module with different inputs . No cross-workspace state; no remote_state needed; module is a reusable code pattern, not a runtime state lookup. 8. The \"default\" workspace gotcha 1 When you first terraform init with a b"},{"anchor":"pattern-a-sizing-per-workspace-the-textbook-pattern","text":"ackend, you're on the implicit workspace called default . State goes to the unprefixed key: Trap: if you terraform init and apply on a fresh project WITHOUT selecting a workspace, your resources land in default and look \"real\" in plan output, but they have no env tag, no per-env key, and will collide with your first terraform workspace new dev later. Always create a workspace before the first apply for a new env-tagged project. 9. Locking across workspaces gotcha 2 One DynamoDB lock table locks all states in the same bucket by default. Two workspaces cannot apply at the same time. For real concurrency, you need either: - A separate state bucket per workspace see State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ per-env patterns , or - Separate lock tables per workspace rare; over-engineered For most teams: one lock table is fine because humans shouldn't apply to multiple envs simultaneously anyway. CI pipelines serialize via the TF_WORKSPACE env var + concurrency groups in the pipeline. 10. Renaming and deletion gotchas gotcha 3 - Workspace delete removes the label from terraform workspace list only. The state file is untouched; S3 still has the object. To remove state: aws s3"},{"anchor":"pattern-b-default-per-env-overrides-with-a-map","text":" rm s3://bucket/env/dev/terraform.tfstate after terraform state list confirms you don't need it . - terraform workspace delete default fails — default is permanent. - Renaming a workspace changes the state path for any future apply . Existing state stays at the old key, new state at the new key. Plan accordingly; rarely useful. 11. Workspaces vs Terragrunt the next step Terragrunt separate tool, not core Terraform wraps multiple terraform apply calls with workspace-like isolation plus DRY configuration across envs. It is what you reach for when: - 5+ envs with shared config - Multi-region / multi-account - You want to inject remote_state config without copy-paste - You need to manage dependencies between stacks network → app → data For study 15-30 resources, 1-2 envs : pure workspaces + a few .tfvars files is enough. For production at scale: Terragrunt or a per-folder repo. 12. Common errors and fixes Error Cause Fix --- --- --- Workspace \"dev\" doesn't exist typo or never created terraform workspace new dev idempotent — errors if exists Switched to workspace \"dev\" but state still in default terraform init was on default; you need to migrate state see State Backend /courses/terrafor"},{"anchor":"pattern-c-toggle-a-resource-per-workspace","text":"m/level1/state-backend/ migration — but workspaces are per-key, so this is unusual Error acquiring state lock another apply in progress in any workspace sharing the lock confirm idle → terraform force-unlock current workspace not specified; please specify a workspace terraform.workspace used in a code block, but the user ran from CI without TF_WORKSPACE set TF_WORKSPACE=dev in the CI env or default to \"default\" Plan shows nothing in new workspace correct: each workspace has its own state; new workspace = empty run apply to create resources Resources from old env still appear in S3 you deleted the workspace, not the state aws s3 rm s3://bucket/env/ /terraform.tfstate after confirming you don't need it default workspace can't be deleted reserved create a new workspace terraform workspace new dev and migrate with init -migrate-state if needed 13. Interview Q&A - What is a Terraform workspace? A named label that scopes the state key under the same backend. One backend → many isolated state files. - Are workspaces the same as terraform.tfvars per env? No. tfvars changes inputs within ONE state. Workspaces create multiple state files. - What's the difference between terraform.workspace a"},{"anchor":"4-state-isolation-proof-the-test-that-wins-arguments","text":"nd var.env ? terraform.workspace is the in-code reference to the active label read-only ; var.env is your own variable you set per apply. They can mirror each other, but workspaces drive state isolation; variables don't. - Why might you NOT use workspaces? Multi-account blast radius, per-env lock tables, visibility of which env you're in. For per-account prod, prefer separate folders or repos with their own backends. - Can workspaces share resources? No — each workspace has its own state. Use module outputs or terraform_remote_state to pass values. - Where does workspace state go? Whichever backend you configured; the key gets a per-workspace prefix automatically. - What happens to state when you delete a workspace? Nothing on the backend. The state file stays in S3. Workspace delete is a label-level operation, not a state-level one. - TF_WORKSPACE env vs terraform.workspace ? TF_WORKSPACE is the CLI override to switch workspaces non-interactively CI . terraform.workspace is the HCL read-only accessor for the active name. --- Next: modules authoring write + publish + consume , or policy-as-code Checkov/OPA gates on workspaces , or Terragrunt intro when workspaces stop scaling ."}]},"aws-32-level-2-multi-env-multi-vpc-patterns-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-multi-env-multi-vpc-patterns-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\05-multi-env-multi-vpc.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Advanced /} Level 2 — Multi-Env + Multi-VPC Patterns — Complete Guide Companion to Workspaces /courses/terraform/level2/workspaces/ and Networking Deep-Dive /courses/terraform/level2/networking/ . How to run more than one environment AND more than one VPC per environment — the layout you'll see in real orgs. Five patterns, from smallest to largest, with the Terraform code, the trade-off, and when to choose each. 1. The shapes you actually want The \"more than one VPC\" requirement usually comes from: - Blast radius separate VPCs per data plane: shared services, apps, data - Network segmentation apps VPC can't reach data VPC except via TGW/peering - Account boundaries each AWS account gets its own VPCs - Multi-region one VPC per region per workload 2. Pattern A — 1 env, 1 VPC study default; your live-verify-floci Already covered in VPC /courses/terraform/level1/vpc/ and EC2 Lab /courses/terraform/level1/ec2-lab/ . Skip if you have it. 3. Pattern B — N envs, 1 VPC per env the classic dev/staging/prod Three layou"},{"anchor":"1-the-shapes-you-actually-want","text":"ts; pick by blast-radius needs. Layout B1 — workspaces 1 folder, 1 backend, many states Best for: solo, single AWS account, dev/staging/prod in one place. Layout B2 — separate folders 1 folder per env, separate backend per env Best for: teams, per-env lock, per-env IAM, CI matrixed dirs. Trade-off: zero code duplication, but three folders; a \"module\" makes this clean. Layout B3 — separate state buckets per env the production grade Best for: regulated/audit, per-account isolation, blast-radius zero. Dev apply cannot touch prod bucket different IAM . This is the pattern that survives audits. 4. Pattern C — N envs × M VPCs the real-world one Three concrete sub-patterns. Sub-pattern C1 — VPCs in the same folder, named by terraform.workspace + a vpc_role variable State: 9 keys in the same bucket env/ / /terraform.tfstate if you set it that way . Each apply touches only one VPC. Blast radius: one VPC. Caveat: only works if the same backend can hold per-stack state, and you're disciplined about the loop. Sub-pattern C2 — folders × folders the cleanest for big teams Each envs/ / /main.tf is a two-liner : This is what Terragrunt makes even cleaner DRY config across 50+ stacks , but the stru"},{"anchor":"2-pattern-a-1-env-1-vpc-study-default-your-live-verify-floci","text":"cture works in pure Terraform too. Sub-pattern C3 — one-account per env the most isolated Each account has its own IAM, its own state bucket different names , its own SCPs. CI assumes a role into the target account aws sts assume-role . Blast radius: one account. Cost: more setup, more accounts to manage, more SCP work. 5. Multi-VPC + Multi-Account the most common in real orgs What you want How --- --- Same VPC structure in dev/staging/prod Module + per-env tfvars Pattern B2 Different CIDRs per env cidr_matrix map Sub-pattern C1 Per-env state isolation Separate key B1/B2 or separate bucket B3 Per-account isolation Pattern C3 Per-role VPCs shared/data/apps Sub-pattern C1 or C2 VPC peering between envs Don't. Use TGW if you must. VPC peering between roles in the SAME env TGW or per-VPC peerings rare Multi-region One VPC per region per role; provider alias Multi-region multi-VPC example sketch — full code below in §6 : 6. Multi-VPC layout for an environment the 3-VPC pattern, end to end This is the most common real shape: shared + data + apps per environment. Full Terraform with provider, peering, and route tables. This is 90% of what a real multi-VPC env looks like . Add Transit Gate"},{"anchor":"3-pattern-b-n-envs-1-vpc-per-env-the-classic-dev-staging-prod","text":"way when peerings grow past 3-4 TGW = hub-and-spoke; pay per attachment . 7. Module approach the right way to scale the 3-VPC pattern Local module = vpc-with-peering/ that takes a CIDR + role + list of peer-CIDRs and outputs the VPC, subnets, peering, and routes. Each env just passes variables. Nine lines for the full pattern, no copy-paste. When you change the module, every env re-applies the change Terraform tracks per-state, so no cross-env pollution . 8. Per-env + per-VPC state layout the production version State bucket tfstate-prod separate per env with keys: Each stack is one terraform init + one apply . A bad change in eks/ can only destroy EKS; it can't touch the data/ VPC or the state file of any other stack. For dev/staging, one bucket is fine; the structure is identical. The blast-radius win comes from separating the prod bucket different account, different IAM . 9. The \"more than one VPC\" gotchas the part interviews test Gotcha Symptom Fix --- --- --- CIDR overlap peering fails, secondary CIDR add fails per-env matrix; document the non-overlap; automate the check PR-time Transitive routing \"apps → shared → data\" doesn't work; peerings are 1:1 add explicit aws_vpc_peerin"},{"anchor":"layout-b1-workspaces-1-folder-1-backend-many-states","text":"g_connection for each pair, OR use TGW DNS over peering apps VPC can't resolve shared VPC private DNS aws_vpc_peering_connection_options { allow_remote_vpc_dns_resolution = true } see networking §10 Duplicate IGW/Route table names confused logs tag with Name = \"${env}-${role}-igw\" Cost of cross-VPC traffic $$ at scale use endpoint services or co-locate in same AZ; TGW data charges apply NACL mistakes traffic drops mid-flow stateless add ephemeral-port rule see networking §6 State in default workspace \"where did this VPC come from?\" always terraform workspace new dev before first apply SGs not cross-VPC SG referencing another SG across a peering fails reference by CIDR across VPCs not SG , or use shared services via Transit Gateway 10. Decision flowchart pick the right pattern 11. Interview Q&A - How do you run multiple environments with Terraform? Two main approaches: workspaces one folder, many state files under one backend or separate folders one project per env, separate backends . Workspaces are simpler; folders give better visibility and per-env blast-radius. - What is the most common multi-VPC pattern in a real org? 3 VPCs per environment: shared NAT, ALB , data RDS, cache , "},{"anchor":"layout-b2-separate-folders-1-folder-per-env-separate-backend-per-env","text":"apps compute . Peered together, isolated at the SG level. - How do you prevent CIDR overlap across envs? Plan a CIDR matrix up front e.g., dev = 10.0.0.0/16, staging = 10.10.0.0/16, prod = 10.20.0.0/16 and document it; add a check in CI. - VPC peering vs Transit Gateway? Peering is 1:1, free per hour, no transitive routing. TGW is hub-and-spoke, paid per attachment, supports transitive routing, easier to scale. Use TGW past 3-4 VPCs. - How do you deploy to prod safely? Never from a laptop. CI pipeline gated by environment approval; TF_WORKSPACE=prod set by the pipeline; manual approval in the repo's environment settings. - Why separate AWS accounts per env? Blast-radius, per-env IAM, per-env billing, SCPs service control policies . The cost of one more account is small compared to the cost of a leaked dev key in prod. - What is the difference between workspaces and modules? Workspaces scope state files. Modules scope code reusable blocks of HCL . You can use both together. - How do you read outputs from another env's state? data \"terraform_remote_state\" legacy but works or, better, an exposed module output consumed via the registry or a local module. --- Next: policy-as-code Checko"},{"anchor":"layout-b3-separate-state-buckets-per-env-the-production-grade","text":"v/Sentinel/OPA gates against the per-env state , or Terragrunt intro DRY config when N envs × M VPCs becomes unmanageable in pure Terraform ."}]},"aws-33-level-2-terraform-count-and-for-each-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-terraform-count-and-for-each-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\06-count-and-for-each.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate /} Level 2 — Terraform count and for_each — Complete Guide Companion to Core Workflow /courses/terraform/level1/workflow/ §2 quick example , Subnets /courses/terraform/level1/subnets/ cidrsubnet , and Multi-Env Multi-VPC /courses/terraform/level2/multi-env-multi-vpc/ . Both meta-arguments copy a resource N times. They look similar but have opposite strengths. This file teaches you the difference in 5 minutes, the gotchas, the math, and the patterns you'll actually use. 1. count — index-based copies count duplicates a resource once per integer in 0..N-1 . Each copy is accessed via count.index 0, 1, 2, ... . Result: aws_instance.web 0 , aws_instance.web 1 , aws_instance.web 2 . The brackets are real — Terraform uses array indexing. 2. for_each — key-based copies sets or maps for_each duplicates a resource once per key in a set or map . Each copy is accessed via each.key the key and each.value the value, which is the value for maps or the key again for sets . 3. The critical difference why most team"},{"anchor":"1-count-index-based-copies","text":"s get burned The difference shows up when you remove an item. But: if you had tags = { Name = \"web-0\" } and the first instance was the meaningful one, the first instance now 0 is still alive — the resource IS the right one. In most cases this is fine , but the moment your downstream code references aws_instance.web 2 which is now gone , Terraform rewrites the reference or errors. Now the failure mode: Stable keys = stable resource identity. That's the headline. 4. The rename trap live demonstration of the difference Same code, two different for_each values. Resources are matched by key, so the other two are untouched. The interview rule: prefer for_each for anything with a meaningful identity servers by name, subnets by AZ, peers by id . Use count only for \"I want N identical copies with no name\" or \"0/1 toggle.\" 5. Quick reference which one when? Use case Meta-argument Why --- --- --- \"Make 3 identical test VMs\" count = 3 no meaningful name; no per-instance difference \"Create a server per team member\" for_each = { ahmed=..., sara=... } names are the keys; per-person config in value \"One bastion, or none\" count = var.create ? 1 : 0 boolean toggle \"0 or 1 of something, read by outpu"},{"anchor":"2-for-each-key-based-copies-sets-or-maps","text":"t\" count = ... ? 1 : 0 + one .attr clean null-safety \"0 or many of a list of names\" for_each = toset var.list stable identity \"Map of name → config block\" for_each = var.config_map structured per-instance values \"For_each over a list not a set \" for_each = { for x in var.list : x = x } Terraform requires set/map, not list \"Add a resource based on a map being non-empty\" for_each = var.optional_map empty map = no resources no error \"Want to use modulo or index math\" count = N with count.index index is integer; math is natural \"Want stable identity for import later\" for_each keys stay consistent across applies 6. The for_each = toset ... pattern converting a list to a set for_each only accepts set or map . Pass a list → Error: Invalid for_each argument . Wrap in toset : The \"BETTER\" pattern gives you each.value as the index AND the AZ name as the key — best of both worlds. 7. count.index vs each.key / each.value cheat sheet Concept count for_each set for_each map --- --- --- --- Number of copies count number length toset ... length map Identity 0 , 1 , 2 \"key1\" , \"key2\" \"key1\" , \"key2\" Per-copy value count.index 0, 1, 2 each.key the key each.key + each.value the value splat works work"},{"anchor":"3-the-critical-difference-why-most-teams-get-burned","text":"s works N indexing works will error if N out of range works use lookup map, key, default works Length length aws_instance.web length aws_instance.web length aws_instance.web Map → list aws_instance.web .attr values aws_instance.web .attr values aws_instance.web .attr Empty list safety length ... == 0 check same same Removing 1 from middle all later indexes shift identity breaks only the removed key dies only the removed key dies Reference syntax aws_instance.web 0 aws_instance.web \"web-a\" aws_instance.team \"ahmed\" 8. Common patterns copy-ready Pattern 1 — subnets per AZ for_each map Pattern 2 — IAM users from a list for_each set Pattern 3 — IAM users with structured per-user config for_each map Pattern 4 — count for the \"I want N identical\" case Pattern 5 — conditional resource with one 9. The depends_on of meta-arguments rare but real Both count and for_each accept depends_on the explicit version : Avoid if possible — references var.X from another resource are clearer. Use this only for invisible side-effects e.g., external S3 bucket policy, IAM eventual consistency . 10. Indexing pitfalls the two errors that break your plan aws_instance.web 0 when count = 0 Fix: aws_instance.web "},{"anchor":"4-the-rename-trap-live-demonstration-of-the-difference","text":"0 .attr if length aws_instance.web 0 else null or use one .attr . aws_instance.team \"nonexistent\" when key is missing Fix: lookup aws_instance.team, \"nonexistent\", null or try aws_instance.team \"x\" , null . 11. count.index and each.value math composing with cidrsubnet The classic \"N subnets from a /16\": 12. Common errors exact messages → fixes Error Cause Fix --- --- --- Invalid for_each argument passed a list, not a set/map wrap in toset var.list or build a map Invalid index with 0 accessing index of empty list use one .attr or try 0 , null Invalid index with \"key\" key not in for_each map lookup map, key, null The given value is not suitable for ... wrong type for count string vs number declare count as number and ensure integer Removing from for_each re-creates resources you changed the keys e.g., toset gave a different set due to object equality check that keys are stable primitives strings/numbers , not objects count removed middle item, downstream state broken downstream aws_x 0 now points to a different resource use for_each with stable keys, OR run terraform state mv to fix for_each values must be known at plan tried to use for_each on a value derived from a resource attribu"},{"anchor":"5-quick-reference-which-one-when","text":"te use count for that case, or refactor 13. Interview Q&A - count vs for_each ? count = integer copies accessed by index 0..N-1 . for_each = copies of a set/map accessed by key. count breaks identity when you remove from the middle; for_each doesn't. - When to use each? Use for_each when copies have meaningful, stable identities servers by name, subnets by AZ, peers by id, etc. . Use count for \"I want N identical copies\" or \"0/1 toggle.\" - What's the rename trap with count ? Terraform assigns identities by index; if you remove the middle item, all later items shift and the resource \"renames\" itself, breaking downstream references. - What if I want for_each but have a list? Wrap it: for_each = toset var.list or build a map: for_each = { for x in var.list : x = x } . - How do you conditionally create a resource 0 or 1 ? count = var.enable ? 1 : 0 and reference with one .attr . - Can you mix count and for_each in the same resource block? No — they're mutually exclusive meta-arguments. - How do you access one specific copy? aws_instance.web 0 count or aws_instance.team \"ahmed\" for_each . Use try or lookup for safe access. - Why does Terraform require set/map for for_each? Because list "},{"anchor":"6-the-for-each-toset-pattern-converting-a-list-to-a-set","text":"values have no stable identity reordering changes indexes ; set/map keys are guaranteed unique and position-independent. - What is one and when to use it? Returns the single element of a 1-element list, or null if the list is empty. Use for safe access of 0/1 count resources. --- Next: locals and expressions the intermediate layer that turns raw variables into useful structure , or outputs deep-dive."}]},"aws-34-level-2-terraform-outputs-complete-guide":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-terraform-outputs-complete-guide","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\07-outputs.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate→Advanced /} Level 2 — Terraform Outputs — Complete Guide Companion to Core Workflow /courses/terraform/level1/workflow/ outputs , State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ state outputs in remote backend , and Multi-Env Multi-VPC /courses/terraform/level2/multi-env-multi-vpc/ . Outputs are how you get values OUT of Terraform — to other modules, to CI, to humans. This file covers every output pattern, the security model, the CLI surface, and the production-grade use cases. 1. Mental model 30 seconds An output block is a named export from your Terraform state. After apply , you can read it from: - the CLI terraform output vpc_id - another module module.network.vpc_id - another state via terraform_remote_state - CI terraform output -json jq .vpc_id.value - a saved plan file terraform show -json tfplan 2. The full anatomy one block, all fields Each field explained: - value required — any expression. The most common source is resource.X.attr or module.X.output . - description — surfaces in terraform"},{"anchor":"1-mental-model-30-seconds","text":" output list view; in terraform output -json it's in the description field. Always set it for non-trivial outputs. - sensitive = true — hides the value in CLI output and in terraform plan/apply log. State still holds plaintext see §6 . - depends_on — rarely needed because Terraform's reference graph already orders outputs. Use only when the output reads from an attribute that wouldn't be tracked otherwise e.g., a manually-imported resource . - precondition — checked before the value is returned. Failed precondition = plan/apply fails with error_message . Useful to enforce \"this output only makes sense if X is true.\" 3. The 5 output types with examples 4. terraform output the CLI surface Rule: never paste a sensitive output into Slack, a PR, or echo . Pipe to a secret store aws secretsmanager create-secret --secret-string \"$ terraform output -raw db_password \" . 5. Cross-module / cross-stack consumption the real use case Module → module the standard pattern Stack → stack the production pattern When two stacks need to share data, expose the source's outputs and consume them in the destination. Important: the source stack must run BEFORE the destination stack no auto-dependency . CI e"},{"anchor":"2-the-full-anatomy-one-block-all-fields","text":"nforces this with a job graph: network job runs first → its terraform output -json is saved as an artifact → app job uses that JSON. Deprecation note: terraform_remote_state is marked legacy by HashiCorp. The modern alternative is to expose a module and have the destination depend on it module versions in module.X.version . The state stays separate; the contract is the module interface. 6. sensitive vs ephemeral vs secrets the security model Mechanism Hidden in CLI? Stored in state? Reversible? When to use --- --- --- --- --- Plain output No Yes plaintext n/a Public IDs, ARNs, anything non-secret sensitive = true Yes warning + masked Yes plaintext Yes toggle DB passwords, API keys you control ephemeral = true Yes No not in state n/a one-shot Short-lived tokens, deployment proofs Vault / SSM / Secrets Manager data source Yes via provider Yes, but encrypted at rest Rotate The only true encryption story The interview rule: sensitive = true does NOT encrypt state. State is plaintext JSON; the S3 bucket must have SSE + restricted IAM. For real secrets, use a data source Vault, SSM, Secrets Manager and pass the value through sensitive = true for CLI masking. ephemeral = true is for one-s"},{"anchor":"3-the-5-output-types-with-examples","text":"hot values you don't want persisted. 7. Output file organization Convention: outputs.tf at the root of your project, separate from main.tf . The exception is module outputs, which live in the module's own outputs.tf . outputs.tf doubles as the \"this is what other stacks/modules can consume\" spec. Reviewers focus on it. 8. Output validation with precondition the underused feature Preconditions run at the same time as output evaluation, after the resources exist. Useful for catching \"this output would be useless because the underlying state is wrong\" before CI proceeds. 9. Outputs in for_each / count resources Naming tip: prefix the output with the resource type so consumers know what they're reading — web_instance_ips not ips , db_endpoint not endpoint . 10. Outputs in terraform plan the plan format Use case: CI checks output_changes to decide whether to deploy any change = proceed; certain destructive changes = gate . 11. Pre-conditions vs post-conditions the underused pair precondition on output = \"this value is sane.\" postcondition on resource = \"this resource was created correctly.\" Both can fail plan / apply before any harm. Use them to enforce invariants you care about. 12. Co"},{"anchor":"4-terraform-output-the-cli-surface","text":"mmon errors and fixes Error Cause Fix --- --- --- Error: Output refers to sensitive values output value comes from a sensitive = true source e.g., data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version and you didn't mark the output sensitive add sensitive = true to the output block Sensitive output value displayed warning asked for a sensitive value in terraform output -raw ; Terraform warned then printed only use in trusted CI; mask in logs Output \"X\" not found output block deleted between applies check git log; terraform state if you need to inspect old outputs Precondition failed: ... an output precondition was violated fix the underlying state often a missing resource or relax the precondition terraform_remote_state returns wrong/stale values the source state was rewritten by a different apply the consumer's data source caches — re-run terraform apply to refresh Output order in CLI terraform output sorts by name use -json + jq for predictable ordering Output value not interpolating the value is null because of a try or lookup default inspect the resource; check if the lookup is correct 13. Production patterns the playbook Pattern A — single stack, outputs are the public API The downstream app"},{"anchor":"5-cross-module-cross-stack-consumption-the-real-use-case","text":" stack consumes rds_sg_id and private_subnets only. Clear contract. Pattern B — outputs as a pipeline artifact Or, more cleanly, use terraform_remote_state with the state bucket. Pattern C — outputs for the run summary Then terraform output -json deploy_summary prints a clean summary at the end of CI. 14. Interview Q&A - What is a Terraform output? A named export from the state, evaluated after the resources are created. Read via CLI terraform output , from other modules module.X.Y , or from other stacks data.terraform_remote_state . - sensitive = true encrypts state? No. It hides the value in CLI output. The state file still contains plaintext. Use a Vault/SSM data source for true encryption-at-rest. - How do you pass outputs between stacks? data \"terraform_remote_state\" legacy but works or module outputs modern . The first requires the source to run first; the second requires the module version to be pinned. - Why use a separate outputs.tf file? It documents the public interface of your stack. Reviewers see exactly what other stacks can consume. - count outputs vs for_each outputs? count : use .attr for the list, one .attr for safe single access. for_each : use values map .attr f"},{"anchor":"module-module-the-standard-pattern","text":"or the list, or lookup map, \"key\", null for one. - What does precondition on an output do? Runs a check when the output is evaluated; failure aborts plan/apply with your error_message . Use to enforce \"this output only makes sense if X.\" - How do you read outputs in CI? terraform output -json jq -r .vpc_id.value — script-friendly. Mask sensitive outputs in CI logs. - Can a sensitive output be displayed in terraform plan ? No — plan shows sensitive value placeholder, never the actual value. The state file, however, contains the plaintext. - Why would you use ephemeral over sensitive ? ephemeral is not stored in state at all; you can only read it in the same apply/plan. Use for one-shot tokens or deployment proofs. For repeated access e.g., another apply re-reading the value , sensitive is required. --- Next: locals and expressions the intermediate layer , or data sources read-only plug into existing infra ."}]},"aws-35-level-2-hands-on-create-a-new-project-use-workspaces-deploy":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"level-2-hands-on-create-a-new-project-use-workspaces-deploy-3-envs","text":"{/ MIGRATED from: D:\\NTI Courses Master Folder\\TerraformMaster Folder\\Terraform\\level2\\08-new-project-workspace-walkthrough.md 2026-09-07 Course metadata: course=terraform level=2 difficulty=Intermediate type=hands-on walkthrough /} Level 2 — Hands-on: Create a New Project, Use Workspaces, Deploy 3 Envs Companion to Workspaces /courses/terraform/level2/workspaces/ , count vs for_each /courses/terraform/level2/count-for-each/ , Outputs /courses/terraform/level2/outputs/ . The full end-to-end walkthrough : scaffold a project from scratch, write the code, run it across dev/staging/prod via workspaces, and verify each env is isolated. 30 minutes if you have Floci; 60 if you hit real AWS. 0. Goal Build a tiny but real \"study cluster\": - 1 VPC per env 3 AZs, public + private subnets - A configurable count of EC2 instances in the public subnet - A simple web page per instance Hello from - - One per env, all from the same code, isolated state The local provider keeps this runnable end-to-end without AWS/Floci; the aws versions are commented in to show what changes. 1. The project layout what we're about to create Rule: keep resource blocks and provider blocks in separate files for review h"},{"anchor":"0-goal","text":"ygiene. Locals and outputs get their own files too. The flat layout is the Terraform norm for projects under ~30 resources. 2. Step 1 — versions.tf always first - required_version — fail fast if someone runs with an older CLI matters because we use for_each = { for i, az ... } syntax that needs 1.x . - required_providers — pins AWS provider; the ~ 5.0 means 5.x but not 6.0 . Companion file created by terraform init : .terraform.lock.hcl — auto-generated, commit it . It locks the exact provider binary so your teammate's init downloads the same bits. 3. Step 2 — variables.tf declared inputs Why these shapes: - sizing and cidrs are maps keyed by env name — Terraform's map is perfect for \"lookup by name\" patterns. - env_override exists so the same code can run with -var=\"env_override=qa\" even if a qa workspace doesn't exist yet in that case the code creates it on first use via count . - The CIDR matrix is the production answer to \"how do you avoid overlap\" — set it once in code, never in tfvars . 4. Step 3 — providers.tf provider blocks; one per cloud For the Floci practice free, no AWS account , keep AWS provider as-is — endpoints will be added in main.tf . The local provider is unuse"},{"anchor":"1-the-project-layout-what-we-re-about-to-create","text":"d in the AWS example; the comment block shows the real shape. 5. Step 4 — main.tf the actual resources This is the file that does the work. Read top to bottom. What each block does so you can teach it back : - locals.env_name → picks var.env_override if set, else terraform.workspace . The \"default\" workspace is the implicit one before you create any workspace; we treat it as \"dev\" so first-time users don't get a 404. - data.aws_availability_zones → 3 real AZs, stable across applies won't drift if AWS adds AZs . - for_each = local.azs a map keyed by AZ name → stable identity. Remove one AZ from a future Terraform AWS update → only that subnet dies. - cidrsubnet math: VPC /16 + 8 newbits + index = /24 per subnet. - The user_data is a heredoc that boots a real web server with a per-instance page. - values aws_subnet.public 0 → grab the first public subnet ID simplification; in production you'd balance across all 3 . 6. Step 5 — outputs.tf what we expose Notice: instance_public_ips is a map keyed by name . If you want a list, drop the name = part and use .public_ip . The map form is friendlier for curl -based verification and downstream consumption. 7. Step 6 — Initialize one time What"},{"anchor":"2-step-1-versions-tf-always-first","text":" happens: 1. Terraform reads required_version and required_providers . 2. Downloads the AWS provider into .terraform/ . 3. Creates .terraform.lock.hcl commit it . 4. No state created yet no backend configured, uses local . Output you should see: 8. Step 7 — Workspace setup one time, after init The shows you're currently on prod — that was the last new operation. terraform workspace select dev to switch. 9. Step 8 — Deploy dev the full plan + apply After apply: Important: the state file is now terraform.tfstate local but the workspace is \"dev\". When you move to staging, a new local state is created. With a remote backend, the workspace name is appended to the key e.g., s3://bucket/env/staging/terraform.tfstate . 10. Step 9 — Deploy staging and prod proving isolation The proof of isolation: No apply in any env touches the resources of the others. That's the whole point of workspaces. 11. Step 10 — Verify state files the S3 / file-system proof With local backend: With S3 backend recommended for teams; see State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ for the full backend config : 12. Bonus — Remote backend so the team shares state Add a backend.tf file separate from versions."},{"anchor":"3-step-2-variables-tf-declared-inputs","text":"tf so init order is clear : 13. Common gotchas the 5 you'll hit first Gotcha 1 — \"I see default workspace when I expected my env\" You ran terraform init and apply BEFORE creating any workspace. The \"default\" workspace is implicit. Fix: terraform workspace new dev then terraform apply creates the dev state from scratch; the \"default\" apply was a one-off . Going forward, always create a workspace before the first apply . Gotcha 2 — Invalid index on aws_subnet.public 0 You used count + index and the count is 0, or you used for_each and the key is missing. Fix: values aws_subnet.public 0 first element of the map or one aws_subnet.public .id . See count vs for_each /courses/terraform/level2/count-for-each/ . Gotcha 3 — Resources in another workspace \"leak\" into plan They don't. Run terraform workspace show to confirm; the state you're seeing is the current workspace's. If you see \"outside\" resources, check terraform_remote_state calls — those are cross-stack, not cross-workspace. Gotcha 4 — \"Module output not found\" on terraform_remote_state The source stack hasn't applied yet, OR the key path is wrong, OR the IAM role can't read the state file. Run aws s3api get-object --bucket X --key"},{"anchor":"4-step-3-providers-tf-provider-blocks-one-per-cloud","text":" Y to verify. Gotcha 5 — Two engineers apply at once, second errors with \"state lock\" That's the DynamoDB lock working correctly. Read the error, find the holder, coordinate. If the holder died, terraform force-unlock see State Backend /courses/terraform/level1/state-backend/ . Never hand-delete the DynamoDB row. 14. The 5 commands you'll use 90% of the time workspace select is the most dangerous of the five — running apply on the wrong workspace hits the wrong resources. CI removes the risk by setting TF_WORKSPACE automatically; humans should still type the workspace name and read it back. 15. Full lab checklist use this when you do it for real - Created study-cluster/ with the 5 files above - terraform init succeeded; .terraform.lock.hcl exists - terraform workspace new dev / staging / prod ran - terraform workspace list shows 3 or 4 with default - apply on dev created 1 instance, on staging 2, on prod 3 - curl returns the right Hello from -node-XX per env - terraform plan on each env is 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy - State files are isolated local or S3 — confirmed by listing - terraform output -json jq .instance_count.value matches expectations - Tried terraform workspac"},{"anchor":"5-step-4-main-tf-the-actual-resources","text":"e delete dev -force once and re-created to see the warning - Destroyed one env; the other two unaffected 16. Interview Q&A - Walk me through creating a new Terraform project with workspaces. Scaffold versions/variables/main/outputs files → init → workspace new for each env → switch + apply per env. State stays per-workspace under one backend. - How do you keep state isolated per env? Workspaces one folder, many state files under one backend OR separate folders with separate backends. Workspaces is simpler but less visible. - What does terraform.workspace give you in code? The active workspace name. Use it as a key into per-env maps sizing, CIDR, tags . - How do you verify isolation between workspaces? terraform plan on each env should be 0 add, 0 change, 0 destroy the \"second-run is a no-op\" property . - How do you destroy one env without affecting others? Switch to that workspace and terraform destroy ; other workspaces are untouched. - Why use -out=tfplan + apply tfplan ? Guarantees you apply EXACTLY the plan you reviewed — no drift between plan and apply, no surprise prompts. - What's the first thing to do after terraform init ? Create a workspace BEFORE the first apply . Otherw"},{"anchor":"6-step-5-outputs-tf-what-we-expose","text":"ise you land in default and have to migrate. - How do you share outputs between workspaces? Don't — workspaces are isolated. Share via terraform_remote_state legacy or via a module consumed in both workspaces. --- Next: locals and expressions patterns that make the above cleaner — sizing maps, CIDR matrices, tag accumulation , or data sources read-only lookups for AMI/AZs/RDS endpoint ."}]},"aws-36-observability-pptx":{"chunks":[]},"aws-37-aws-certified-solutions-architect-slides-v48":{"chunks":[{"page":1,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com https://links.datacumulus.com/aws - cert - solution - architect - pt - coupon https://links.dat acumulus.com/ aws - cert - solution - architect - pt - coupon https://links.da tacumulus.com /aws - certified - sa - associate - coupon https://links.data cumulus.com/aw s - certified - sa - associate - coupon https://links.datacumulus.com/aw s - certified - sa - associate - coupon AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate By Stéphane Maarek EXTRA PRACTICE EXAMS COURSE"},{"page":2,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Disclaimer: These slides are copyrighted and strictly for personal use only • This document is reserved for people enrolled into the Ultimate AWS Solutions Architect Associate Course • Please do not share this document , it is intended for per sonal use and exam preparation only, thank you. • If you’ve obtained these slides for free on a website that is not the course’s website, please reach out to piracy@datacumulus.com . Thanks! • Best of luck for the exam and happy learning!"},{"page":3,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ta b l e o f C o n t e n t s • Getting Started with AWS • AWS Identity & Access Management (AWS IAM) • Amazon EC2 – Basics • Amazon EC2 – Associate • Amazon EC2 – Instance Storage • High Availability & Scalability • RDS, Aurora & ElastiCache • Amazon Route 53 • Classic Solutions Architecture • Amazon S3"},{"page":4,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ta b l e o f C o n t e n t s • Amazon S3 – Advanced • Amazon S3 – Security • CloudFront & Global Accelerator • AWS Storage Extras • AWS Integration & Messaging • Containers on AWS • Serverless Overview • Serverless Architectures • Databases in AWS • D ata & Analytics"},{"page":5,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Ta b l e o f C o n t e n t s • Machine Learning • AWS Monitoring, Audit & Performance • Advanced Identity in AWS • AWS Security & Encr yption • Amazon VPC • Disaster Recovery & Migrations • More Solutions Architecture • Other Services • White Papers & Architectures • Exam Preparation • Congratulations"},{"page":6,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cer tified Solutions Architect Associate Course SAA - C03"},{"page":7,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Welcome! We’re starting in 5 minutes • We’re going to prepare for the Solutions Architect exam - SAA - C03 • It’s a challenging certification, so this course will be long and interesting • Basic IT knowledge is necessary • This course contains videos... • From the Cloud Practitioner, Developer and SysOps course - shared knowledge • Specific to the Solutions Architect exam - exciting ones on architecture! • We will cover over 30 AWS ser vices • AWS / IT Beginner s welcome! (but take your time, it’s not a race)"},{"page":8,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com My SAA - C03 certification: 96.1%"},{"page":9,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com About me • I’m Stephane! • Wor ked as in IT consultant and AWS Solutions Architect, Developer & SysOps • Wor ked with AWS many year s: built websites, apps, streaming platfor ms • Veteran Instructor on AWS (Certifications, CloudFormation, Lambda, EC2...) • Yo u c a n f i n d m e o n • GitHub: https://github.com/simplesteph • LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/stephanemaarek • Medium: https://medium.com/@stephane.maarek • Tw i t t e r : https://twitter.com/stephanemaarek"},{"page":10,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What’s AWS? • AWS (Amazon Web Ser vices) is a Cloud Provider • They provide you with servers and services that you can use on demand and scale easily • AWS has revolutionized IT over time • AWS power s some of the biggest websites in the wor ld • Amazon.com • Netflix"},{"page":11,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What we’ll learn in this course (and more!) Amazon EC2 Amazon ECR Amazon ECS Elastic Load Balancing AWS Elastic Beanstalk AWS Lambda Amazon S3 Amazon RDS Amazon DynamoDB Amazon ElastiCache Amazon CloudFront Amazon Route 53 Amazon CloudWatch AWS CloudFormation AWS CloudTrail IAM AWS KMS Amazon Kinesis Amazon API Gateway AWS Step Functions Auto Scaling Amazon SQS Amazon SNS Amazon SES Amazon Aurora"},{"page":12,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Navigating the AWS spaghetti bowl"},{"page":13,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Udemy Tips"},{"page":14,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Getting started with AWS"},{"page":15,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Histor y 2002: Internally launched 2003: Amazon infrastructure is one of their core strength. Idea to market 2004: Launched publicly with SQS 2006: Re - launched publicly with SQS, S3 & EC2 2007: Launched in Europe"},{"page":16,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Number Facts • In 2023 , AWS had $ 90 billion in annual revenue • AWS accounts for 31 % of the market in Q1 2024 (Microsoft is 2nd with 2 5 %) • Pioneer and Leader of the AWS Cloud Mar ket for the 13 th consecutive year • Over 1,000,000 active users Gartner Magic Quadrant"},{"page":17,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Cloud Use Cases • AWS enables you to build sophisticated, scalable applications • Applicable to a diverse set of industries • Use cases include • Enterprise IT , Backup & Stor age , Big Data analytics • Web site hosting , Mobile & Social Apps • Gaming"},{"page":18,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Global Infrastr ucture • AWS Regions • AWS Availability Zones • AWS Data Center s • AWS Edge Locations / Points of Presence • https://infrastructure.aws/"},{"page":19,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Regions • AWS has Regions all around the world • Names can be us - east - 1, eu - west - 3... • A region is a cluster of data centers • Most AWS services are region - scoped https://aws.amazon.com/about - aws/global - infrastructure/"},{"page":20,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com How to choose an AWS Region? • Compliance with data governance and legal requirements : data never leaves a region without your explicit permission • Proximity to customers : reduced latency • Available ser vice s within a Region : new services and new features aren’t available in every Region • Pricing : pricing varies region to region and is transparent in the service pricing page If you need to launch a new application, where should you do it? ? ? ? ?"},{"page":21,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Availability Zones • Each region has many availability zones (usually 3, min is 3, max is 6). Example: • ap - southeast - 2a • ap - southeast - 2b • ap - southeast - 2c • Each availability zone (AZ) is one or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking, and connectivity • They’re separate from each other, so that they’re isolated from disasters • They’re connected with high bandwidth, ultra - low latency networking AWS Region Sydney: ap - southeast - 2 ap - southeast - 2a ap - southeast - 2b ap - southeast - 2c"},{"page":22,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Points of Presence (Edge Locations) • Amazon has 400+ Points of Presence (400+ Edge Locations & 10+ Regional Caches) in 90+ cities across 40+ countries • Content is delivered to end users with lower latency https://aws.amazon.com/cloudfront/features/"},{"page":23,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com To u r o f t h e A W S C o n s o l e • AWS has Global Ser vices: • Identity and Access Management (IAM) • Route 53 (DNS service) • CloudFront (Content Delivery Network) • WAF (Web Application Firewall) • Most AWS services are Region - scoped : • Amazon EC2 (Infrastructure as a Service) • Elastic Beanstalk (Platform as a Service) • Lambda (Function as a Service) • Rekognition (Software as a Service) • Region Table: https://aws.amazon.com/about - aws/global - infrastructure/regional - product - services"},{"page":24,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com AWS Identity and Access Management (AWS IAM)"},{"page":25,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM: Users & Groups • IAM = Identity and Access Management, Global service • Root account created by default, shouldn’t be used or shared • Users are people within your organization, and can be grouped • Groups only contain users, not other groups • Users don’t have to belong to a group, and user can belong to multiple groups Alice Bob Charles David Edward Group: Developers Group: Operations Group Audit Team Fred"},{"page":26,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM: Permissions • Users or Groups can be assigned JSON documents called policies • These policies define the permissions of the users • In AWS you apply the least privilege principle : don’t give more permissions than a user needs { \"Version\" : \"2012 - 10 - 17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : \"ec2:Describe*\" , \"Resource\" : \"*\" }, { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : \" elasticloadbalancing:Describe *\" , \"Resource\" : \"*\" }, { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \" cloudwatch:ListMetrics \" , \" cloudwatch:GetMetricStatistics \" , \" cloudwatch:Describe *\" ], \"Resource\" : \"*\" } ] }"},{"page":27,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Policies inheritance Alice Bob Charles David Edward Developers Operations Audit Team Fred inline"},{"page":28,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Policies Structure • Consists of • Ver sion: policy language version, always include “2012 - 10 - 17” • Id: an identifier for the policy (optional) • Statement: one or more individual statements (required) • Statements consists of • Sid: an identifier for the statement (optional) • Effect: whether the statement allows or denies access (Allow, Deny) • Principal: account/user/role to which this policy applied to • Action: list of actions this policy allows or denies • Resource: list of resources to which the actions applied to • Condition: conditions for when this policy is in effect (optional)"},{"page":29,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM – Password Policy • Strong passwords = higher security for your account • In AWS, you can setup a password policy: • Set a minimum password length • Require specific character types: • including uppercase letters • lowercase letters • numbers • non - alphanumeric characters • Allow all IAM users to change their own passwords • Require users to change their password after some time (password expiration) • Prevent password re - use"},{"page":30,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Multi Factor Authentication - MFA • Users have access to your account and can possibly change configurations or delete resources in your AWS account • Yo u w a n t t o p r o t e c t y o u r R o o t A c c o u n t s a n d I A M u s e r s • MFA = password you know + security device you own • Main benefit of MFA: if a password is stolen or hacked, the account is not compromised Alice + Password => Successful login"},{"page":31,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com MFA devices options in AWS Virtual MFA device Google Authenticator (phone only) Authy (phone only) Universal 2nd Factor (U2F) Security Key YubiKey by Yubico (3 rd party) Support for multiple tokens on a single device. Support for multiple root and IAM users using a single security key"},{"page":32,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com MFA devices options in AWS Hardware Key Fob MFA Device Provided by Gemalto (3 rd party) Hardware Key Fob MFA Device for AWS GovCloud (US) Provided by SurePassID (3 rd party)"},{"page":33,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com How can users access AWS ? • To a c c e s s A W S , y o u h a v e t h r e e o p t i o n s : • AWS Management Console (protected by password + MFA) • AWS Command Line Interface (CLI): protected by access keys • AWS Software Developer Kit (SDK) - for code: protected by access keys • Access Keys are generated through the AWS Console • Users manage their own access keys • Access Keys are secret, just like a password. Don’t share them • Access Key ID ~= username • Secret Access Key ~= password"},{"page":34,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Example (Fake) Access Keys • Access key ID: AKIASK4E37PV4983d6C • Secret Access Key: AZPN3zojWozWCndIjhB0Unh8239a1bzbzO5fqqkZq • Remember : don’t share your access keys"},{"page":35,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What’s the AWS CLI? • A tool that enables you to interact with AWS services using commands in your command - line shell • Direct access to the public APIs of AWS services • Yo u c a n d e v e l o p s c r i p t s t o m a n a g e y o u r r e s o u r c e s • It’s open - source https://github.com/aws/aws - cli • Alternative to using AWS Management Console"},{"page":36,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com What’s the AWS SDK? • AWS Software Development Kit (AWS SDK) • Language - specific APIs (set of libraries) • Enables you to access and manage AWS services programmatically • Embedded within your application • Supports • SDKs (JavaScript, Python, PHP, .NET, Ruby, Java, Go, Node.js, C++) • Mobile SDKs (Android, iOS, ...) • IoT Device SDKs (Embedded C, Arduino, ...) • Example: AWS CLI is built on AWS SDK for Python AWS SDK Your Application"},{"page":37,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Roles for Services • Some AWS service will need to perform actions on your behalf • To d o s o , w e w i l l a s s i g n permissions to AWS services with IAM Roles • Common roles: • EC2 Instance Roles • Lambda Function Roles • Roles for CloudFormation EC2 Instance (virtual server) IAM Role Access AWS"},{"page":38,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Security Tools • IAM Credentials Report (account - level) • a report that lists all your account's users and the status of their various credentials • IAM Access Advisor (user - level) • Access advisor shows the service permissions granted to a user and when those services were last accessed. • Yo u c a n u s e t h i s i n f o r m a t i o n t o r e v i s e y o u r p o l i c i e s ."},{"page":39,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Guidelines & Best Practices • Don’t use the root account except for AWS account setup • One physical user = One AWS user • Assign users to groups and assign permissions to groups • Create a strong password policy • Use and enforce the use of Multi Factor Authentication (MFA) • Create and use Roles for giving permissions to AWS services • Use Access Keys for Programmatic Access (CLI / SDK) • Audit permissions of your account using IAM Credentials Repor t & IAM Access Advisor • Never share IAM users & Access Keys"},{"page":40,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com IAM Section – Summary • Users: mapped to a physical user, has a password for AWS Console • Groups: contains users only • Policies: JSON document that outlines permissions for users or groups • Roles: for EC2 instances or AWS services • Security: MFA + Password Policy • AWS CLI: manage your AWS services using the command - line • AWS SDK: manage your AWS services using a programming language • Access Keys: access AWS using the CLI or SDK • Audit: IAM Credential Reports & IAM Access Advisor"},{"page":41,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Amazon EC2 – Basics"},{"page":42,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Amazon EC2 • EC2 is one of the most popular of AWS’ offering • EC2 = Elastic Compute Cloud = Infrastructure as a Service • It mainly consists in the capability of : • Renting virtual machines (EC2) • Storing data on virtual drives (EBS) • Distributing load across machines (ELB) • Scaling the services using an auto - scaling group (ASG) • Knowing EC2 is fundamental to understand how the Cloud works"},{"page":43,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 sizing & configuration options • Operating System ( OS ): Linux, Windows or Mac OS • How much compute power & cores ( CPU ) • How much random - access memory ( RAM ) • How much storage space: • Network - attached ( EBS & EFS ) • hardware ( EC2 Instance Store ) • Network card: speed of the card, Public IP address • Firewall rules: security group • Bootstrap script (configure at first launch): EC2 User Data"},{"page":44,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 User Data • It is possible to bootstrap our instances using an EC2 User data script. • bootstrapping means launching commands when a machine starts • That script is only run once at the instance first star t • EC2 user data is used to automate boot tasks such as: • Installing updates • Installing software • Downloading common files from the internet • Anything you can think of • The EC2 User Data Script runs with the root user"},{"page":45,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Hands - On: Launching an EC2 Instance running Linux • We’ll be launching our fir st vir tual ser ver using the AWS Console • We’ll get a fir st high - level approach to the various parameters • We’ll see that our web server is launched using EC2 user data • We’ll lear n how to star t / stop / ter minate our instance ."},{"page":46,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types - Overview • You can use different types of EC2 instances that are optimised for different use cases ( https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/instance - types/ ) • AWS has the following naming convention: m 5 . 2xlarge • m : instance class • 5 : gener ation (AWS improves them over time) • 2xlarge : size within the instance class"},{"page":47,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types – General Purpose • Great for a diversity of workloads such as web servers or code repositories • Balance between: • Compute • Memory • Networking • In the course, we will be using the t2.micro which is a General Purpose EC2 instance * this list will evolve over time, please check the AWS website for the latest information"},{"page":48,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types – Compute Optimized • Great for compute - intensive tasks that require high performance processors : • Batch processing workloads • Media transcoding • High performance web servers • High performance computing (HPC) • Scientific modeling & machine learning • Dedicated gaming servers * this list will evolve over time, please check the AWS website for the latest information"},{"page":49,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types – Memory Optimized • Fast performance for workloads that process large data sets in memory • Use cases: • High performance, relational/non - relational databases • Distributed web scale cache stores • In - memory databases optimized for BI (business intelligence) • Applications performing real - time processing of big unstructured data * this list will evolve over time, please check the AWS website for the latest information"},{"page":50,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types – Storage Optimized • Great for storage - intensive tasks that require high, sequential read and write access to large data sets on local storage • Use cases: • High frequency online transaction processing (OLTP) systems • Relational & NoSQL databases • Cache for in - memory databases (for example, Redis) • Data warehousing applications • Distributed file systems * this list will evolve over time, please check the AWS website for the latest information"},{"page":51,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com EC2 Instance Types : example Instance vCPU Mem (GiB) Storage Network Performance EBS Bandwidth (Mbps) t2.micro 1 1 EBS - Only Low to Moderate t2.xlarge 4 16 EBS - Only Moderate c5d.4xlarge 16 32 1 x 400 NVMe SSD Up to 10 Gbps 4,750 r5.16xlarge 64 512 EBS Only 20 Gbps 13,600 m5.8xlarge 32 128 EBS Only 10 Gbps 6,800 Great website: https://instances.vantage.sh"},{"page":52,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Introduction to Security Groups • Security Groups are the fundamental of network security in AWS • They control how traffic is allowed into or out of our EC2 Instances. • Security groups only contain rules • Security groups rules can reference by IP or by security group Inbound traffic Outbound traffic Security Group WWW EC2 Instance"},{"page":53,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Security Groups Deeper Dive • Security groups are acting as a “firewall” on EC2 instances • They regulate: • Access to Ports • Authorised IP ranges – IPv4 and IPv6 • Control of inbound network (from other to the instance) • Control of outbound network (from the instance to other)"},{"page":54,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Security Groups Diagram EC2 Instance IP XX.XX.XX.XX Port 22 Port 22 Security Group 1 Inbound Filter IP / Port with Rules Any Port Security Group 1 Outbound Filter IP / Port with Rules Your Computer - IP XX.XX.XX.XX (authorised port 22) Other computer (not authorised port 22) WWW Any IP – Any Port"},{"page":55,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Security Groups Good to know • Can be attached to multiple instances • Locked down to a region / VPC combination • Does live “outside” the EC2 – if traffic is blocked the EC2 instance won’t see it • It’s good to maintain one separate security group for SSH access • If your application is not accessible (time out), then it’s a security group issue • If your application gives a “connection refused“ error, then it’s an application error or it’s not launched • All inbound traffic is blocked by default • All outbound traffic is authorised by default"},{"page":56,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Referencing other security groups Diagram EC2 Instance IP XX.XX.XX.XX Port 123 Port 123 Port 123 Security Group 1 Inbound Authorising Security Group 1 Authorising Security Group 2 EC2 Instance IP XX.XX.XX.XX Security Group 2 (attached) EC2 Instance IP XX.XX.XX.XX Security Group 1 (attached) EC2 Instance IP XX.XX.XX.XX Security Group 3 (attached)"},{"page":57,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Classic Ports to know • 22 = SSH (Secure Shell) - log into a Linux instance • 21 = FTP (File Transfer Protocol) – upload files into a file share • 22 = SFTP (Secure File Transfer Protocol) – upload files using SSH • 80 = HTTP – access unsecured websites • 443 = HTTPS – access secured websites • 3389 = RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol) – log into a Windows instance"},{"page":58,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com SSH Summary Table Mac Linux Windows < 10 Windows >= 10 SSH Putty EC2 Instance Connect"},{"page":59,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com Which Lectures to watch • Mac / Linux: • SSH on Mac/Linux lecture • Windows: • Putty Lecture • If Windows 10: SSH on Windows 10 lecture • All: • EC2 Instance Connect lecture"},{"page":60,"text":"© Stephane Maarek NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION © Stephane Maarek www.datacumulus.com SSH troubleshooting • Students have the most problems with SSH • If things don’t work... 1. Re - watch the lecture. You may have missed something 2. Read the troubleshooting guide 3. Try EC2 Instance Connect • If one method works (SSH, Putty or EC2 Instance Connect) you’re good • If no method works, that’s okay, the course won’t use SSH much"}]},"aws-38-map-of-the-walkthrough":{"chunks":[{"anchor":"map-of-the-walkthrough","text":"ALB Stack Walkthrough · INK &amp; AMBER · By Nabawy ONEXT · LEVEL 2 · HANDS-ON · PROJECT WALKTHROUGH The Application Load Balancer Stack Public ALB · Internal ALB 2 Tiers · 18 Resources Internet-facing and internal ALBs, target groups, listeners, four security groups, four EC2 instances, three workspaces. Terraform ~&gt; 5.0 , Floci-verified, ready for the FLOCI pipeline. 18 resource blocks 3 workspaces 66 resources deployed ~&gt;5.0 AWS provider pin A Technical Book · INK &amp; AMBER Series · By Nabawy Project: live-verify-floci/alb-cluster PUBLIC ALB INTERNAL ALB 4 SGS FLOCI-READY IDEMPOTENT · VERSION-PINNED · MULTI-ENV — BY NABAWY CONTENTS 🗺️ Map of the Walkthrough 📖 15 SHEETS One task, three phases: plan → build → prove . Click any row to jump; every page has a TOC badge back. I PLAN 03 Executive Summary — the task in one paragraph 5 min 04 The Resource Map — two tiers, one picture 5 min 05 Project Layout — 10 files, why each exists 4 min II BUILD — THE FILES 06 versions.tf + providers.tf — the pin &amp; the Floci switch 7 min 07 variables.tf + locals.tf — every knob, workspace naming 6 min 08 main.tf I — Networking + the 4 Security Groups 7 min 09 main.tf II — EC2 fleet + th"},{"anchor":"the-task-in-one-paragraph","text":"e Public ALB quartet 8 min 10 main.tf III — Internal ALB + health-check anatomy 6 min 11 outputs.tf — the 10-value contract 4 min III PROVE 12 Workspaces — dev/staging/prod from one codebase 6 min 13 Verify + Idempotence + Docs cross-reference 6 min 14 FLOCI pipeline + Cleanup + the Truth Table 6 min IV FINALE 15 Recap + Cheat Sheet + Self-Check 🏁 end DEBUG Reading order: pages 4–5 are the plan what exists, where it lives , 6–11 are the build every file, in dependency order , 12–14 are the proof apply, verify, pipeline . Estimated time: 30 min with Floci, 60 min on real AWS. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 2 — ▤ TOC prev next EXECUTIVE SUMMARY The Task — In One Paragraph 📖 5 MIN Provision a public Application Load Balancer in front of two frontend EC2 instances public subnets and an internal Application Load Balancer in front of two backend EC2 instances private subnets . Four custom security groups, two target groups with HTTP health checks, two listeners, four target-group attachments, plus the surrounding VPC plumbing. Use Terraform 1.x with the hashicorp/aws ~&gt; 5.0 provider — exactly the modern arguments from the official docs — and drive dev/staging/prod with workspaces so one co"},{"anchor":"the-resource-map","text":"debase ships three environments. 🔑 TIER 1 — PUBLIC internet → public ALB → frontend EC2 ×2. SG allows 80/443 from the world. The front door. 🔑 TIER 2 — INTERNAL frontend + VPC CIDR → internal ALB → backend EC2 ×2. SG chains by reference. Defense-in-depth. ▣ The route through this walkthrough 🗺️ Resource Map The full traffic picture — both tiers, SGs, TGs, attachments — one diagram. 🌳 Project Layout 10 files, and why each is its own file. 🏛️ Pin &amp; Switch versions.tf locks providers; providers.tf redirects every API to Floci. 🎛️ Inputs &amp; Naming 12 tunable knobs; workspace-aware name prefix + common tags. 🌐 SG Chain 4 security groups chained by reference — no 0.0.0.0/0 beyond the public ALB. ⚖️ The Quartet LB + target group + listener + attachment, per tier — the canonical four. 🔍 The Proof Workspaces ×3 applied to Floci, verify script, \"No changes\" idempotence gate. ⚙️ The Pipeline FLOCI CI — fmt / init / validate / select / apply / verify per workspace. 🔧 The Rescue Kit Troubleshooting, cleanup commands, and the honest Floci truth table. 🎓 TIME BUDGET 30 minutes with Floci emulated control plane, EC2 excluded · 60 minutes on real AWS full stack including EC2 + atta"},{"anchor":"project-layout","text":"chments . ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 3 — TOC prev next SECTION 01 · THE PICTURE 🗺️ The Resource Map 📖 5 MIN The whole stack in one diagram. Read it top-down for Tier 1 public , then the second block for Tier 2 internal . Every arrow is an SG rule or a TG attachment. internet │ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ sg public_alb 80/443 │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ aws_lb.public public │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ tg public │ health_check { /, 200 } └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ attachment → frontend │ │ 0 1 │ └─────────────────────────┘ sg frontend_ec2 ──80──▶ ┌─────────────────────────┐ VPC CIDR ──────80──▶ │ aws_lb.internal int │ │ sg internal_alb │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ tg internal │ health_check { /, 200 } └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ attachment → backend │ │ 0 1 │ └─────────────────────────┘ 🔑 THE SHAPE Both tiers are the same quartet — ALB → target group → listener → attachments — differing only in exposure internal = false/true , security group, subnets, and which instance set they point at. Learn one tier, you know bot"},{"anchor":"versions-tf-providers-tf","text":"h. 💡 COUNT THE ARROWS The SG chain: world → public_alb SG → frontend_ec2 SG by reference → internal_alb SG by reference + VPC CIDR → backend_ec2 SG by reference . No 0.0.0.0/0 anywhere except the public ALB. That chain is the security model. ⚠ NOT IN THE MAP The VPC plumbing IGW, route tables, 4 subnets across 2 AZs sits underneath everything — counted in the 18 resource blocks, but it carries no app traffic itself. 🎓 EXAM \"What does this stack do?\" → two ALB tiers: public ALB → 2 frontend EC2s in public subnets; internal ALB → 2 backend EC2s in private subnets; HTTP health checks on / expecting 200; SGs chained by reference; 3 environments via workspaces. ⏭️ Where do the 18 resource blocks live? → Project Layout ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 4 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 02 · THE FILES 🌳 Project Layout 📖 4 MIN Ten files under live-verify-floci/alb-cluster/ . Each is its own file for a reason: live-verify-floci/alb-cluster/ ├── versions.tf Terraform + AWS provider pin ├── providers.tf AWS provider with Floci endpoint switching ├── variables.tf all declared inputs ├── locals.tf name_prefix + common_tags ├── main.tf 18 resources VPC, subnets, SGs, EC2, ALBs… ├── outputs.tf DNS names,"},{"anchor":"variables-tf-locals-tf","text":" TG ARNs, instance IDs ├── terraform.tfvars.example sample inputs ├── run-alb.sh one-command deploy across workspaces ├── verify-alb.sh post-apply smoke test queries Floci └── README.md usage notes TASK Why each file is its own file the reviewer's view File Why it's its own file versions.tf Locks Terraform &amp; provider versions — protects the team from drift . providers.tf Floci endpoint switching lives here so the rest of the code never knows whether AWS is real or emulated . variables.tf Every input the next platform engineer might tweak — one place to read the contract. locals.tf name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${terraform.workspace}\" so every resource names itself correctly per environment. main.tf All 18 resources — VPC plumbing + 4 SGs + EC2 + 2 ALBs + 2 TGs + 2 listeners + 4 TG attachments. outputs.tf DNS names and ARNs that downstream stacks DNS, autoscaling consume. 🔑 KEY IDEA The split is a review strategy , not a style preference: a PR touching providers.tf gets different scrutiny than one touching main.tf . Mixed files hide intent; separated files declare it. 🎓 EXAM Memorize the six core files: versions pin · providers switch · variables contract · locals naming · mai"},{"anchor":"networking-the-4-security-groups","text":"n resources · outputs handoff . Plus two shell scripts for the pipeline, one tfvars example, one README. ⏭️ The two files that make Floci possible: → versions.tf + providers.tf ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 5 — ▤ TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 03 · THE PIN &amp; THE SWITCH 🏛️ versions.tf + providers.tf ⏱ 7 MIN §3 versions.tf — the pin that makes it portable terraform { required_version = \"&gt;= 1.6.0, &lt; 2.0.0\" required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\" version = \"~&gt; 5.0\" } } } ~&gt; 5.0 means 5.x but not 6.0 — roll forward to bug-fixes without re-approval. Commit the auto-generated .terraform.lock.hcl so a teammate's init downloads the same provider bytes . §4 providers.tf — Floci or real AWS with one variable provider \"aws\" { region = var.region access_key = \"test\" secret_key = \"test\" skip_credentials_validation = true skip_metadata_api_check = true skip_requesting_account_id = true s3_use_path_style = true insecure = true dynamic \"endpoints\" { for_each = var.floci_endpoint = null ? 1 : content { ec2 = var.floci_endpoint elb = var.floci_endpoint elbv2 = var.floci_endpoint iam = var.floci_endpoint sts = var.floci_endpoint s3 = var.floci_endpoint } } } 🔑 THE TRICK The dynami"},{"anchor":"ec2-fleet-the-public-alb-quartet","text":"c \"endpoints\" block: when var.floci_endpoint is null the block is empty → real AWS. When it points at Floci → every API the provider might call ec2 , elb , elbv2 , iam , sts , s3 is redirected. One variable, two worlds. ⚠ THE BUG I HIT AND FIXED The elbv2 line is mandatory for ALBs — the classic elb endpoint only serves CLBs. A missing elbv2 line makes aws_lb silently call real AWS even with TF_VAR_floci_endpoint set. 💡 WHY THE FLAGS insecure = true plus the four skip_ flags are LocalStack/Floci-friendly defaults — they strip TLS, IMDS, account-ID and STS validation that's pointless when the API is a stub. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 6 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTIONS 05–06 · CONTRACT &amp; NAMES 🎛️ variables.tf + locals.tf ⏱ 6 MIN §5 variables.tf — every knob the platform team can tune per workspace Var Default Notes region us-east-1 One region per deploy. floci_endpoint null Set http://localhost:4566 to use Floci. project onext Resource-name prefix. vpc_cidr 10.30.0.0/16 public_subnets \"10.30.1.0/24\",\"10.30.2.0/24\" private_subnets \"10.30.10.0/24\",\"10.30.11.0/24\" frontend_instance_type t3.micro backend_instance_type t3.micro frontend_count 2 Used by count and TG attachments. backend_cou"},{"anchor":"internal-alb-health-check-anatomy","text":"nt 2 health_check_path / Target ping path on the backend. health_check_matcher 200 Comma-separated acceptable HTTP codes. 💡 WHY A STRING, NOT A BOOL The Floci switch is a string because the provider's endpoints { ... } block accepts an HTTP URL — a bool would force an extra indirection. §6 locals.tf — workspace-aware naming locals { name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${terraform.workspace}\" common_tags = { Project = var.project Environment = terraform.workspace ManagedBy = \"terraform\" } } 🔑 ONE CODEBASE, THREE NAME SPACES Every Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-vpc\" becomes onext-dev-vpc in dev, onext-staging-vpc in staging, onext-prod-vpc in prod. Same for ALB names, TG names, listener names. No renaming, no collisions. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 7 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF I 🌐 Networking + the 4 Security Groups ⏱ 7 MIN §7.1 Networking foundation — always first data \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\" gives real AZ names — never hard-code them. Then: aws_vpc · aws_internet_gateway · aws_subnet.public · aws_subnet.private · aws_route_table.public IGW default route · aws_route_table.private . Two aws_route_table_association blocks use count = length aws_subnet.public / length "},{"anchor":"outputs-tf-what-the-platform-consumes","text":"aws_subnet.private — the count-over-a-list lesson: going from 2 AZs to 1 shrinks the code without touching the associations . §7.2 Security groups — each its own resource so changes review cleanly SG Ingress Why public_alb TCP 80/443 from 0.0.0.0/0 Internet-facing load balancer. internal_alb TCP 80 from frontend_ec2 SG and VPC CIDR Defense-in-depth. frontend_ec2 TCP 80 from public_alb SG only ALB → EC2 path. backend_ec2 TCP 80 from internal_alb SG only Internal traffic only. 🔑 THE DEPENDENCY CHAIN The two SG pairs that reference each other frontend_ec2 ↔ public_alb , backend_ec2 ↔ internal_alb form a dependency chain Terraform resolves automatically — the SGs are created in topological order, no depends_on needed . ⚠ READ THE CHAIN ALOUD world → public ALB → frontend EC2 → internal ALB → backend EC2. Traffic can only flow down the chain , and every hop narrows the source. There is no path from the internet to the backends — that's the design. 🎓 EXAM Memorize the 4 SGs and their ingress: public_alb 80/443 from world · internal_alb 80 from frontend SG + VPC CIDR · frontend_ec2 80 from public_alb SG only · backend_ec2 80 from internal_alb SG only . ⏭️ Network and walls in place — th"},{"anchor":"workspaces-dev-staging-prod","text":"e machines and the first quartet: → EC2 + Public ALB ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 8 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF II 🖥️→⚖️ EC2 Fleet + the Public ALB Quartet ⏱ 8 MIN §7.3 EC2 fleet 2 + 2 data \"aws_ami\" \"amazon_linux\" looks up the latest amzn2 AMI so the code never pins an outdated AMI id. aws_instance.frontend / .backend use count with a user_data script installing Apache and serving a workspace-aware page — so the health-check / path returns 200 the moment the instance boots. Spread across AZs: subnet_id = aws_subnet.public count.index % length ... .id . ⚠ FLOCI NOTE Floci's EC2 returns instances in terminated state, which crashes Terraform's state serializer. The IaC is correct and works on real AWS — run-alb.sh excludes the EC2 + TG-attach targets from the Floci apply so the rest verifies end-to-end. §7.4 Public ALB — the canonical quartet resource \"aws_lb\" \"public\" { name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-alb\" load_balancer_type = \"application\" internal = false ← internet-facing security_groups = aws_security_group.public_alb.id subnets = aws_subnet.public .id tags = merge local.common_tags, { Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-alb\" } } resource \"aws_lb_target_group\" \"public\" { "},{"anchor":"verify-idempotence-docs-cross-ref","text":"name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-tg\" port = 80 protocol = \"HTTP\" target_type = \"instance\" vpc_id = aws_vpc.this.id health_check { enabled = true path = var.health_check_path port = \"traffic-port\" matcher = var.health_check_matcher interval = 30 timeout = 5 healthy_threshold = 2 unhealthy_threshold = 3 } tags = merge local.common_tags, { Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-tg\" } } resource \"aws_lb_listener\" \"public_http\" { load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.public.arn port = 80 protocol = \"HTTP\" default_action { type = \"forward\" target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.public.arn } } resource \"aws_lb_target_group_attachment\" \"public\" { count = length aws_instance.frontend target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.public.arn target_id = aws_instance.frontend count.index .id port = 80 } 🔑 THE CANONICAL QUARTET Load balancer + target group + listener + attachment. Every ALB stack in AWS needs at least these four blocks per tier — and these four resources are exactly what the internal tier copies. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 9 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF III 🔒 Internal ALB + Health-Check Anatomy ⏱ 6 MIN §7.5 Internal ALB — same quartet, internal = true The internal block is the public block "},{"anchor":"floci-pipeline-cleanup-truth-table","text":"copied with internal = true , swapped security group internal_alb , swapped subnets private , swapped target group, swapped instance set backend . The name changes int vs pub keep ALB names unique in AWS . public: internal = false · pub SG · public subnets · frontend TG ⇄ internal: internal = true · int SG · private subnets · backend TG §7.6 Health-check anatomy — every line, one meaning health_check { enabled = true path = \"/\" GET / on each target port = \"traffic-port\" follow the TG port 80 matcher = \"200\" expect HTTP 200 interval = 30 probe every 30 s timeout = 5 give up after 5 s healthy_threshold = 2 2 passes → healthy unhealthy_threshold = 3 3 fails → unhealthy } 💡 TUNING RULES The numbers are tuned for a small backend. Rule of thumb: interval ≥ 2 × timeout a probe must finish before the next fires and healthy_threshold + unhealthy_threshold = 5 for fast convergence on small fleets. ⚠ WHY THE HEALTH CHECK IS THE STAR The ALB only routes to healthy targets. If your app serves / with anything but the matcher code, the TG marks everything unhealthy and the ALB returns 503s — while every instance is technically running . The health check is the contract between the LB and the app"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":". 🎓 EXAM Internal-vs-public differences: internal flag · SG · subnets · TG · instance set — five swaps, one copied quartet. Health check contract: GET path, expect matcher, interval/timeout/thresholds as tuned. ⏭️ The stack is coded — what does the rest of the platform consume? → outputs.tf ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 10 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 08 · THE HANDOFF 📤 outputs.tf — What the Platform Consumes ⏱ 4 MIN Outputs are this stack's contract — ten values that DNS, autoscaling, and observability stacks will reference: Output Purpose vpc_id For peering / TGW / observability stacks. public_subnet_ids For ASGs, Lambda ENIs, etc. private_subnet_ids Same. public_alb_dns_name Route53 alias records, app config. public_alb_zone_id Required for a Route53 alias to the ALB. internal_alb_dns_name Internal service discovery. public_target_group_arn For autoscaling attachments. internal_target_group_arn Same. frontend_instance_ids For SSM / CloudWatch inventory. backend_instance_ids Same. 🔑 WHY THE ZONE ID A Route53 alias record to an ALB needs both the DNS name and the ALB's hosted zone ID — that's why public_alb_zone_id is exported separately. Forgetting it breaks the alias Terraform-side."},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":" 💡 TG ARNS EXIST FOR AUTOSCALING An ASG can register itself with a target group directly via target_group_arn — exporting both TG ARNs means a future autoscaling stack attaches to the ALB without editing this code . 🎓 EXAM Count them: 1 VPC + 2 subnet sets + 2 DNS + 1 zone ID + 2 TG ARNs + 2 instance sets = 10 outputs . Every one exists because some other stack will need it — outputs are written for the consumer, not the author. ⏭️ Code and contract done — three workspaces, three applies: → Workspaces ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 11 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 09 · THREE ENVIRONMENTS 🗂️ Workspaces — dev / staging / prod ⏱ 6 MIN TASK The command sequence one codebase, three applies cd live-verify-floci/alb-cluster export TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 $ terraform init $ terraform workspace new dev creates + selects $ terraform workspace new staging $ terraform workspace new prod $ ./run-alb.sh dev apply dev 22 non-EC2 resources $ ./run-alb.sh staging $ ./run-alb.sh prod $ terraform workspace list default dev staging prod 🔑 ISOLATION PROOF Each workspace gets its own state file under terraform.tfstate.d/&lt;name&gt;/terraform.tfstate — and its own resources in Floci: 3 se"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"parate VPCs, 12 subnets, 12 SGs, 6 ALBs, 6 target groups, 6 listeners , all live. Nothing is shared between environments. 💡 WHERE THE 66 COMES FROM The cover said \"66 resources deployed\": ~22 non-EC2 resources × 3 workspaces ≈ 66 live objects in Floci. The EC2 + attachment blocks are coded and state-tracked but excluded from the Floci apply the terminated-state limitation from page 9 . ⚠ THE DEFAULT WORKSPACE First init + apply without creating a workspace lands everything in default with an unprefixed state key. Create dev before the first apply — the sequence above is ordered deliberately. 🎓 EXAM The proof of multi-env: 3 workspaces → 3 state files → 3 complete, separately-named stacks onext-dev- , onext-staging- , onext-prod- , all from one codebase with zero environment conditionals in the resource blocks — the naming comes from locals.tf . ⏭️ Applied — now prove what's live matches what's coded: → Verify + Idempotence ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 12 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTIONS 10–12 · THE PROOF ✅ Verify · Idempotence · Docs Cross-Ref ⏱ 6 MIN §10 Verifying the apply $ ./verify-alb.sh onext-dev-vpc The script hits the Floci endpoint directly and dumps every VPC, subnet, security g"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"roup, load balancer, target group, and listener it sees — confirming Apply complete matches what's actually in the emulator . §11 Idempotence check — the correctness gate $ terraform plan -target =aws_lb.public -target =aws_lb.internal … No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration. 🎓 THE PROOF Re-running terraform plan on a clean workspace returning \"No changes\" is the proof the IaC is correct — not just one-shot . Apply-once luck isn't engineering; the no-change plan is. §12 Cross-reference — every argument from the 5.x docs Resource Doc anchor registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs/… aws_lb resources/lb aws_lb_listener resources/lb_listener aws_lb_target_group resources/lb_target_group aws_lb_target_group_attachment resources/lb_target_group_attachment aws_security_group resources/security_group aws_vpc · aws_subnet · aws_route_table · aws_internet_gateway · aws_route_table_association resources/&lt;name&gt; each aws_instance resources/instance data \"aws_ami\" data-sources/ami ⚠ THE HARD REQUIREMENT Every argument used here — internal , load_balancer_type , target_type , health_check { … } , default_action { type = \"forward\" } , ingress { security"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"_groups = ... } — comes from the 5.x docs . No deprecated syntax, no guessed arguments. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 13 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTIONS 13–15 · THE PIPELINE ⚙️ FLOCI Pipeline · Cleanup · Truth Table ⏱ 6 MIN §13 FLOCI pipeline integration 1 CI sets TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 2 terraform fmt -check -recursive — formatting gate 3 terraform init -input=false — pulls the locked provider 4 terraform validate — fails the build on syntax errors 5 Per workspace dev/staging/prod : workspace select $ws → ./run-alb.sh $ws -auto-approve , EC2 excluded → ./verify-alb.sh proves it's live → terraform plan must report No changes idempotence gate 🔑 SAME CODE, REAL AWS The EC2/TG-attach blocks stay in main.tf so the same code ships to staging/prod on real AWS unchanged. Floci is for pipeline verification , not production. §14 Cleanup $ ./run-alb.sh destroy dev tears down dev non-EC2 $ ./run-alb.sh destroy staging $ ./run-alb.sh destroy prod $ terraform workspace delete dev staging prod Real AWS teardown: drop the -target filters and let terraform destroy -auto-approve reap everything — EC2s and TG attachments included. §15 The FLOCI truth table Resource Floci behavior Real A"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"WS aws_vpc + subnets + routes + IGW full full aws_security_group full full aws_lb ALB full elbv2 endpoint full aws_lb_target_group full full aws_lb_listener full full aws_lb_target_group_attachment full target is a stub full aws_instance returns terminated → crashes state write full data \"aws_ami\" most-recent amzn2 stub full ⚠ THE HONEST SUMMARY Floci is complete for the ALB control plane and partial for EC2 . The IaC here is complete for both — Floci verifies the control plane, real AWS ships the full stack. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 14 — TOC ◀ Prev · Next SECTION 16 · FINALE 🏁 Recap · Cheat Sheet · Self-Check 🏁 THE END §16 Recap — the five-line summary 18 resource blocks in main.tf: VPC, IGW, 4 subnets, 2 RTs + 2 associations, 4 SGs, 2+2 EC2, 2 ALBs, 2 TGs, 2 listeners, 2 count-driven attachments. Every resource uses modern AWS ~&gt; 5.0 syntax — internal , load_balancer_type , target_type , health_check {…} , default_action { type = \"forward\" } , ingress { security_groups } . Workspaces isolate three environments from one codebase — all three deployed and verified on Floci. Idempotent — re-running plan reports no changes. FLOCI-ready — run/verify scripts are the pipeline gates; "},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"EC2 excluded only because of Floci's stub, not an IaC defect. 📌 Command cheat sheet export TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 $ terraform init && terraform workspace new dev && ./run-alb.sh dev $ ./verify-alb.sh onext-dev-vpc $ terraform plan → No changes = idempotent ✅ $ ./run-alb.sh destroy dev 🎯 Self-check — answer before peeking 1. Which provider endpoint is mandatory for ALBs on Floci? elbv2 — elb only serves CLBs. 2. Which SG can use 0.0.0.0/0? Only public_alb 80/443 . Everything else chains by reference. 3. The \"canonical quartet\" per ALB tier? aws_lb · target group · listener · attachment. 4. What proves idempotence? Re-plan → \"No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.\" 5. Why is EC2 excluded from the Floci apply? Floci returns terminated instances → state write crashes. IaC itself is correct. 6. Health-check rule of thumb? interval ≥ 2×timeout; healthy+unhealthy thresholds sum to 5. 🔑 CLOSING LINE The task done end-to-end: IaC authored, validated, applied to an AWS-compatible emulator across three workspaces, verified live, and reproducible from a fresh clone with three shell commands. ▶ ALB Stack · By Nabawy · INK &amp; AMBER Series — 15 — ▤ T"},{"anchor":"recap-cheat-sheet-self-check","text":"OC ▶ The End 🎬"}]},"aws-39-alb-stack-walkthrough-ink-amber-by-nabawy":{"chunks":[{"page":1,"text":"O N E X T · L E V E L 2 · H A N D S - O N · P R O J E C T W A L K T H R O U G H The Application Load Balancer Stack Internet -facing and int ernal ALBs, t arget gr oups, list ener s, four s ecur ity gr oups, four EC2 inst anc es, thr ee worksp aces. T err afor m ~> 5.0 , Floci-v erified, r eady for the FL OCI pipeline. 18 resource blocks 3 workspaces 66 resources deployed ~>5.0 AWS provider pin A TECHNICAL BOOK · INK & AMBER SERIES · BY NABAWY PROJECT: LIVE-VERIFY-FLOCI/ALB-CLUSTER PUBLIC ALB INTERNAL ALB 4 SGS FLOCI-READY Public ALB · Internal ALB 2 Tiers · 18 Resources I D E M P O T E N T · V E R S I O N - P I N N E D · M U L T I - E N V — B Y N A B A W Y"},{"page":2,"text":"CONTENTS  Map of the Walkthrough  15 SHEETS One task, three phases: plan → build → prove . Click any row to jump; every page has a TOC badge back. I PLAN 03 Executive Summar y — the task in one p aragraph 5 min 04 The Resource Map — tw o tiers, one pictur e 5 min 05 Project Lay out — 10 files, why each exists 4 min II BUILD — THE FILES 06 versions.tf + pr oviders.tf — the pin & the Floci swit ch 7 min 07 variables.tf + locals.tf — ev ery knob, w orkspace naming 6 min 08 main.tf I — Netw orking + the 4 Security Gr oups 7 min 09 main.tf II — EC2 fleet + the Public ALB quar tet 8 min 10 main.tf III — Int ernal ALB + health-check anat omy 6 min 11 outputs.tf — the 10-v alue contract 4 min III PROVE 12 Workspaces — dev/staging/pr od from one codeb ase 6 min 13 Verify + Idempot ence + Docs cr oss-reference 6 min 14 FLOCI pipeline + Cleanup + the T ruth Table 6 min IV FINALE 15 Recap + Cheat Sheet + Self -Check  end DEBUG Reading order: pages 4–5 are the plan (what exists, where it lives), 6–11 are the build (every file, in dependency order), 12–14 are the proof (apply, verify, pipeline). Estimated time: 30 min with Floci, 60 min on real AWS. ALB Stack · By Nabawy — 2 — ▤ TOC prev | ne"},{"page":3,"text":"EXECUTIVE SUMMARY The Task — In One Paragraph  5 MIN Provision a public Application Load Balancer in front of two frontend EC2 instances (public subnets) and an internal Application Load Balancer in front of two backend EC2 instances (private subnets). Four custom security groups, two target groups with HTTP health checks, two listeners, four target-group attachments, plus the surrounding VPC plumbing. Use Terraform 1.x with the hashicorp/aws ~> 5.0 provider — exactly the modern arguments from the official docs — and drive dev/staging/prod with workspaces so one codebase ships three environments.  TIER 1 — PUBLIC internet → public ALB → frontend EC2 ×2. SG allows 80/443 from the world. The front door.  TIER 2 — INTERNAL frontend + VPC CIDR → internal ALB → backend EC2 ×2. SG chains by reference. Defense-in-depth. ▣ The route through this walkthrough  Resource Map The full traffic picture — both tiers, SGs, T Gs, attachments — one diagram.  Project Layout 10 files, and wh y each is its own file.  Pin & Switch versions.tf locks providers; providers.tf redirects every API to Floci.  Inputs & Naming 12 tunable knobs; workspace-aware name prefix + common tags.  SG Chain 4 securi"},{"page":4,"text":"SECTION 01 · THE PICTURE  The Resource Map  5 MIN The whole stack in one diagram. Read it top-down for Tier 1 (public), then the second block for Tier 2 (internal). Every arrow is an SG rule or a TG attachment. internet │ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ sg public_alb (80/443) │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ aws_lb.public (public) │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ tg public │ health_check { /, 200 } └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ attachment → frontend │ │ [0] [1] │ └─────────────────────────┘ sg frontend_ec2 ──80── ▶ ┌─────────────────────────┐ VPC CIDR ──────80── ▶ │ aws_lb.internal (int) │ │ sg internal_alb │ └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ tg internal │ health_check { /, 200 } └────────────┬────────────┘ ▼ ┌─────────────────────────┐ │ attachment → backend │ │ [0] [1] │ └─────────────────────────┘  THE SHAPE Both tiers are the same quartet — ALB → target group → listener → attachments — differing only in exposure ( internal = false/true ), security group, subnets, and which instance set they point at. Learn one tier, you know both.  COUNT THE ARROWS The SG chain:"},{"page":5,"text":"SECTION 02 · THE FILES  Project Layout  4 MIN Ten files under live-verify-floci/alb-cluster/ . Each is its own file for a reason: live-verify-floci/alb-cluster/ ├── versions.tf # Terraform + AWS provider pin ├── providers.tf # AWS provider with Floci endpoint switching ├── variables.tf # all declared inputs ├── locals.tf # name_prefix + common_tags ├── main.tf # 18 resources (VPC, subnets, SGs, EC2, ALBs...) ├── outputs.tf # DNS names, TG ARNs, instance IDs ├── terraform.tfvars.example # sample inputs ├── run-alb.sh # one-command deploy across workspaces ├── verify-alb.sh # post-apply smoke test (queries Floci) └── README.md # usage notes TASK [Why each file is its own file] the reviewer's view File Why it's its own file versions.tf Locks Terraform & provider versions — protects the team from drift . providers.tf Floci endpoint switching lives here so the rest of the code never knows whether AWS is real or emulated . variables.tf Every input the next platform engineer might tweak — one place to read the contract. locals.tf name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${terraform.workspace}\" so every resource names itself correctly per environment. main.tf All 18 resources — VPC plumbing + 4 SGs"},{"page":6,"text":"SECTION 03 · THE PIN & THE SWITCH  versions.tf + providers.tf ⏱ 7 MIN §3 versions.tf — the pin that makes it portable terraform { required_version = \">= 1.6.0, < 2.0.0\" required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\" version = \"~> 5.0\" } } } §4 providers.tf — Floci or real AWS with one variable provider \"aws\" { region = var.region access_key = \"test\" secret_key = \"test\" skip_credentials_validation = true skip_metadata_api_check = true skip_requesting_account_id = true s3_use_path_style = true insecure = true dynamic \"endpoints\" { for_each = var.floci_endpoint != null ? [1] : [] content { ec2 = var.floci_endpoint elb = var.floci_endpoint elbv2 = var.floci_endpoint iam = var.floci_endpoint sts = var.floci_endpoint s3 = var.floci_endpoint } } }  THE TRICK The dynamic \"endpoints\" block: when var.floci_endpoint is null the block is empty → real AWS. When it points at Floci → every API the provider might call ( ec2 , elb , elbv2 , iam , sts , s3 ) is redirected. One variable, two worlds. ⚠ THE BUG I HIT AND FIXED The elbv2 line is mandatory for ALBs — the classic elb endpoint only serves CLBs. A missing elbv2 line makes aws_lb silently call real AWS even with TF_VAR_floci_endpoin"},{"page":7,"text":"SECTIONS 05–06 · CONTRACT & NAMES  variables.tf + locals.tf ⏱ 6 MIN §5 variables.tf — every knob the platform team can tune per workspace Var Default Notes region us-east-1 One region per deploy. floci_endpoint null Set http://localhost:4566 to use Floci. project onext Resource-name prefix. vpc_cidr 10.30.0.0/16 public_subnets [\"10.30.1.0/24\",\"10.30.2.0/24\"] private_subnets [\"10.30.10.0/24\",\"10.30.11.0/24\"] frontend_instance_type t3.micro backend_instance_type t3.micro frontend_count 2 Used by count and TG attachments. backend_count 2 health_check_path / Target ping path on the backend. health_check_matcher 200 Comma-separated acceptable HTTP codes.  WHY A STRING, NOT A BOOL The Floci switch is a string because the provider's endpoints { ... } block accepts an HTTP URL — a bool would force an extra indirection. §6 locals.tf — workspace-aware naming locals { name_prefix = \"${var.project}-${terraform.workspace}\" common_tags = { Project = var.project Environment = terraform.workspace ManagedBy = \"terraform\" } }  ONE CODEBASE, THREE NAME SPACES Every Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-vpc\" becomes onext-dev-vpc in dev, onext-staging-vpc in staging, onext-prod-vpc in prod. Same for ALB nam"},{"page":8,"text":"SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF I  Networking + the 4 Security Groups ⏱ 7 MIN §7.1 Networking foundation — always first §7.2 Security groups — each its own resource so changes review cleanly SG Ingress Why public_alb TCP 80/443 from 0.0.0.0/0 Internet-facing load balancer. internal_alb TCP 80 from frontend_ec2 SG and VPC CIDR Defense-in-depth. frontend_ec2 TCP 80 from public_alb SG only ALB → EC2 path. backend_ec2 TCP 80 from internal_alb SG only Internal traffic only.  THE DEPENDENCY CHAIN The two SG pairs that reference each other ( frontend_ec2 ↔ public_alb , backend_ec2 ↔ internal_alb ) form a dependency chain Terraform resolves automatically — the SGs are created in topological order, no depends_on needed . ⚠ READ THE CHAIN ALOUD world → public ALB → frontend EC2 → internal ALB → backend EC2. Traffic can only flow down the chain , and every hop narrows the source. There is no path from the internet to the backends — that's the design.  EXAM Memorize the 4 SGs and their ingress: public_alb (80/443 from world) · internal_alb (80 from frontend SG + VPC CIDR) · frontend_ec2 (80 from public_alb SG only) · backend_ec2 (80 from internal_alb SG only). ⏭ Network and walls in place — the machin"},{"page":9,"text":"SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF II  → ⚖ EC2 Fleet + the Public ALB Quartet ⏱ 8 MIN §7.3 EC2 fleet (2 + 2) data \"aws_ami\" \"amazon_linux\" looks up the latest amzn2 AMI so the code never pins an outdated AMI id. aws_instance.frontend / .backend use count with a user_data script installing Apache and serving a workspace-aware page — so the health-check / path returns 200 the moment the instance boots. Spread across AZs: subnet_id = aws_subnet.public[count.index % length(...)].id . ⚠ FLOCI NOTE Floci's EC2 returns instances in terminated state, which crashes Terraform's state serializer. The IaC is correct and works on real AWS — run-alb.sh excludes the EC2 + TG-attach targets from the Floci apply so the rest verifies end-to-end. §7.4 Public ALB — the canonical quartet resource \"aws_lb\" \"public\" { name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-alb\" load_balancer_type = \"application\" internal = false # ← internet-facing security_groups = [aws_security_group.public_alb.id] subnets = aws_subnet.public[*].id tags = merge(local.common_tags, { Name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-alb\" }) } resource \"aws_lb_target_group\" \"public\" { name = \"${local.name_prefix}-pub-tg\" port = 80 protocol = \"HTTP\" target_type = \"instance\" "},{"page":10,"text":"SECTION 07 · MAIN.TF III  Internal ALB + Health-Check Anatomy ⏱ 6 MIN §7.5 Internal ALB — same quartet, internal = true The internal block is the public block copied with internal = true , swapped security group ( internal_alb ), swapped subnets (private), swapped target group, swapped instance set (backend). The name changes ( int vs pub ) keep ALB names unique in AWS . public: internal = false · pub SG · public subnets · frontend T G ⇄ internal: internal = tr ue · int SG · private subnets · backend T G §7.6 Health-check anatomy — every line, one meaning health_check { enabled = true path = \"/\" # GET / on each target port = \"traffic-port\" # follow the TG port (80) matcher = \"200\" # expect HTTP 200 interval = 30 # probe every 30 s timeout = 5 # give up after 5 s healthy_threshold = 2 # 2 passes → healthy unhealthy_threshold = 3 # 3 fails → unhealthy }  TUNING RULES The numbers are tuned for a small backend. Rule of thumb: interval ≥ 2 × timeout (a probe must finish before the next fires) and healthy_threshold + unhealthy_threshold = 5 for fast convergence on small fleets. ⚠ WHY THE HEALTH CHECK IS THE STAR The ALB only routes to healthy targets. If your app serves / with anything"},{"page":11,"text":"SECTION 08 · THE HANDOFF  outputs.tf — What the Platform Consumes ⏱ 4 MIN Outputs are this stack's contract — ten values that DNS, autoscaling, and observability stacks will reference: Output Purpose vpc_id For peering / TGW / observability stacks. public_subnet_ids For ASGs, Lambda ENIs, etc. private_subnet_ids Same. public_alb_dns_name Route53 alias records, app config. public_alb_zone_id Required for a Route53 alias to the ALB. internal_alb_dns_name Internal service discovery. public_target_group_arn For autoscaling attachments. internal_target_group_arn Same. frontend_instance_ids For SSM / CloudWatch inventory. backend_instance_ids Same.  WHY THE ZONE ID A Route53 alias record to an ALB needs both the DNS name and the ALB's hosted zone ID — that's why public_alb_zone_id is exported separately. Forgetting it breaks the alias Terraform-side.  TG ARNS EXIST FOR AUTOSCALING An ASG can register itself with a target group directly via target_group_arn — exporting both TG ARNs means a future autoscaling stack attaches to the ALB without editing this code .  EXAM Count them: 1 VPC + 2 subnet sets + 2 DNS + 1 zone ID + 2 T G ARNs + 2 instance sets = 10 outputs . Every one exists be"},{"page":12,"text":"SECTION 09 · THREE ENVIRONMENTS  Workspaces — dev / staging / prod ⏱ 6 MIN TASK [The command sequence] one codebase, three applies cd live-verify-floci/alb-cluster export TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 $ terraform init $ terraform workspace new dev # creates + selects $ terraform workspace new staging $ terraform workspace new prod $ ./run-alb.sh dev # apply dev (22 non-EC2 resources) $ ./run-alb.sh staging $ ./run-alb.sh prod $ terraform workspace list default * dev staging prod  ISOLATION PROOF Each workspace gets its own state file under terraform.tfstate.d/<name>/terraform.tfstate — and its own resources in Floci: 3 separate VPCs, 12 subnets, 12 SGs, 6 ALBs, 6 target groups, 6 listeners , all live. Nothing is shared between environments.  WHERE THE 66 COMES FROM The cover said \"66 resources deployed\": ~22 non-EC2 resources × 3 workspaces ≈ 66 live objects in Floci. The EC2 + attachment blocks are coded and state-tracked but excluded from the Floci apply (the terminated-state limitation from page 9). ⚠ THE DEFAULT WORKSPACE First init + apply without creating a workspace lands everything in default with an unprefixed state key. Create dev before the first apply —"},{"page":13,"text":"SECTIONS 10–12 · THE PROOF ✅ Verify · Idempotence · Docs Cross-Ref ⏱ 6 MIN §10 Verifying the apply $ ./verify-alb.sh onext-dev-vpc The script hits the Floci endpoint directly and dumps every VPC, subnet, security group, load balancer, target group, and listener it sees — confirming Apply complete! matches what's actually in the emulator . §11 Idempotence check — the correctness gate $ terraform plan -target =aws_lb.public -target =aws_lb.internal ... No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.  THE PROOF Re-running terraform plan on a clean workspace returning \"No changes\" is the proof the IaC is correct — not just one-shot . Apply- once luck isn't engineering; the no-change plan is. §12 Cross-reference — every argument from the 5.x docs Resource Doc anchor (registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/aws/latest/docs/...) aws_lb resources/lb aws_lb_listener resources/lb_listener aws_lb_target_group resources/lb_target_group aws_lb_target_group_attachment resources/lb_target_group_attachment aws_security_group resources/security_group aws_vpc · aws_subnet · aws_route_table · aws_internet_gateway · aws_route_table_association resources/<name> each aws_instance resources"},{"page":14,"text":"SECTIONS 13–15 · THE PIPELINE ⚙ FLOCI Pipeline · Cleanup · Truth Table ⏱ 6 MIN §13 FLOCI pipeline integration 1 CI sets TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 2 terraform fmt -check -recursive — formatting gate 3 terraform init -input=false — pulls the locked provider 4 terraform validate — fails the build on syntax errors 5 Per workspace (dev/staging/prod): workspace select $ws → ./run-alb.sh $ws ( -auto-approve , EC2 excluded) → ./verify-alb.sh proves it's live → terraform plan must report No changes (idempotence gate)  SAME CODE, REAL AWS The EC2/TG-attach blocks stay in main.tf so the same code ships to staging/prod on real AWS unchanged. Floci is for pipeline verification , not production. §14 Cleanup $ ./run-alb.sh destroy dev # tears down dev (non-EC2) $ ./run-alb.sh destroy staging $ ./run-alb.sh destroy prod $ terraform workspace delete dev staging prod Real AWS teardown: drop the -target filters and let terraform destroy -auto-approve reap everything — EC2s and TG attachments included. §15 The FLOCI truth table Resource Floci behavior Real AWS aws_vpc + subnets + routes + IGW full full aws_security_group full full aws_lb (ALB) full (elbv2 endpoint) full aws_lb_targe"},{"page":15,"text":"SECTION 16 · FINALE  Recap · Cheat Sheet · Self-Check  THE END §16 Recap — the five-line summary  Command cheat sheet export TF_VAR_floci_endpoint=http://localhost:4566 $ terraform init && terraform workspace new dev && ./run-alb.sh dev $ ./verify-alb.sh onext-dev-vpc $ terraform plan # → No changes = idempotent ✅ $ ./run-alb.sh destroy dev  Self-check — answer before peeking 1. Which provider endpoint is mandatory for ALBs on Floci? elbv2 — elb only serves CLBs. 2 . Which SG can use 0.0.0.0/0? Only public_alb (80/443). Everything else chains by reference. 3 . The \"canonical quartet\" per ALB tier? aws_lb · target group · listener · attachment. 4 . What proves idempotence? Re-plan → \"No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.\" 5 . Why is EC2 excluded from the Floci apply? Floci returns terminated instances → state write crashes. IaC itself is correct. 6 . Health-check rule of thumb? interval ≥ 2×timeout; healthy+unhealthy thresholds sum to 5.  CLOSING LINE The task done end-to-end: IaC authored, validated, applied to an AWS-compatible emulator across three workspaces, verified live, and reproducible from a fresh clone with three shell commands. ▶ 18 resource blo"}]}});